1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Ida Nielsdotter=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Niels, King of Denmark and Margareta Ingesdotter Fredkulla 
2 CONT * '''Ida''', var en dansk prinsessa, moder till Bogislaw I. och Kasimir I.
2 CONT * (Troligen dotter till Nils av Danmark och Margareta Fredkulla enl. den Pommerska krönikören Thomas Kantzow,).
2 CONT * Wartislaw I Duke of Pomerania, his second wife Ida was allegedly a Danish princess and is believed to be the mother of his sons Bogislaw I and Casimir I , who were probably born after 1124 His brother Ratibor, founder of the sid
2 CONC eline of the Ratiboriden, took over the guardianship of the children after Wartislaw's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wartislaw_I._ Wartislaw I Duke of Pomerania, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wartislaw I (* around 1100 ; † before 1148 in Stolpe ) was a prince in Pomerania . He is considered the progenitor of the Griffin dynasty and founder of the Pomeranian ducal house . He significantly promoted the introduction of Ch
2 CONC ristianity in Pomerania.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing is known about the origin of Wartislaw. Its first mention can be found in the vita of the bishop and missionary Otto von Bamberg . His first mission trip took place on behalf of Polish (senior) Duke Bolesław III. Schiefmun
2 CONC d , who had conquered the estuary of the Oder and with the city of Stettin in 1121/1122. Wartislaw I. took over the administration of Bolesław III. conquered territory of the Liutizen west of the Oder. In 1128 Otto von Bamberg mad
2 CONC e a second mission trip to this area, this time under the protection of King Lothar of Supplinburg and the Margrave (at that time still the Mark Lausitz ) Albrecht the Bear. In the presence of Otto and Wartislaw, the nobles of the
2 CONC  state of Pentecost decided in 1128 at a state parliament in Usedom to adopt Christianity.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1135 Wartislaws fiefdom Boleslaw had to recognize the fiefdom of the Holy / Roman Empire for the areas west of the Oder . As a result, Wartislaw, as an aftervasal, was also liable to the Reich as a fief.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to various sources, Wartislaw died between 1135 and 1148. He is said to have been slain near Stolpe an der Peene , where a pagan cult site was possibly located, by a turn that opposed Christianization. His brother Ratibo
2 CONC r later donated a church at the place where Wartislaw was probably buried, and in 1153 the Stolpe Monastery with Wartislaw's sons .
2 CONT
2 CONT Wartislaw I was married twice. His first wife was named Heila, her connection to the Welfenhaus is not documented. '''His second wife Ida was allegedly a Danish princess and is believed to be the mother of his sons Bogislaw I and 
2 CONC Casimir I , who were probably born after 1124. His brother Ratibor, founder of the sideline of the Ratiboriden , took over the guardianship of the children after Wartislaw's death.'''
