2 CONT From the Foundation For Medieval Genealogy Medlands Project page on Denmark (Covering Adela's first marriage family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/DENMARK.htm#KnudIIdied1086
2 CONT
2 CONT KNUD (-murdered Odense, St Albans Church 10 Jul 1086, bur Odense, St Albans Church, later called St Knuds Church). 
2 CONT *Illegitimate son of King Svend listed in Knytlinga Saga, third in order in the list[362], while Saxo Grammaticus lists him ninth in order and implies that he was the full brother of Erik[363].  
2 CONT *Florence of Worcester records that "Suani regis Danorum filii, Haroldus, Canutus et patruus eorum Esbernus comes et comes Turkillus" sailed from Denmark in [1069] and landed "in ostio Humbræ fluminis"[364].  He challenged the suc
2 CONC cession of his brother Harald in 1074, supported in particular by the Scanians, but went into exile when he was unsuccessful[365].  
2 CONT *Jarl in Sjælland 1074.  
2 CONT *He invaded England in 1075, joining a revolt against William I King of England, raiding Yorkshire.  He succeeded his brother in 1080 as KNUD II “den Hellige/the Holy” King of Denmark.  The Chronicon Roskildense records that "frat
2 CONC er eius Kanutus" succeeded on the death of "Haroldus"[366].  
2 CONT *He led expeditions against the Curones (in coastal Latvia, later Courland) and Estonians[367].  
2 CONT *He was planning another invasion of England with his father-in-law in 1085, but was delayed "almost two years by the adverseness of the wind, changed his design, affirming that it must be the determination of God that he could no
2 CONC t put to sea", but later being "misled by the suggestions of some persons who attributed the failure of their passage to the conjurations of certain old women, he sentenced the chiefs whose wives were accused … to an intolerable f
2 CONC ine" and was murdered in consequence[368].  
2 CONT *Saxo Grammaticus records that he was besieged in the church of St Alban, while praying, by "the mutinous populace" who eventually broke in and killed him on the altar[369].  The Chronicon Roskildense records that "Kanutus" was ki
2 CONC lled "Othinse in ecclesia sancti Albani" in 1090 in the eleventh year of his reign[370].  
2 CONT *Canonised 1100 by Pope Pascal II, feast day 19 Jan.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m ([1080]) as her first husband, ADELA de Flandre, daughter of ROBERT I "le Frison" Count of Flanders & his wife Gertrud of Saxony [Billung] ([1065]-Apr 1115)'''
2 CONT *'''Her marriage and parentage are referred to by Saxo Grammaticus[371].'''
2 CONT *'''She returned to Flanders from Denmark after her husband's murder with her son, leaving her daughters behind[372].'''
2 CONT *'''She married secondly (1092) Roger "Borsa/the Purse" Duke of Apulia.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Knud II & his wife had three children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         CARL ([1084]-murdered Bruges 2 Mar 1127, bur Bruges, St Donatien, later Saint-Sauveur).  
2 CONT *Saxo Grammaticus names him as his father's legitimate son[373].  "Carolus filius Canuti, regis Dacie, ex filia primogenita Robert Frisonis et Gertrudis" is named in the Cartulaire de Saint-Bertin[374].  "Caroli regis Danorum fili
2 CONC i" is named in the donation to Saint-Bertin of "Balduinus Flandrensium marchisus" dated 1119[375].  
2 CONT *His mother took him to Flanders for safety after the murder of his father in 1086.   (He made a pilgrimage to Palestine [1107][376].  
2 CONT *He was received in Flanders in 1111 by Count Robert II.  He succeeded in 1119 as CHARLES "the Good" Count of Flanders. 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         CÆCILIA ([1085/86]-after Jan 1131).  
2 CONT *Twin with her sister Ingegerd, they were left in Denmark when their mother fled to Flanders after their father's murder according to Saxo Grammaticus who also names her husband[377].  
2 CONT *She is named in the Passio as the hostess of Knud "Lavard" during his stay with her husband in early 1131[378].  
2 CONT *m ERIK "prefect of the Goths".  
2 CONT *Jarl at Falster (island off south Zealand).  
2 CONT *Saxo Grammaticus records that Knud "Lavard" was murdered while staying at the house of "Ericus prefect of Falster" in Haraldsted in 1131[379].   
2 CONT *As "Ericus dux" he witnessed the 1 Sep 1145 charter of Archbishop Eskill at Lund[380].  
2 CONT *Erik & his wife had two children: Knud and Karl
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         INGEGERD ([1085/86]-).  
2 CONT *Twin with her sister Cæcilie, they were left in Denmark when their mother fled to Flanders after their father's murder according to Saxo Grammaticus who also names her husband[383].  
2 CONT *m FOLKE "den Digre/the Fat" Jarl in Sweden , son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
