2 CONT
2 CONT Sweyn I, or Sweyn Forkbeard, in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicles, also known as Swegen and Swein, (Old Norse: Sveinn Tjúguskegg, Norwegian: Svein Tjugeskjegg, Swedish: Sven Tveskägg; Danish: Svend Tveskæg, originally Tjugeskæg or Tyvskæ
2 CONC g), (c. 960–February 3, 1014), was king of Denmark and England, as well as parts of Norway. He was a Viking leader and the father of Canute the Great. On his father Harald Bluetooth's death in late 986 or early 987, he became King
2 CONC  of Denmark; in 1000, with allegiance of the Trondejarl, Erik of Lade, he was ruler over most of Norway. After a long effort at conquest, and shortly before his death, in 1013 he became King of England. For the last months of his 
2 CONC life, he was the Danish sovereign of a North Sea empire, which only his son Cnut was to rival in northern Europe
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 986-February 3, 1014 (Denmark)
2 CONT 999- February 3, 1014 (Norway)
2 CONT 1013-February 3, 1014 (England) 
2 CONT Born c. 960 
2 CONT  Denmark 
2 CONT Died February 3, 1014 
2 CONT  England 
2 CONT Buried Roskilde Cathedral 
2 CONT Consort Gunhild[1](Gunhild of Wenden, Sigrid the Haughty ???) 
2 CONT Father Harald Bluetooth 
2 CONT Mother Gunhilde 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the death of his father, Harald Bluetooth, a certain Sigvald and the Vikings from Jom took the young King Swein prisoner.  To free him, Swein’s followers were forced to pay ransom money.   From that moment on, Swein 
2 CONC felt that his name, honour and reputation had been blemished.  A way to retain his name and reputation was to resume raiding the coasts of England.  In the meantime, Denmark was left in the hands of the Swedish King Erik Sejrsæl. 
2 CONC  As a matter of interest, Swein later married Erik’s widow, Gunhild, the daughter of Duke Miscio of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT For various reasons, the King of England, Æthelred the ‘Unready’ (born 979 - died 1016) was unable to defend the English coast from the Viking raiders.  To keep the peace and avoid defeat the English were forced to pay the Vikings
2 CONC  huge amounts of silver, the so-called Dane geld.  Together with the Norwegian Olav Trygveson, Swein collected 16.000 pounds of silver in Dane geld in 994.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few years later, Swein returned to Denmark to free it from the Swedes.  After some hard and vicious fighting near Hedeby, the Swedes were beaten and Swein finally took full control over Denmark again.  Swein’s next step was to r
2 CONC id himself of his former ally, the Norwegian Olav Trygveson.  This he did at the battle of Svold, (whereabouts unknown) were the Norwegian fleet was beaten, and Olav killed.  Swein was now master of Norway and Denmark.  To show hi
2 CONC s strength, Swein minted Denmark’s first official coinage
2 CONT In the mean time, Æthelred found it increasingly difficult to sustain peace with the Vikings by payment of Dane geld alone.  He then made his life’s greatest mistake, by ordering the murder of all Danes living in England.  This ha
2 CONC ppened in 1002.  Back in Denmark, the natural reaction was to demand revenge.  The following years the Vikings raided the coasts of England again and again.  In 1013 these raids took on the form of an invasion.  Together with a hu
2 CONC ge fleet, and together with his young son, Canute, Swein tried to take London by siege, but without success.  Undaunted by this, Swein was paid homage to by the rest of the population.  London was alone, and consequently surrender
2 CONC ed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1014, at a meeting in Gainsborough, Swein fell off his horse and died.  His body was taken to York, prior to being sailed back to Denmark for burial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Swein’s eldest son, Harald, was elected as King of Denmark, whilst his youngest son, the 18-year-old Canute was to become the next Viking king of England.  Later to be known as Canute the Great.  Before this could happen, Canute h
2 CONC ad to fight Edmund Ironside, elected King of England and son of King Æthelred, who died in 1016.  The two, who divided England between them, made a truce.  Everyone expected them to make battle again, but Edmund died suddenly on t
2 CONC he 30th of November 1016.  Now Canute was finally crowned as King of England, and reigned until his death in 1035.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Swein Forkbeard and Queen Gunhild had the children
1 NOTE King of Denmark from 985 to 1014
