2 CONT ble det also et godt forhold and stabile grenser mot Germany. Oppholdt seg
2 CONT       mest in England.
1 NOTE Canute, Cnut or Knut, called The Great (995-1035), King of England, Denmark, and Norway, was the son of Sweyn of Denmark. On the death of his father, during an invasion of England, Canute was proclaimed king of that country, but h
2 CONC is supremacy was contested by Edmund Ironsides, son of Ethelred I, who was at that time a refugee in Normandy. Within a short time, however, Edmund was treacherously assassinated, and Canute was proclaimed king of all England. On 
2 CONC the death of his brother Harold, in 1019, he became king of Denmark also. He conciliated the higher clergy by his liberality, and secured his position still further by the creation of a standing army. Canute overawed and partially
2 CONC  subjugated the Wendish pirates; and when the kings of Norway and Sweden invaded Denmark, checked them at the battle of Helgeaa (1026). In 1027 he made a pilgrimage to Rome, and in 1028 invaded Norway, and added it to his dominion
2 CONC s. A famous story relates how he proved to his flattering courtiers that the waves did not retreat at his command. He was buried at Winchester.
2 CONT [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional information: Britannia.com http://britannia.com/history/monarchs/mon17.html
