2 CONT Another name for Eric was Erik VII Segersäll of Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric the Victorious (VI), or Erik Segersäll, (985?- 995), was king of the Swedes during the last two decades of the 10th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT The extent of his kingdom is disputed. In addition to the Svealand heartland round lake Mälaren it may have extended down the Baltic Sea coast as far south as Blekinge. Details on Eric's reign are sparse. It is claimed that he def
2 CONC eated a major Danish army under the command of his nephew Styrbjörn Starke at the Fyris Wolds at Old Uppsala, from which his accolade "the victorious" stems. In all probability he founded the town of Sigtuna, which still exists an
2 CONC d where the first Swedish coins were stamped for his son and successor Olof Skötkonung.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric is claimed to have married Sigrid the Haughty, daughter of Mieszko I of Poland, in the 980s.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Sagas, Eric the Victorious ruled together with his brother Olof (II) Björnsson. After Olof Björnsson's death, Eric proclaimed his still unborn child co-ruler instead of his nephew Styrbjörn Starke. The disinherite
2 CONC d Styrbjörn was to attack Eric with a large Danish force. The victory rendered Eric the cognomen "Victorious".
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Adam of Bremen, who is considered to be more reliable than the sagas, only gives Emund Eriksson as predecessor to Eric the Victorious. He does, however, not claim that Emund was the father of Erik, and nothing contradicts
2 CONC  the possibility that Emund and Björn were co-rulers, like Erik and Olof and their semi-legendary ancestors Björn at Hauge and Anund Uppsale.
2 CONT
2 CONT Munsöslekten etterfulgte Lodbrokslekten som konger av Sverige fra 785 til 1056. Erik regnes som den første historiske kongen av et samlet Sverige. Slekten ble etterfulgt av Stenkilslekten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det foreligger ingen vitnesikre opplysninger om Eriks byrd. I islandsk litteratur berettes at han skulle være sønn til en kong Bjørn. Dette er imidlertid historiskt umulig, og man har i stedet antatt at han skulle være sønn til de
2 CONC n Emund Eriksson, som Adam av Bremen i sin krønika oppfører som svensk konge umiddelbart før Erik. Unektelig finnes det ikke noe som hindrer at Eriks far het Emund, en sønnesønn til Erik bar dette navn, eller at han som Erik hadde
2 CONC  vært svensk konge. At navneskikken bland Eriks arvtagere er den samme som hos hans foretredere på tronen gjør det imidlertid sannsynlig at hans tronebestigelse ikke innebar et nytt dynasti.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik hadde seiersrike strider med sine fiender. Mest kjent er slaget ved Fyrisvallene, hvor han seiret over anfallende skånske vikinger. I allianse med slavere har han i henhold til en kilde bekjempet Danmark, at han også erobret 
2 CONC landet og der mottok dåpen er omstridt. I de nordiske sagaene er Erik ofte omtalt, og hans historie overdrives kraftig. Det ovennevnte slaget ved Fyrisvallene skal han ifølge sagaene ha utkjempet mot sin brorsønn Styrbjørn og for 
2 CONC seieren fått vie seg selv til Odin. Slaget skal ha funnet sted i 982.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brorsønnen skulle tidligere ha flyktet til Danmark og blitt høvding i Jomsborg. Med hjelp av Harald Blåtann kunne han sette seg i spissen for en mektig hær og dra mot sin farbror. Styrbjørn falt i slaget ved Fyrisvallene. Etter se
2 CONC ieren fikk Erik tilnavnet "Segersäll".
2 CONT
2 CONT I henhold til sagaene skal Erik ha vært gift med Sigrid Storråde, men i virkeligheten var han gift med en slavisk prinsesse.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagre's Saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "28. KING EIRIK EYMUNDSON'S DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Guthorm dwelt principally at Tunsberg, and governed the whole of Viken when the king was not there. He defended the land, which, at that time, was much plundered by the vikings. There were disturbances also up in Gautland as 
2 CONC long as King Eirik Eymundson lived; but he died when King Harald Harfager had been ten years king of all Norway."
2 CONT
2 CONT "29.GUTHORM'S DEATH IN TUNSBERG.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Eirik, his son Bjorn was king of Svithjod for fifty years. He was father of Eirik the Victorious, and of Olaf the father of Styrbjorn. Guthorm died on a bed of sickness at Tunsberg, and King Harald gave his son Guthorm the g
2 CONC overnment of that part of his dominions and made him chief of it."
2 CONT
2 CONT   Noted events in his life were:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Acceded: King of Sweden, Cir 965.
2 CONT
2 CONT • Acceded: King of Sweden, 980.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric married Sigrid Toftesdatter Storråde, daughter of Skoglar-Toste and Unknown. (Sigrid Toftesdatter Storråde was born in 967 and died in 1014.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric next married Gunhild Mieczyslavsdatter of Poland, daughter of Mieszko I of Poland and Dobrawka of Bohemia, circa 980-985. (Gunhild Mieczyslavsdatter of Poland was born in 967 and died in 1014
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_the_Victorious
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric the Victorious (VI), Old Norse: Eiríkr inn sigrsæli, Modern Swedish: Erik Segersäll, (945?- c 995), was the first Swedish king (970-995) about whom anything definite is known.
2 CONT
2 CONT His original territory lay in Uppland and neighbouring provinces. He was victorious over an invasion from the south in the Battle of the Fýrisvellir close to Uppsala. Reports that Eric's brother Olof was the father of Styrbjörn th
2 CONC e Strong belong to the realm of myth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The extent of his kingdom is unknown. In addition to the Swedish heartland round lake Mälaren it may have extended down the Baltic Sea coast as far south as Blekinge.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Flateyjarbok, his success was due to the fact that he allied with the peasants against the nobility, and it is obvious from archeological findings that the influence of the latter diminished during the last part o
2 CONC f the tenth century.[4] He was also, probably, the introducer of the famous medieval Scandinavian system of universal conscription known as the ledung in the provinces around Mälaren.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Adam of Bremen, Eric allied himself with the Polish prince Boleslav to conquer Denmark and chase away its king Sweyn Forkbeard. He proclaimed himself the king of Sweden and Denmark which he ruled until his death which
2 CONC  would have taken place in 994 or 995.[citation needed] Adam says that Eric was baptised in Denmark, but later returned to the Norse gods. Adam of Bremen gives Emund Eriksson as Eric's predecessor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In all probability he founded the town of Sigtuna, which still exists and where the first Swedish coins were stamped for his son and successor Olof Skötkonung.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik Segersäll är den förste kung som man kan vara helt säker på att han verkligen har existerat. De källor som berättar om vad han uträttade som kung är dock osäkra och delvis motstridiga. Han antas ha blivit kung omkring år 970.
2 CONC  Till en början regerade han tillsammans med sin bror Olof. Efter dennes död styrde han svearnas rike ensamt (det är okänt ifall Götaland ingick i sveaväldet vid den här tiden). Han kom dock i konflikt med sin brorson Styrbjörn St
2 CONC arke som krävde att få inta sin faders plats som medkung till Erik. Konflikten kulminerade med slaget vid Fyrisvallarna i närheten av Uppsala som Erik vann. Det är denna seger som gav honom tillnamnet Segersäll (den segerrike). St
2 CONC yrbjörn hade fått stöd av Danmark och som hämnd anföll han riket. Erik Segersäll kontrollerade sannolikt delar av Danmark i slutet av sin regeringstid. Han sägs också ha låtit döpa sig i Danmark men avföll till hedendomen när han 
2 CONC återvände till Sverige.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Was baptized in Sweden, but eventually went back to worshiping the Norse gods.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT The book, 'Kings & Queens of Europe'
2 CONT
2 CONT The book, 'The Oxford History of the British Monarchy'
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erik_Segersäll
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik Segersäll (fornnordiska: Eiríkrinn sigrsæli,[källa behövs] av Adam av Bremen på latin kallad Hericus Victor,[1]) död 995,[1] var en svensk kung mellan åren 970 och 995 som möjligen tidvis även styrde över Danmark.[2][3] Han ä
2 CONC r den förste svenske kung som man vet något bestämt om[4] och han är den förste kung som man tror sig veta behärskade hela det område som idag utgör Svealand, Västergötland och Östergötland, det vill säga större delen av det områd
2 CONC e som skulle komma att utgöra det tidigmedeltida Sverige.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik brukar anses vara den som grundade Sigtuna
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric the Victorious (Old Norse: Eiríkr inn sigrsæli, Modern Swedish: Erik Segersäll), (945? – c. 995), was the first Swedish king (970–995) about whom anything definite is known. Whether he actually qualifies as king of Sweden is 
2 CONC debatable as his son Olof Skötkonung was the first ruler documented to have been accepted both by the Svear around Lake Mälaren and by the Götar around Lake Vättern.
2 CONT
2 CONT Referring to Eric the Victorious as Eric VI (or with any other numeral) is a later invention. The Swedish kings Erik XIV (1560–68) and Charles IX (1604–11) took their numbers after studying a highly fictitious History of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT His original territory lay in Uppland and neighbouring provinces. He acquired the name "victorious" as a result of his defeating an invasion from the south in the Battle of the Fýrisvellir close to Uppsala. But reports that Eric's
2 CONC  brother Olof was the father of his opponent in that battle, Styrbjörn the Strong, belong to the realm of myth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The extent of his kingdom is unknown. In addition to the Swedish heartland round lake Mälaren it may have extended down the Baltic Sea coast as far south as Blekinge.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Flateyjarbok, his success was due to the fact that he allied with the free farmers against the aristocratic jarl class, and it is obvious from archeological findings that the influence of the latter diminished dur
2 CONC ing the last part of the tenth century. He was also, probably, the introducer of the famous medieval Scandinavian system of universal conscription known as the ledung in the provinces around Mälaren.
2 CONT
2 CONT In all probability he founded the town of Sigtuna, which still exists and where the first Swedish coins were stamped for his son and successor Olof Skötkonung.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sagas
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric the Victorious appears in a number of Norse sagas, historical stories which nonetheless had a heathy dose of fiction. In various stories, he is described as the son of Björn Eriksson, and as having ruled together with his bro
2 CONC ther Olof. It was claimed that he married the infamous (and likely fictional) Sigrid the Haughty, daughter of the legendary Viking Skagul Toste, would later divorce her and give her Götaland as a fief. According to Eymund's saga h
2 CONC e took a new queen, Auð, the daughter of Haakon Sigurdsson, the ruler of Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before this happened, his brother Olof died, and a new co-ruler had to be appointed, but the Swedes are said to have refused to accept his rowdy nephew Styrbjörn the Strong as his co-ruler. Styrbjörn was given 60 longships by Eric
2 CONC  and sailed away to live as a Viking. He would become the ruler of Jomsborg and an ally and brother-in-law of the Danish king Harold Bluetooth. Styrbjörn returned to Sweden with an army, although Harald and the Danish troops suppo
2 CONC sedly turned back. Eric won the Battle of the Fýrisvellir at Old Uppsala, according to Styrbjarnar þáttr Svíakappa after sacrificing to Odin and promising that if victorious, he would give himself to Odin in ten years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam of Bremen relates that Eric was baptised in Denmark but that he forgot about the Christian faith after he returned to Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric the Victorious Old Norse : Eiríkr inn sigrsæli, Modern Swedish: Erik Segersäll, (945? – c. 995), was the first Swedish king  (970–995) about whom anything definite is known. Whether he actually qualifies as king of Sweden is 
2 CONC debatable as his son Olof Skötkonung  was the first ruler documented to have been accepted both by the Svear around Lake Mälaren  and by the Götar around Lake Vättern.
2 CONT
2 CONT Referring to Eric the Victorious as Eric VI (or with any other numeral) is a later invention. The Swedish kings Erik XIV  (1560–68) and Charles IX  (1604–11) took their numbers after studying a highly fictitious History of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT His original territory lay in Uppland  and neighbouring provinces. He was victorious over an invasion from the south in the Battle of the Fýrisvellir  close to Uppsala. Reports that Eric's brother Olof  was the father of Styrbjörn
2 CONC  the Strong  belong to the realm of myth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The extent of his kingdom is unknown. In addition to the Swedish heartland round lake Mälaren it may have extended down the Baltic Sea  coast as far south as Blekinge .
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Flateyjarbok , his success was due to the fact that he allied with the peasants against the nobility, and it is obvious from archeological findings that the influence of the latter diminished during the last part 
2 CONC of the tenth century. He was also, probably, the introducer of the famous medieval Scandinavian system of universal conscription known as the ledung  in the provinces around Mälaren.
2 CONT
2 CONT In all probability he founded the town of Sigtuna , which still exists and where the first Swedish coins were stamped for his son and successor Olof Skötkonung.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sagas
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric the Victorious appears in a number of Norse sagas , historical stories which nonetheless had a heathy dose of fiction. In various stories, he is described as the son of Björn Eriksson (III), and as having ruled together with 
2 CONC his brother Olof. It was claimed that he married the infamous (and likely fictional) Sigrid the Haughty , daughter of the legendary Viking Skagul Toste , would later divorce her and give her Götaland  as a fief. According to Eymun
2 CONC d's saga  he took a new queen, Auð , the daughter of Haakon Sigurdsson , the ruler of Norway .
2 CONT
2 CONT Before this happened, his brother Olof died, and a new co-ruler had to be appointed, but the Swedes refused to accept his rowdy nephew Styrbjörn the Strong as his co-ruler. Styrbjörn was given 60 longships by Eric and sailed away 
2 CONC to live as a Viking. Styrbjörn would become the ruler of Jomsborg  and an ally and brother-in-law of the Danish king Harold Bluetooth . Styrbjörn returned to Sweden with a major Danish  army, which Eric defeated in the Battle of t
2 CONC he Fýrisvellir  at Old Uppsala .
