2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20nobility.htm#Godwindied1053A
2 CONT
2 CONT GODWIN, son of WULFNOTH & his wife --- ([993]-Winchester 15 Apr 1053, bur Winchester Old Minster[398]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Florence of Worcester, Earl Godwin was son of Wulfnoth[399].   Ætheling Æthelstan, under his will dated [1014], made a bequest to "Godwine, Wulfnoth's son, the estate at Compton which his father possessed", listed sec
2 CONC ond among the bequests to non-members of the royal family[400].  Installed as Earl in 1018 by King Canute, and as bajulus[401] over almost the whole kingdom[402].  He heads the lists of witnesses to all the king's lay charters aft
2 CONC er 1023[403], indicating an unrivalled position of power.  By the end of King Canute's reign he was referred to as Ealdorman of Wessex[404].  He supported Queen Emma in opposing the accession of King Harold I in 1036[405].  He app
2 CONC ears to have played a major role in the accession and acceptance in England of King Edward "the Confessor" in 1042, and in 1045 married his daughter to the king.  However, his relations with the king became tense following a dispu
2 CONC te over the appointment of a new archbishop of Canterbury in 1050.  In 1051, Earl Godwin refused the king's order to punish an affray at Canterbury, in which one of the men of Eustache Comte de Boulogne was killed.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The dispute escalated, and 1 Sep 1051 Godwin and his two older sons made a show of force to the king near Tetbury.  Earls Leofric and Siward rallied to the king's support, and battle was avoided.  Godwin and his family were given 
2 CONC five days' safe conduct to leave the country by the King's Council held on 8 Sep 1051[406].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He fled with his wife and sons Svein, Tostig and Gyrth to Bruges.  Godwin returned with an army in Sep 1052, joined forces with his son Harold who returned from Ireland, and sailed into London.  The family gathered popular support
2 CONC  and was fully reinstated[407].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that Earl Godwin died soon after, three days after suffering a stroke[408].  Florence of Worcester records his death and burial at Winchester[409]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1019) GYTHA, daughter of THORGIL Sprakalegg & his wife --- (-St Omer after 1069).  She is named by Florence of Worcester[410].  Her origin is deduced from Ulf Jarl of Denmark being described as the uncle of her son Svein by Flo
2 CONC rence of Worcester[411].  Morkinskinna names “Gytha, the daughter of Thorgils sprakaleggr and the sister of Jarl Úlfr” as the wife of “Godwin”[412].  Adam of Bremen records that "Wolf sororem" married "duci Gudvino", and was mothe
2 CONC r of "ducis Suein, Tostin et Haroldum [genuit parricidas]"[413], although this last reference is unexplained.  She was living in Exeter when King William I attacked it in 1068.  She took refuge at Flat Holme, an island in the Bris
2 CONC tol Channel[414].  Florence of Worcester records that "Gytha…comitissa…mater Haroldi regis Anglorum ac soror Suani regis Danorum" fled to Flanders, in a passage dealing with events in early 1068[415].  She became a nun at Saint-Om
2 CONC er.  According to Orderic Vitaliis, she "secretly gathered together a great store of treasure and … fled to France never to return"[416]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin & his wife had ten children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         EADGYTH ([1020/22]-Winchester 18 Dec 1075, bur Westminster Abbey).  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that in 1045 "king Edward took to wife Edith the daughter of Earl Godwin, ten days before Candlemas"[417].  She was e
2 CONC ducated in Wilton nunnery.  Florence of Worcester records that her husband repudiated her in 1051 when he sent the rest of her family into exile and sent her "in disgrace with only a single handmaid" to Wherwell Abbey[418].  She w
2 CONC as brought back to court when her father was restored the following year[419].  She commissioned the Vita Ædwardi Regis from a foreign clerk, probably from Saint-Omer, setting out the history of her family.  She continued to live 
2 CONC around Winchester after the Norman conquest, and appears to have been treated well by King William I[420].  Florence of Worcester records the death "XIV Kal Jan" in [1074] of "Edgitha regis Haroldi germana quondam Anglorum regina"
2 CONC  at Winchester and her burial at Westminster[421].  m (23 Jan 1045) EDWARD "the Confessor" King of England, son of ÆTHELRED II King of England & his second wife Emma of Normandy ([1005]-Palace of Westminster 5 Jan 1066, bur Westmi
2 CONC nster Abbey). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         SVEIN ([1021/23]-Constantinople[422] 29 Sep 1052).  Florence of Worcester specifies that Svein was the eldest son of "earl Godwin and Githa"[423].  He was created Earl in Berkshire, Gloucestershire, Herefordshire, Oxfor
2 CONC dshire and Somerset in 1043 by King Edward "the Confessor".  He led an expedition into South Wales in 1046, allied with Gruffydd ap Llywellyn King of Gwynedd in North Wales.  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that he seduced Eadgi
2 CONC fu Abbess of Leominster on his way back[424], for which he was outlawed by King Edward, who divided his earldom between his brother Harold and his cousin Bjørn Ulfsen.  The Chronicle records that Svein fled to Bruges in 1047, then
2 CONC  to Denmark the year after[425].  He returned to England in 1049, murdered his cousin Bjørn Ulfsen, and was exiled again in 1049 by an assembly of the whole army at Sandwich, this time taking refuge with Baudouin IV Count of Fland
2 CONC ers[426].  After his father secured his recall in [1050], he was pardoned and partially reinstated[427].  After joining his father's threatened armed rebellion against the king in 1051, he was outlawed yet again and fled with his 
2 CONC parents to Bruges.  According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, he died while returning from a pilgrimage to Jerusalem[428], before the family's restoration.  According to Florence of Worcester[429], Svein died "in Lycia from illness 
2 CONC brought on by the severity of the cold".  Svein is not mentioned in the Vita Ædwardi, commissioned by his sister Queen Eadgyth, presumably because his dissolute life was considered best forgotten.  Svein had one illegitimate child
2 CONC , maybe by EADGIFU Abbess of Leominster, whom he was refused permission to marry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         HAAKON ([1046/47]-after 1065).  "Hacun filius Suani filii sui [Godwini]" was sent as a hostage to Normandy in 1051, possibly after Duke Guillaume II's visit to England[430].  Eadmer of Canterbury records that he was fre
2 CONC ed and returned to England with his uncle Harold Godwinsson in [1064/65][431]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         HAROLD ([1022/25]-killed in battle Hastings 14 Oct 1066, bur [Waltham Abbey]).  His parentage is confirmed in several places in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle[432].  He succeeded as HAROLD II King of England and was crowned 
2 CONC 6 Jan 1066. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -        KINGS of ENGLAND. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         TOSTIG ([1025/30]-killed in battle Stamford Bridge 25 Sep 1066).  After joining his father's threatened armed rebellion against the king in 1051, Tostig fled to Bruges with his parents, although he returned the followin
2 CONC g year.  He was created Earl of Northumbria in 1055 to succeed Earl Siward[433].  Northumbria rebelled against him in Oct 1065, after several years of Scottish raids which Tostig had failed to halt.  In his place, the thegns of Yo
2 CONC rkshire elected Morcar, younger brother of Edwin Earl of Mercia, as Earl of Northumbria.  King Edward attempted unsuccessfully to crush the rebellion on Tostig's behalf, but Tostig was forced to flee to Flanders with his wife in D
2 CONC ec 1065[434].  Baudouin V Count of Flanders installed him as castellan of Saint-Omer[435].  Returning to England in search of revenge, he was repulsed from landing at Sandwich in Kent in May 1066, ravaged Norfolk and then sailed t
2 CONC o the Humber where he was defeated by Edwin Earl of Mercia.  Tostig sailed to Scotland, where he joined forces with Harald III "Hardråde" King of Norway who had recently arrived to invade England.  Morkinskinna records that Tostig
2 CONC  first sailed to Denmark to seek report from King Svend II, and then to Norway to King Harald III, sending “Gunnhildr´s son Guthormr” to offer Northumbria to the king in return for his help[436].  After defeating the Northumbrians
2 CONC  at Gate Fulford near York 20 Sep 1066, their combined army was defeated by King Harold's forces at Stamford Bridge 25 Sep 1066, where both he and the Norwegian king were killed[437].  m (before Sep 1051[438]) as her first husband
2 CONC , JUDITH de Flandre, daughter of BAUDOUIN IV Count of Flanders & his second wife [Eléonore] de Normandie ([1033]-5 Mar 1094, bur St Martin Monastery).  The Annalista Saxo names "Iudhita…amita Rodberti comitis de Flandria ex cognat
2 CONC ione beati Ethmundi regis" as husband of "Haroldi" (in error for Tostig) but correctly names her second husband "Welphus filius Azzonis marchionis Italorum"[439].  The Genealogia Welforum names "filiam comitis Flandrie, reginam An
2 CONC glie, Iuditam nomine" as wife of Welf[440].  Florence of Worcester says that Judith was "daughter of Baldwin Count of Flanders" but does not specify which Count Baldwin nor is this clear from the context[441].  According to the Vi
2 CONC ta Ædwardi Regis, she was the sister of Count Baudouin V[442].  Alberic de Trois Fontaines asserts that Judith was one of the children of Baudouin V Count of Flanders and his wife Adela de France[443], but there are other clear er
2 CONC rors in his listing of this couple's children so the statement should be viewed with caution.  Judith is also listed as the daughter of Count Baudouin V (after Mathilde) in a manuscript whose attribution to Orderic Vitalis is disp
2 CONC uted, which also shows her first marriage[444].  Judith moved to Denmark after her first husband was killed.  The Chronicon of Bernold records the death "1094 IV Non Mar" of "Iuditha uxor ducis Welfonis Baioariæ" and her burial "a
2 CONC pud monasterium…Sancti Martini" built by her husband[445].  The necrology of Raitenbuch records the death "III Non Mar" of "Iudinta regina Anglie, filia marchionis de Este uxor Welfonis nostri fundatoris"[446], exaggerating her st
2 CONC atus resulting from her first marriage and confusing her paternity.  The necrology of Weingarten records the death "III Non Mar" of "Judita dux regina Anglie"[447], also exaggerating her status resulting from her first marriage.  
2 CONC Tostig & his wife had [--- children]:
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         infant children .  According to the Vita Ædwardi Regis, the children of Tostig and Judith were "unweaned" when their father died[448]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig had [three] [probably illegitimate] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         SKULI "kongsfostre" .  Snorre names "Skule, a son of Earl Toste, who since has been called the king's foster-son, and his brother Ketil Krok…of high family in England" when recording that they accompanied Olav King of N
2 CONC orway[449], the context suggesting that he and his brother were at least young adults at the time.  If this is correct, they must have been their father's illegitimate sons and were not the "unweaned" children referred to above.  
2 CONC Morkinskinna records that “Skúli, the son of Jarl Tostig Godwinson, and Ketill krókr from Hálogaland came to Norway” with Olaf III King of Norway after the failed invasion of England in 1066[450].  Morkinskinna records that “not l
2 CONC ong after King Haraldr´s fall Skúli went west to England to ask for the return of King Haraldr´s body” which was “readily granted to him”[451]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -        NORWEGIAN NOBILITY. 
2 CONT
2 CONT c)          [KETIL "krókr" .  Snorre names "Skule, a son of Earl Toste, who since has been called the king's foster-son, and his brother Ketil Krok…of high family in England" when recording that they accompanied Olav King of Norwa
2 CONC y[452], the context suggesting that he and his brother were at least young adults at the time.  If this is correct, they must have been their father's illegitimate sons and were not the "unweaned" children referred to above.  Mork
2 CONC inskinna records that “Skúli, the son of Jarl Tostig Godwinson, and Ketill krókr from Hálogaland came to Norway” with Olav III King of Norway after the failed invasion of England in 1066, without specifying any relationship betwee
2 CONC n Skuli and Ketil[453].  One possibility is that Ketil was the uterine brother of Skuli and not the son of Tostig.  Morkinskinna records that King Olav III “arranged a good marriage for Ketil and appointed him a district chieftain
2 CONC  in the north”, adding that “many distinguished men are descended from Ketil”[454].  m ---.  Morkinskinna records that Olav III King of Norway “arranged a good marriage for Ketil”[455].  The name of Ketil´s wife is not known.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT d)          [OLAV (-1066 or after).  Simeon of Durham names "Tosti's son Olave and earl from the isle of Orkney named Paul" in 1066[456].  The reference to this son named Olav has not been corroborated in other sources so far cons
2 CONC ulted.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.         GYRTH ([1032]-killed in battle Hastings 14 Oct 1066[457]).  Florence of Worcester records that he fled with his parents to Bruges in 1051, after the family's disgrace[458].  He was appointed Earl of the East Angles in 1
2 CONC 057, in succession to Earl Ælfgar who had succeeded his father as Earl of Mercia.  After Gyrth's death, his lands were given to Ralph "the Staller" by William I King of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         [ÆLFGAR .  Orderic Vitalis names Ælfgar after Gyrth and before Leofwine in his list of the children of Earl Godwin[459].  He is not named among Earl Godwin's sons by Florence of Worcester[460] and no other reference to 
2 CONC him has so far been found.]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.         LEOFWINE (-killed in battle Hastings 14 Oct 1066457).  Florence of Worcester records that he fled to Ireland with his brother Harold in 1051, after the family's disgrace[461].  He was appointed Earl of Kent and Essex in
2 CONC  1057.  After his death, the county of Kent was awarded to Odo, Bishop of Bayeux, half-brother of William I King of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.         WULFNOTH ([1036/46][462]-Feb [1094][463]).  Eadmer records that "Wulfnothus itaque filius Godwini" was sent as a hostage to Normandy with his nephew Haakon in 1051[464], possibly after Duke Guillaume II's visit to Engla
2 CONC nd but presumably before Sep of that year as he is not named among the sons of Godwin who fled England on the family's disgrace.  His brother Harold unsuccessfully negotiated for his release in [1064/65] during his visit to Duke G
2 CONC uillaume's court[465].  Florence of Worcester records that "comites Morkarum et Rogerum, Siwardum cognomento Barn, et Wlnothum regis Haroldi germanum" were released by King William on his deathbed in 1087[466].  He was taken to En
2 CONC gland by King William II but placed in confinement again in Winchester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.         GUNHILD (-Bruges 24 Aug 1087).  Having taken a vow of chastity, she went to Saint-Omer in Flanders after 1066, then Bruges before moving to Denmark.  She returned to Bruges before she died[467]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.      ÆLFGIFU (-before Jan 1066[468]).  During the period of captivity of her brother Harold Godwinsson at the court of Guillaume II Duke of Normandy, Harold promised one of his sisters in marriage to one of Duke Guillaume's no
2 CONC bles[469], most probably Ælfgifu.  She is mentioned by name in Domesday Book.   
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin (sometimes Godwine) (c.1001 - April 15, 1053), was one of the most powerful lords in England under the Danish king Canute the Great and his successors. Canute made him the first Earl of Wessex. Godwin was the father of Haro
2 CONC ld II and of Edith of Wessex, wife of Edward the Confessor.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin was a seventh generation descendant of King Ethelred of Wessex (c. 837-871). Ethelred was succeeded by his younger brother Alfred the Great because his own son Aethelhelm of Wessex (c. 870-927) was still a minor at the time
2 CONC  of his death. Aethelhelm was later granted lands by his uncle but was never able to claim the throne. His daughter Elfleda of Wessex (c.890-918) however became Queen consort to King Edward the Elder (c.871-924).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Aethelfrith of Wessex (c.900-927) lived the life of a common landlord. His son Eadric of Wessex was granted possession of Washington in the South Downs of West Sussex. His own son Aethelwerd of Wessex (c.930-998) is kn
2 CONC own as "the Historian". Aethelmar Cild (c.960-1015), son of the later, was a benefactor of Eynsham Abbey. His son was Wulfnoth Cild (c.983-1015) who was Thegn of Sussex but is otherwise obscure. Godwin was son to Wulfnoth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1014, Godwin was first married to Thyra Sveinsdóttir, daughter of Sweyn I , King of Denmark, Norway and England. Thyra however died in 1018. Her death apparently did not prevent her brother Canute from creating Godwin Earl 
2 CONC of Wessex during the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1019, Godwin was married again to Gytha Thorkelsdóttir, granddaughter of the legendary Viking Styrbjörn Starke and great-granddaughter to Harold Bluetooth. The marriage resulted in the birth of at least twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Driella of Wessex (c. 1019)
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith of Wessex, (c. 1020 - December 19, 1075), queen consort of Edward the Confessor
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweyn Godwinson, Earl of Mercia (c. 1021 - 1052). At some point he declared himself an illegitimate son of Canute the Great but this is considered to be a false claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harold II of England (c. 1022 - October 14, 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT Gungilda of Wessex, a nun (c. 1024 - 1080)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ylgiva of Wessex (c. 1025)
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria (c. 1026 - September 25, 1066).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ydgiva of Wessex (c. 1028)
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyrth Godwinson (c. 1032 - October 14, 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofwine Godwinson , Earl of Kent (c. 1035 - October 14, 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT Wulfnoth Godwinson (c. 1036 - 1087)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ylfgar of Wessex (c. 1038)
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 12, 1035, Canute died. His kingdoms were divided among three rival rulers. Harold Harefoot, illegitimate son by Aelgifu of Northampton, usurped the throne of England. Harthacanute, legitimate son by Emma of Normandy, r
2 CONC eigned in Denmark. Norway rebelled under Magnus the Noble. On 1037, the throne of England was reportedly claimed by Alfred of Wessex, son of Emma of Normandy and Ethelred the Unready and half-brother of Harthacanute. Godwin is rep
2 CONC orted to have either captured Alfred himself or to have deceived him by pretending to be his ally and then surrendering him to the forces of Harold Harefoot. Either way Alfred was blinded and soon died.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 17, 1040, Harold Harefoot died and Godwin supported the accession of Harthacanute to the throne of England. When Harthacanute himself died (June 8, 1042), Godwin supported the claim of his half-brother Edward the Confesso
2 CONC r to the throne. Edward was another son of Emma and Ethelred, having spent most of the previous thirtyyears in Normandy. His reign restored the native royal house of Wessex to the throne of England. Despite his alleged responsibil
2 CONC ity for the death of Edward's brother Alfred, Godwin secured the marriage of his daughter Edith (Eadgyth) to Edward in 1045. Godwin soon became the leader of opposition to growing Norman influence as Edward drew advisors, nobles a
2 CONC nd priests from his former place of refuge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Exiled from the kingdom in September 1051 for refusing to punish the people of Dover for a violent clash with the visiting Eustace II, Count of Boulogne, Godwin returned the following year with an armed force, compelling Edward to
2 CONC  restore his earldom.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 15, 1053, Godwin died. His son Harold succeeded him as Earl of Wessex, an area then covering roughly the southernmost third of England. Harold later succeeded Edward the Confessor and became King of England in his own rig
2 CONC ht.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin, earl av Wessex
2 CONT
2 CONT Från Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin, född ca 1001, död 5 april 1053, var en av de mäktigaste adelsmännen i England under Knut den store och hans efterträdare. Knut gjorde honom till den förste earlen av Wessex. Godwin var far till Harald II, Tostig Godwinson 
2 CONC och Edith av Wessex, Edvard Bekännarens drottning.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin var den sjunde generationens ättling till kung Ethelred av Wessex, Alfred den stores äldre bror. Hans ättlingar förbisågs i den kungliga successionsordningen, men blev framstående adelsmän i kungariket. Godwins far var Wulf
2 CONC noth Cild (ca 983-1015) thegn av Sussex. Wulfnoth ledde en del av den kungliga flottan till sjöröveri, vilket fick konsekvensen att hans landområden förverkades och att han landsförvisades. Det ankom därefter på hans unge son, God
2 CONC win, att förbättra familjens förmögenhet efter faderns död 1014.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin stödde Edmund Järnsida, kung Ethelred den rådvilles son. Då Edmund revolterade mot fadern, invaderade Knut den store England. Edmund dödades tillsammans med många av sina anhängare, men Godwin undkom och svor trohet till Kn
2 CONC ut den store. Han blev en av Knuts rådgivare och följde honom till Danmark för att kväsa ett uppror där. 1022 gifte han sig med Knuts syster, Thyra Sveinsdóttir. Hon dog strax därefter, men Godwin fortsatte att vinna anseende och 
2 CONC 1023 var han den mäktigaste earlen i England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin gifte sig med en annan dansk adelsdam, Gytha Thorkelsdóttir, barnbarn till den legendariske vikingen Styrbjörn Starke och barnbarnsbarn till Harald Blåtand. Äktenskapet ledde till många barn:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Sweyn Godwinson, earl av Mercia (ca. 1025-1052). Han hävdade att han var Knut den stores illegitime son, men detta anses vara falskt.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Harald II av England (ca. 1022-14 oktober 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Tostig Godwinson, earl av Northumbria (ca. 1026-25 september 1066).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Edith av Wessex, (ca. 1030- 19 december 1075), Edvard Bekännarens drottning
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Gyrth Godwinson (ca. 1030-14 oktober 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.Gunhilda av Wessex, (ca. 1035-1080), nunna
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.Ælfgifu av Wessex (ca.1035)
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.Leofwine Godwinson, earl av Kent (ca. 1035-14 oktober 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.Wulfnoth Godwinson (ca.1040)
2 CONT
2 CONT 12 november 1035 dog Knut den store. Hans kungarike delades upp mellan tre rivaliserande härskare. Harald Harfot, en illegitim son med Aelgifu av Northampton, tillskanskade sig Englands tron medan Hardeknut, en legitim son med Emm
2 CONC a av Normandie regerade i Danmark. Norge gjorde uppror genom Magnus I av Norge. 1037 krävde Emmas och Ethelreds son, Hardeknuts halvbror, Alfred av Wessex tronen. Godwin ska antingen själv ha gripit Alfred eller lurat honom att va
2 CONC ra en allierad och överlämnat honom till Harald Harfots trupper. I alla händelser gjordes Alfred blind och han dog inom kort.
2 CONT
2 CONT 17 mars 1040 dog Harald Harfot, varefter Godwin stödde Hardeknut som tronföljare. Då Hardeknut i sin tur dog 8 juni 1042 stödde Godwin hans halvbrors, Edvard Bekännaren tronanspråk. Edvard var ytterligare en son till Ethelred och 
2 CONC Emma som hade tillbringat större delen av de gångna trettio åren i Normandie. Hans regering återställde den kungliga familjen Wessex på den engelska tronen. Trots Godwins ansvar för Edvards bror Alfreds död säkrade makten genom gi
2 CONC ftermålet mellan sin dotter Eadgyth (Edith av Wessex) 1045. Godwin blev ledare för oppositionen mot det ökande normandiska inflytandet, då Edvard anlitade rådgivare, adelsmän och präster från sin tidigare tillflyktsort.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin landsförvisades från kungariket i september 1051 för att han vägrat att straffa folket i Dover för en våldsam sammandrabbning med den besökande Eustace II, greve av Bologna. Godwin återvände dock följande år och tvingade me
2 CONC d militära styrkor Edvard att återställa hans samhällsposition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin dog 15 april 1053. Hans son Harald efterträdde honom som earl av Wessex, ett område som på den tiden täckte den södra tredjedelen av England. Harald efterträdde senare Edvard Bekännaren och blev kung av England.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin, Earl of Wessex
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT  This article needs additional citations for verification.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please help improve this article by adding reliable references. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (June 2007) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin (sometimes Godwine, Goodwin, Godwyn, Goodwyn and sometimes known as Godwin of Wessex) (c. 1001–April 15, 1053), was one of the most powerful lords in England under the Danish king Canute the Great and his successors. Canute
2 CONC  made him the first Earl of Wessex. Godwin was the father of Harold II and of Edith of Wessex, wife of Edward the Confessor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Rise: support of Edmund, then Canute 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Height of power: support of Harold 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Later conflicts, decline, and death 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Progeny 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.1 Family Trees 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Sources 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 External links 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Rise: support of Edmund, then Canute
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin's father was probably Wulfnoth Cild, Cild being a nickname (c. 983-1015) who was Thegn of Sussex, although later documents describe his father as a churl. Wulfnoth was supposedly a sixth generation descendant of King Ethelr
2 CONC ed of Wessex (also caled Aethelred I), the elder brother of Alfred the Great; Ethelred's descendants were passed over in the royal succession, but became prominent nobles in the kingdom. The patrilineal descent of Godwin from a Ki
2 CONC ng of Wessex is still being researched, however, and should not be taken as certain. Wulfnoth led a section of the royal fleet into piracy and as a consequence had his lands forfeited, and was exiled. It was left to his young son,
2 CONC  Godwin, to improve the family fortunes after his father's death in 1014. In his day, Earl Godwin was seen as very much of a new man, who had been "made" by two advantageous marriages to Danish noblewomen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin was a major supporter of Edmund Ironside, the son of King Aethelred the Unready. While Edmund was in rebellion against his father, Canute and his army invaded England. Edmund was killed, along with many of his supporters, b
2 CONC ut Godwin survived and pledged his loyalty to Canute. He befriended Canute's brother-in-law, Earl Ulf, and became one of Canute's advisors, accompanying him to Denmark to suppress a rebellion there. By 1018 he was an earl, becomin
2 CONC g Earl of the West Saxons in about 1019. In 1022 he married Thyra Sveinsdóttir (also called Thyra Swensdottir), Canute's sister. She died soon afterwards without issue, but Godwin continued to gain prestige and by 1023 he was the 
2 CONC most powerful earl in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Height of power: support of Harold
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 12, 1035, Canute died. His kingdoms were divided among three rival rulers. Harold Harefoot, Canute's illegitimate son by Aelgifu of Northampton, seized the throne of England. Harthacanute, Canute's legitimate son by Em
2 CONC ma of Normandy, reigned in Denmark. Norway rebelled under Magnus the Noble. In 1037, the throne of England was reportedly claimed by Alfred of Wessex, younger son of Emma of Normandy and Ethelred the Unready and half-brother of Ha
2 CONC rthacanute. Godwin is reported to have either captured Alfred himself or to have deceived him by pretending to be his ally and then surrendering him to the forces of Harold Harefoot. Either way Alfred was blinded and soon died at 
2 CONC Ely.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 17, 1040, Harold Harefoot died and Godwin supported the accession of his half-brother Harthacanute to the throne of England. When Harthacanute himself died (June 8, 1042), Godwin finally supported the claim of his half-br
2 CONC other Edward the Confessor to the throne. Edward was another son of Emma and Ethelred, having spent most of the previous thirty years in Normandy. His reign restored the native royal house of Wessex to the throne of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Later conflicts, decline, and death
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his alleged responsibility for the death of Edward's brother Alfred, Godwin secured the marriage of his daughter Edith (Eadgyth) to Edward in 1045. As Edward drew advisors, nobles and priests from his former place of refug
2 CONC e in a bid to develop his own power base, Godwin soon became the leader of opposition to growing Norman influence. After a violent clash between the people of Dover and the visiting Eustace II, Count of Boulogne, Edward's new brot
2 CONC her-in-law, Godwin was ordered to punish the people of Dover (as he and Earl Leofric had done in Worcester, in Leofric's own earldom). This time, however, Godwin refused, choosing to champion his own countrymen against a (visiting
2 CONC ) foreign ruler and his own king. Edward rightly saw this as a test of power, and managed to enlist the support of the other earls Earl Siward from Northumbria and Earl Leofric from Mercia. Godwin and his sons were exiled from the
2 CONC  kingdom in September 1051. However, they returned the following year with an armed force, which gained the support of the navy, burghers, and peasants, so compelling Edward to restore his earldom. This however set a precedent to 
2 CONC be followed by a rival earl some years later, and then by Godwin's own son in 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 15, 1053, Godwin died at Winchester, supposedly while denying that he had any role in the death of the King's brother Alfred in 1036. His son Harold succeeded him as Earl of Wessex, an area then covering roughly the south
2 CONC ernmost third of England. With the death of Earl Siward (1055) and later Earl Algar (1062), the children of Godwin were poised to assume sole control. Tostig was helped into the earldom of Northumbria, thus controlling the north. 
2 CONC The Mercian earl was sidelined, especially after Harold and Tostig broke the Welsh-Mercian alliance in 1063. Harold later succeeded Edward the Confessor and became King of England in his own right. At this point, both Harold's rem
2 CONC aining brothers in England were earls in their own right, Harold was himself king and in control of Wessex, and he had married the sister of Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar of Northumbria (who had succeeded his brother Tostig
2 CONC ). Godwin's family looked set to inaugurate a new royal dynasty, much as the Capetians had replaced the Carolingians in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Progeny
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin married a second time to a Danish noblewoman, Gytha Thorkelsdóttir (also known as Githa), said to be the granddaughter of the legendary Viking Styrbjörn Starke and great-granddaughter to Harold Bluetooth, king of Denmark an
2 CONC d thus also ancestor to King Canute. The marriage resulted in the birth of many children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweyn Godwinson, Earl of Herefordshire (c. 1023-1052). At some point he declared himself an illegitimate son of Canute the Great but this is considered to be a false claim. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Harold II of England (c. 1022-October 14, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria (c. 1026-September 25, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith of Wessex, (c. 1030-December 19, 1075), queen consort of Edward the Confessor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyrth Godwinson (c. 1030-October 14, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunhilda of Wessex, a nun (c. 1035-1080) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ælfgifu of Wessex (c. 1035) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofwine Godwinson, Earl of Kent (c. 1035-October 14, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wulfnoth Godwinson (c. 1040) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marigard of Wessex (February 6, 1033 - August 6, 1083) 
2 CONT
