2 CONT Height of power: support of Harold
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 November 1035, Cnut died. His kingdoms were divided among three rival rulers. Harold Harefoot, Cnut's illegitimate son with Ælfgifu of Northampton, seized the throne of England. Harthacnut, Cnut's legitimate son with Emma of
2 CONC  Normandy, reigned in Denmark. Norway rebelled under Magnus the Noble. In 1035, the throne of England was reportedly claimed by Alfred Ætheling, younger son of Emma of Normandy and Æthelred the Unready, and half-brother of Harthac
2 CONC nut. Godwin is reported to have either captured Alfred himself or to have deceived him by pretending to be his ally and then surrendering him to the forces of Harold Harefoot. Either way Alfred was blinded and soon died at Ely.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1040, Harold Harefoot died and Godwin supported the accession of his half-brother Harthacnut to the throne of England. When Harthacnut himself died in 1042 Godwin finally supported the claim of his half-brother Edward the Confe
2 CONC ssor to the throne. Edward was another son of Emma and Æthelred, having spent most of the previous thirty years in Normandy. His reign restored the native royal house of Wessex to the throne of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later conflicts, decline, and death
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his alleged responsibility for the death of Edward's brother Alfred, Godwin secured the marriage of his daughter Edith (Eadgyth) to Edward in 1045. As Edward drew advisors, nobles and priests from his former place of refug
2 CONC e in a bid to develop his own power base, Godwin soon became the leader of opposition to growing Norman influence. After a violent clash between the people of Dover and the visiting Eustace II, Count of Boulogne, Edward's brother-
2 CONC in-law, Godwin was ordered to punish the people of Dover (as he and Leofric, Earl of Mercia had done in Worcester, in Leofric's own earldom). This time, however, Godwin refused, choosing to champion his own countrymen against a (v
2 CONC isiting) foreign ruler and his own king. Edward rightly saw this as a test of power, and managed to enlist the support of Siward, Earl of Northumbria and Earl Leofric. Godwin and his sons were exiled from the kingdom in September 
2 CONC 1051. However, they returned the following year with an armed force, which gained the support of the navy, burghers, and peasants, so compelling Edward to restore his earldom. This however set a precedent to be followed by a rival
2 CONC  earl some years later, and then by Godwin's own son in 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 April 1053 Godwin died suddenly, after collapsing during a royal banquet at Winchester. Some colourful accounts claim that he choked on a piece of bread while denying any disloyalty to the king.[4] However this appears to be
2 CONC  later Norman propaganda. Contemporary accounts indicate that he just had a sudden illness, possibly a stroke.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Harold succeeded him as Earl of Wessex, an area then covering roughly the southernmost third of England. With the death of Earl Siward (1055) and later Earl Ælfgar (1062), the children of Godwin were poised to assume sole 
2 CONC control. Tostig was helped into the earldom of Northumbria, thus controlling the north. The Mercian earl was sidelined, especially after Harold and Tostig broke the Welsh-Mercian alliance in 1063. Harold later succeeded Edward the
2 CONC  Confessor and became King of England in his own right. At this point, both Harold's remaining brothers in England were earls in their own right, Harold was himself king and in control of Wessex, and he had married the sister of E
2 CONC arl Edwin of Mercia and Morcar, Earl of Northumbria (who had succeeded his brother Tostig). Godwin's family looked set to inaugurate a new royal dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Sweyn Godwinson, Earl of Herefordshire (c. 1023-1052). At some point he declared himself an illegitimate son of Cnut the Great but this is considered to be a false claim.
2 CONT    2. Harold II of England (c. 1022-14 October 1066)
2 CONT    3. Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria (c. 1026-25 September 1066)
2 CONT    4. Edith of Wessex, (c. 1030-19 December 1075), queen consort of Edward the Confessor
2 CONT    5. Gyrth Godwinson (c. 1030-14 October 1066)
2 CONT    6. Gunhilda of Wessex, a nun (c. 1035-1080)
2 CONT    7. Ælfgifu of Wessex (c. 1035)
2 CONT    8. Leofwine Godwinson, Earl of Kent (c. 1035-14 October 1066)
2 CONT    9. Wulfnoth Godwinson (c. 1040)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family Trees
2 CONT
2 CONT     * House of Wessex family tree
2 CONT     * Godwin family tree
2 CONT     * Cnut's family tree In popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin has been portrayed by Torin Thatcher in the film Lady Godiva of Coventry (1955) and by Bill Wallis in an episode of the British educational TV series Historyonics entitled "1066" (2004). Lewis Collins is set to portray him 
2 CONC in upcoming movie 1066 (2011). Godwin is also the lead character of Justin Hill's novel, Shieldwall (2011).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT     * Mason, Emma. The House of Godwine: The History of Dynasty. Hambledon Press, 2003.
2 CONT     * Stenton, F.M. Anglo-Saxon England (Oxford History of England), 2001.
2 CONT     * Thorne, J.O. and Collocott, T.C. Chambers Biographical Dictionary, Revised Edition. (Edinburgh: Chambers, 1984) ISBN 0-550-16010-8
2 CONT     * Walker, Ian. Harold: The Last Anglo-Saxon King, 1997.
2 CONT
