1 OCCU Queen of England, Râegente d'Angleterre (1035+1037), Queen consort of England, Queen Consort of England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Emma of Normandy, daughter of Richard I and Gunnor. In Anglo-Saxon England, she was called Ælfgifu (NB: avoid mix-ups with others of that name)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married twice:
2 CONT *1. Æthelred the Unready
2 CONT *2. Canute the Great
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with Ethelred the Unready were:
2 CONT *1. Godgifu (Goda)
2 CONT *2. Edward the Confessor 
2 CONT *3. Alfred Aetheling 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with Canute the Great were
2 CONT *1. Harthacanute 
2 CONT *2. Gunhilda of Denmark 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Normandy (covering her birth family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORMANDY.htm#RichardIdied996A
2 CONT
2 CONT RICHARD, son of GUILLAUME Comte [ de Normandie] & his first wife Sprota --- (Fécamp [932]-Fécamp 20 Nov 996, bur Fécamp[73]).  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges names Richard as son of Guillaume and Sprota, recording that news of his birth was brought to his father when he was returning from his victory against the rebels led by "Riulf"[74].  Flodoard records "filio
2 CONC  ipsius Willelmi, nato de concubina Brittana" being granted the land of the Normans by King Louis after his father's death[75].  
2 CONT *Richard is described as "a boy" on the death of his father by Dudo of Saint-Quentin[76], and as "ten years old" at the time by Orderic Vitalis[77]. Guillaume de Jumièges records that Richard succeeded his father under the guardia
2 CONC nship of "Bernard le Danois"[78].  
2 CONT *After the death of Richard's father, Louis IV "d'Outremer" King of France briefly controlled Rouen, and kept Richard prisoner, before the latter was able to escape[79], whereupon he succeeded as RICHARD I "Sans Peur" Comte [de No
2 CONC rmandie].  He used the title Comte de Rouen/comes Rothomagensium, and from 966 Marquis des Normands/marchio Normannorum[80].  
2 CONT *Soon after succeeding, he suppressed the rebellion of Rodulf "Torta", who was banished and fled to Paris[81].  These events are not dated, but are recounted with the betrothal of Richard to the daughter of Hugues Duc des Francs, 
2 CONC which took place in 945.  His forces defeated the army of Otto I King of Germany after it attempted to capture Rouen in revenge for the escape of comte Richard from captivity[82].  Comte Richard defeated French forces after King L
2 CONC othaire of France captured Evreux.  
2 CONT *Hugues "le Grand" Duc des Francs nominated comte Richard as guardian of his son, the future Hugues "Capet" King of France, in 956, the arrangement being confirmed by Richard's betrothal to Hugues's sister[83].  He invited William
2 CONC  of Volpiano, Italian abbot of Saint-Bénigne at Dijon, to reform the Norman abbeys, installing monks at Mont-Saint-Michel and Fécamp[84].  He agreed a non-aggression pact with Æthelred II King of England 1 Mar 991, designed no dou
2 CONC bt to prevent either side from sheltering Viking marauders[85].  
2 CONT *"Ricardus filius Willelmi, dux Normannie" founded Louviers "in Ebroicensi pago" by undated charter[86].  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges records the death of Duke Richard at Fécamp in 996[87].  The Brevis Relatio de Origine Willelmi Conquestoris records that "Ricardus…filius Willelmi et alius Ricardus" were buried "Fiscanni"[88]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (betrothed 956, Rouen 960) EMMA, daughter of HUGUES "le Grand" Duc des Francs, Comte de Paris & his third wife Hedwig of Germany ([943]-after 19 Mar 968).  
2 CONT *The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum records the marriage in 956 of "Richardus filius Guillelmi principis Normannorum" with "filiam Hugonis ducis", although she is not named[89].  Guillaume de Jumièges records the betrothal of Em
2 CONC ma daughter of Hugues to Richard, arranged at the same time her father appointed her future husband as guardian of her brother Hugues, the future Hugues "Capet" King of France, and in a later passage records their marriage at Roue
2 CONC n after the death of her father[90].  No direct proof has yet been identified that Emma was the daughter of her father's third marriage.  However, this is likely given that betrothals at the time normally took place when the femal
2 CONC e partner was in early adolescence.  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges records the death of Emma without children[91]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([before 989]) GUNNORA, daughter of --- ([950]-5 Jan 1031).  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges records the marriage of Duke Richard and "Gunnor, issue d'une très-noble famille danoise" soon after the death of his first wife[92].  According to Robert de Torigny, the marriage took place to legitimise Ri
2 CONC chard and Gunnora's son Robert to permit his appointment as Bishop of Rouen[93].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Gunnor" as the wife of "dux Normannie primus Richardus"[94].  It appears from Dudo de Saint-Quent
2 CONC in that Gunnora was Richard I's mistress before she married him.  
2 CONT *"Duke Richard [II]" donated property to the abbey of Mont Saint-Michel by charter dated to [1024/26], subscribed by "…Gonnor matris comitis…"[95].  
2 CONT *Robert of Torigny records the death in 1030 of "Gunnor comitissa uxor primi Ricardi"[96].  The necrology of Saint-Père-en-Vallée records the death "Non Jan" of "Gonnoridis…comitissa Normannie"[97]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard & his second wife had eight children (legitimated [before 989] by the subsequent marriage of their parents): 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  RICHARD (-23 Aug 1026, bur Fécamp).  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges names (in order) "Richard, Robert, Mauger" as three of the five sons of Duke Richard and Gunnora[98].  
2 CONT *He succeeded his father in 996 as RICHARD II "le Bon/l'Irascible" Comte de Normandie.  Duke of Normandy [1015].   
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. ROBERT (-1037).  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges names (in order) "Richard, Robert, Mauger" as three of the five sons of Duke Richard and Gunnora, recording in a later passage that Robert succeeded Hugues as Archbishop of Rouen[99].  The Chronicle of Alber
2 CONC ic de Trois-Fontaines names "Robertus archiepiscopus Rothomagensis" as brother of "dux Normannie Richardus II"[100].  He is named brother of Duke Richard II by Orderic Vitalis[101].  
2 CONT *Comte d'Evreux.  
2 CONT *Bishop of Rouen 989, after his parents married to legitimise him in order to regularise his appointment[102].  An agreement between the abbots of Jumièges and Bougeuil concerning an exchange of land in Poitou, by charter dated [1
2 CONC 3 Apr/4 Apr] 1012, is subscribed by "Richardus…filius Ricardi principi magni…Robertus archiepiscopus…ecclesie Rotomagensis et Vuillelmus et Malgerus fratres Richardi comitis…"[103].  
2 CONT *He quarrelled with his nephew Robert II Duke of Normandy and took refuge in France.  
2 CONT *Robert of Torigny records the death in 1037 of "Robertus…archiepiscopus Rothomagensis"[104]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. ROBERT ["Danus"] (-12 Aug [985/89]).  
2 CONT *Robert of Torigny names "Ricardum…qui ei successit et Robertum postea archiepiscopum Rothomagensium et Malgerium comitem Curbuliensem, aliosque duos" as the sons of "Ricardi primi ducis Normanniæ" and Gunnora[105].  Houts names o
2 CONC ne of the unnamed sons Robert "Danus" but does not give her source[106].  
2 CONT *The necrology of Saint-Père-en-Vallée records the death "II Id Aug" of "Robertus puer filius comitis Richardi"[107]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  MAUGER (-[1033/40]).  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges names (in order) "Richard, Robert, Mauger" as three of the five sons of Duke Richard and Gunnora[108].  Robert of Torigny names "Ricardum…qui ei successit et Robertum postea archiepiscopum Rothomagensium et 
2 CONC Malgerium comitem Curbuliensem, aliosque duos" as the sons of "Ricardi primi ducis Normanniæ" and Gunnora[109].  An agreement between the abbots of Jumièges and Bougeuil concerning an exchange of land in Poitou, by charter dated [
2 CONC 13 Apr/4 Apr] 1012, is subscribed by "Richardus…filius Ricardi principi magni…Robertus archiepiscopus…ecclesie Rotomagensis et Vuillelmus et Malgerus fratres Richardi comitis…"[110].  
2 CONT *Comte de Corbeil, by right of his wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  son .  
2 CONT *Robert of Torigny names "Ricardum…qui ei successit et Robertum postea archiepiscopum Rothomagensium et Malgerium comitem Curbuliensem, aliosque duos" as the sons of "Ricardi primi ducis Normanniæ" and Gunnora[111].  No reference 
2 CONC has been found to the name of this son. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  EMMA ([985]-Winchester 14 Mar 1052, bur Winchester Cathedral).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges names Emma as one of the three daughters of Duke Richard and Gunnora[112].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Emma Anglorum regina" as sister of "dux Normannie Richardus II"[113].  Emma was d
2 CONC escribed by Henry of Huntingdon as "Emma Normanorum gemma"[114], although it is not known whether this was a particular indication of her beauty or mere hyperbole.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She was known as ÆLFGIFU in England[115].  Her first husband sent her to her brother's court in Normandy in 1013 after the invasion of Svend King of Denmark[116].  She was living in Normandy in 1017 when King Æthelred's successor 
2 CONC King Canute proposed marriage to her.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger of Wendover records the marriage in Jul 1018 of "Cnuto" and "ducem Ricardum…Emmam sororem suam et regis Ethelredi relictam"[117].  
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her second husband, she continued to live at Winchester.  After the election of her step-son as regent in early 1036, it was recognised that she would continue to live there to look after the interests of her so
2 CONC n Harthacnut who had nominally succeeded his father as King of England and Denmark but was still absent in Denmark.  It is likely that she encouraged her sons by her first husband, Edward and Alfred, to join her, Alfred being capt
2 CONC ured and murdered during the visit.  
2 CONT
2 CONT After Harold was recognised as king of England in 1037, Queen Emma was expelled and took refuge at Bruges[118].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She commissioned the Encomium Emmæ Reginæ from a Flemish convent at Saint-Omer, maybe St Bertin's, designed to promote her son Harthacnut's claim to the English throne.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Harthacnut joined her in Bruges in early 1040, and after the death of King Harold, they returned together to England.  After the accession of Edward "the Confessor", her son by her first husband, Emma appears to have supported the
2 CONC  rival claim of Magnus King of Norway[119].  Whatever the truth of this, King Edward did confiscate her property in 1043 according to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle[120].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She seems to have spent the last years of her life in retirement in Winchester[121].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records the death of "Ælfgifu Emma, the mother of king Edward and of king Harthacnut" in 1052[122].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (betrothed 1000, 1002[123]) as his [second/third] wife, ÆTHELRED II King of England, son of EDGAR "the Peacable" King of England & his second wife Ælfthryth ([966]-London 23 Apr 1016, bur Old St Paul's Cathedral).  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (2 or 31 Jul 1017) CANUTE King of England, son of SVEND I "Tveskæg/Forkbeard" King of Denmark & his first wife Šwiętosława [Gunhild] of Poland ([995]-Shaftesbury, Dorset 12 Nov 1035, bur Winchester Cathedral).  King of 
2 CONC Denmark 1018, King of Norway 1028. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. HAVISE (-21 Feb 1034).  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges names Hadvise, wife of "Geoffroi comte des Bretons", as the second of the three daughters of Duke Richard and Gunnora, and in a later passage records her marriage after the death of her father[124].  A chart
2 CONC er dated 1008 records that, after the death of "Gaufrido comite Britanniæ", "filii eius Alanus et Eudo cum matre eorum Hadeguisia" restored the abbey of Saint-Méen[125].  "…Aduise matre eorum comitum…" signed the charter dated to 
2 CONC [1013/22] under which "Alanus et Egio Britannorum monarchi" founded the priory of Livré "in pago Redonensi"[126].  The Chronico Kemperlegiensi records the death "1034 IX Kal Mar" of "Haduisa comitissa Britanniæ, vidua Gauffridi"[1
2 CONC 27].  
2 CONT *m (996) GEOFFROY I Duke of Brittany, son of CONAN I "le Tort" Duke of Brittany & his wife Ermengarde d'Anjou ([980]-20 Nov 1008). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. MATHILDE (-[1005]).  
2 CONT *Guillaume de Jumièges names Mathilde wife of "le comte Odon" as the third of the three daughters of Duke Richard and Gunnora, specifying in a later passage that her husband was "Eudes comte de Chartres" when recording their marri
2 CONC age after the death of her father, her dowry being half the castle of Dreux given to her by her brother Duke Richard II, and her death without children "quelques années après"[128].  
2 CONT *m ([1003/04]) as his first wife, EUDES II Comte de Blois, son of EUDES I Comte de Blois & his wife Berthe de Bourgogne [Welf] ([982/83]-15 Nov 1037). 
2 CONT
