2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Emma of Normandy:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emma_of_Normandy
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma (c. 985 – March 6, 1052 in Winchester, Hampshire), was daughter of Richard the Fearless, Duke of Normandy, by his second wife Gunnora. She was Queen consort of the Kingdom of England twice, by successive marriages: initially 
2 CONC as the second wife to Ethelred the Unready of England (1002-1016); and then to Canute the Great of Denmark (1017-1035). Two of her sons, one by each husband, and two stepsons, also by each husband, became kings of England, as was 
2 CONC her great-nephew, William the Conqueror, Duke of Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the Danish invasion of England in 1013, Emma's sons by Ethelred - Edward the Confessor and Alfred Atheling - went to Normandy as exiles, where they were to remain. Canute, the King of England, after the deaths of Ethelred and
2 CONC  his son, and Emma's stepson, Edmund II Ironside, married her himself. He was to pledge that Harthacanute, Emma's son by him, should be the heir to his Danish sovereignty, which meant that, through this marriage, the Normans were 
2 CONC kept content and deterred from intervening.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ethelred's marriage to Emma was an English strategy to avert the aggression of dangerous Normandy, and the Danish strategy was much the same. With a Normandy in feudal subordination to the kings of France, who kept it as their duk
2 CONC edom, England was the Norman dukes' main target, after baronic feuds and rampaging pillages through Brittany had run their course. English kings could not afford to underestimate the Norman threat. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Harthacanute was certainly intended to rule as the Danish ruler of England, along with most of Scandinavia, which, if he had succeeded, might have made for a very different history. It is thought though, due not least to the extol
2 CONC ling of her encomium, that in addition to political machinations, Canute was fond of Emma. In this, an affectionate marriage and the ability to keep the threat from over the channel at bay, was seen as a happy coincidence. Unfortu
2 CONC nately, events did not go as well as they might.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Canute's death, Edward and Alfred returned to England out of exile in 1036, in an expedition to see their mother, and under their half-brother Harthacanute's protection. This was seen as a move against Harold Harefoot, Canut
2 CONC e's son by Aelfgifu of Northampton, who now put himself forward as Harold I with the support of many of the English nobility. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In contempt of Harthacanute, and at war with his enemies in Scandinavia, the younger Alfred was captured, blinded, and shortly after died from his wounds. The elder, Edward, escaped to Normandy. Emma herself was soon to leave for 
2 CONC Bruges and the court of the Count of Flanders. It was at this court that the Encomium Emmae was written.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twice the Queen of the English kingdom, Emma of Normandy sits here in receipt of the Encomium Emmae, with her sons Harthacanute and Edward the Confessor in the frame.The death of Harold I in 1040 and the accession of the more conc
2 CONC iliatory Harthacanute, who had lost his Norwegian and Swedish lands, although he had made his Danish realm secure, meant Edward was officially made welcome in England the next year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Harthacanute told the Norman court that Edward should be made king if he himself had no sons. Edward was subsequently King of England on the death of Harthacanute, who, like Harold I, met his end in the throes of a fit. Emma was a
2 CONC lso to return to England, yet was cast aside, as she supported Magnus the Noble, not Edward, her son - she is not thought to have had any love for her children from her first marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma of Normandy might well have seen herself as coming second to the first wife, in both of her marriages. In England, with respect to Ethelred's first wife Aelfgifu, who possibly died in childbirth or from complications during l
2 CONC abour[1], she was known as Aelfgifu[1], a mere replacement. With her marriage to Canute, set in the shade of his 'handfast' wife, Aelfgifu of Northampton, she, at the time was known as Aelfgifu of Normandy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Each of her marriages, then, in some way left her as a second Aelfgifu, which she was clearly inclined to abandon, preferring her other name, Emma. Despite her being a second wife, her noble marriages created a strong connection b
2 CONC etween England and Normandy, which was to find its culmination under her great-nephew William the Conqueror in 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma's issue with Ethelred the Unready were:
2 CONT *1. Goda 
2 CONT *2. Edward the Confessor 
2 CONT *3. Alfred Aetheling 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her issue with Canute the Great were
2 CONT *1. Harthacanute 
2 CONT *2. Gunhilda of Denmark 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma is said to have been a great-granddaughter of Cnut's grandfather Harald Bluetooth, but this was probably a fiction intended to give her a royal bloodline.
2 CONT
2 CONT In popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma was played by Elizabeth Hubbard in the 1970 television movie The Ceremony of Innocence.
2 CONT
