1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =''''''Bjørn Óláfsson, called Styrbjørn the Strong=
2 CONT In his early twelfth-century chronicle, under the year 1049, John of Worcester records the ancestry of Earl Beorn Estrithson as:
2 CONT Ulfi, filii Spraclingi, filii Ursi
2 CONT “Ulf, son of Spracling, son of Ursus.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Ursi is the genitive of Latin Ursus, “bear,” corresponding to the Scandinavian personal name Bjørn/Björn. John therefore records:
2 CONT Bjørn/Ursus → Thorgils Sprakalägg → Ulf jarl.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Saxo Grammaticus preserves the same Swedish-associated family tradition. He relates that a Swedish noblewoman bore a son who was named after his bear father and who subsequently fathered Thrugillus, cognomine Sprageleg. The mediev
2 CONC al Compendium Saxonis states the name explicitly:
2 CONT qui nomen a patre recipiens Byorn est dictus. Hic genuit Thorkillum Spracaleg, qui genuit dictum Vlfonem.
2 CONT “Receiving his name from his father, he was called Bjørn. He begot Thorkil Sprakalägg, who begot the aforementioned Ulf.”
2 CONT The bear story is therefore a legendary explanation of the human personal name Bjørn, not evidence that Ursus himself was an animal.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The Old Norse royal genealogies identify this Bjørn. Heimskringla states that King Bjørn of Sweden was father of Eiríkr the Victorious and Óláfr, father of Styrbjørn.
2 CONT Styrbjarnar þáttr Svíakappa supplies the decisive additional information:
2 CONT Björn hét son Ólafs ok Ingibjargar.
2 CONT “Bjørn was the name of the son of Óláfr and Ingibjørg.”
2 CONT The tale consistently calls him Bjørn until King Eiríkr gives him a byname:
2 CONT Eirekr konungr gaf honum viðrnefni … ok kallaði hann Styrbjörn.
2 CONT “King Eiríkr gave him a byname … and called him Styrbjørn.”
2 CONT Thus Bjørn Óláfsson and Styrbjørn the Strong were explicitly the same person. He was the son of Óláfr Bjørnsson and Ingibjørg Þrándardóttir, became leader at Jómsborg and married Þyri Haraldsdóttir, daughter of Harald Bluetooth.
2 CONT Taken together, the medieval traditions identify the Swedish Bjørn recorded as father of Thorgils Sprakalägg with Bjørn Óláfsson, later called Styrbjørn the Strong:
2 CONT Bjørn the Old, king of Sweden
2 CONT → Óláfr Bjørnsson and Ingibjørg Þrándardóttir
2 CONT → Bjørn Óláfsson, called Styrbjørn the Strong
2 CONT → Thorgils Sprakalägg
2 CONT → Ulf jarl
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuscript evidence for the verses:
2 CONT The verses are preserved in the original manuscript witness Flateyjarbók, GKS 1005 fol., in Styrbjarnar þáttr Svíakappa, fols. 87r–87v, corresponding to the manuscript’s older column numbering 342–344.
2 CONT The first stanza occurs at f. 87rb. The tale introduces it as a verse spoken by the Danes, and the modern critical edition classifies it as anonymous:
2 CONT Eigi vildu Jótar
2 CONT reiða gjald til skeiða,
2 CONT áðr Styrbjarnar stœði
2 CONT strandar dýr á landi;
2 CONT nú er Danmarkar dróttinn
2 CONT í drengja lið genginn;
2 CONT landa vanr ok lýða
2 CONT lifir ánauðr hann auðar.
2 CONT — Anonymous, preserved at Flateyjarbók f. 87rb.
2 CONT After Styrbjørn’s defeat at Fýrisvellir, the manuscript explicitly states:
2 CONT Þorvaldr Hjaltason orti vísur þessar.
2 CONT “Þorvaldr Hjaltason composed these verses.”
2 CONT The following two stanzas occur at f. 87va/9 and f. 87va/12:
2 CONT Farið til Fýrisvallar,
2 CONT folka tungls, hverr’s hungrar,
2 CONT vörðr, at virkis garði
2 CONT vestr kveldriðu hesta;
2 CONT þar hefr hreggdrauga höggvit,
2 CONT hóllaust es þat, sólar
2 CONT elfar skíðs fyr ulfa
2 CONT Eiríkr í dyn geira.
2 CONT Ill varð ölna fjalla
2 CONT auðkveðjendum beðjar
2 CONT til Svíþjóðar síðan
2 CONT sveim víkinga heiman;
2 CONT þat eitt lifir þeira,
2 CONT þeir höfðu lið fleira,
2 CONT gótt vas hers at henda,
2 CONT hundmargs, es rann undan.
2 CONT — Þorvaldr Hjaltason, preserved at Flateyjarbók f. 87va/9 and f. 87va/12.
2 CONT The first stanza is therefore anonymous. The final two are explicitly attributed in the manuscript tradition to Þorvaldr Hjaltason.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Corrections to the previous Geni text:
2 CONT * John of Worcester was an early 12th-century chronicler, although the genealogy appears under the year 1049.
2 CONT * John writes Ursi, the genitive of Ursus (“bear”), corresponding to the Scandinavian name Bjørn/Björn. “Ursius” and “urso” are incorrect.
2 CONT * The bear legend concerns Thorgils’s father Bjørn/Ursus, not Thorgils himself.
2 CONT * The Gesta antecessorum comitis Waldevi does not make Ursus an actual bear. It says that the human Ursus was born from a white bear and a noblewoman.
2 CONT * “Wild Björn” and “Jomsviking brigand leader” are unsupported or unnecessarily loaded descriptions.
2 CONT * Heimskringla identifies Styrbjørn as the son of Óláfr Bjørnsson.
2 CONT * Styrbjarnar þáttr explicitly states that Óláfr’s son was originally named Bjørn and was later called Styrbjørn.
2 CONT *The medieval sources together identify Bjørn/Styrbjørn as the same Bjørn recorded as the father of Thorgils Sprakalägg.
2 CONT *The first quoted stanza is anonymous; the final two are attributed to Þorvaldr Hjaltason.
