2 CONC P
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} We have no dates for Thyra. She was the wife of Gorm the Old, who was born about 890/900 and died about 958/61. She certainly died before he did. He erected a rune stone in her honor. She is called Thyra Danebod (o
2 CONC r Dannebod), 'Denmark's ornament'. This nickname is derived from a phrase on the Jelling stones, but it is not clear whether the phrase applies to Thyra or whether this is a correct translation.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Dispute About Her Parents ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Thyra’s ancestry is uncertain. The Jelling stones, the only contemporary source, do not name her parents. They say:
2 CONT
2 CONT * “King Haraldr ordered this monument made in memory of Gormr, his father, and in memory of Thyrvé, his mother; that Haraldr who won for himself all of Denmark and Norway and made the Danes Christian.”
2 CONT
2 CONT * “King Gormr made this monument in memory of Thyrvé, his wife, Denmark's adornment.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Later sources given conflicting information about her parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Snorri Sturluson (Heimskringla, 13th century) and Jómsvíkinga saga (also 13th century) say she was a daughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/Harald-Klak-II-Halfdansson/6000000004586565238 Harald Klak], who died about 846/52, but
2 CONC  that’s not possible.  Thyra’s husband [http://www.geni.com/people/Gorm-Hardeknudsen/5473900890130120000 Gorm the Old] was born about 890/900. She cannot have been the daughter of a man who died in 852. She would have been 50 year
2 CONC s older than her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Saxo Grammaticus (early 13th century) says she was a daughter of Aethelred, King of England, and a sister of Aethelstan. It is not clear which Aethelred this might have been. [http://www.geni.com/people/%C3%86%C3%BEelr%C3%A6d-I-
2 CONC the-Pious/6000000005517038003 Aethelred I]  (c837-871) is too early. Thyra would have been 20 years older than her husband. [http://www.geni.com/people/%C3%86thelred-the-Unready-King-of-the-English/6000000000769899901 Aethelred II
2 CONC ] (c968-1016) is too late. He was born after Thyra died. Saxo’s story and chronology fit for Thyra to have been a sister of [http://www.geni.com/people/%C3%86thelstan/6000000002197505728 Aethelstan] (c893-893), but he was son of [
2 CONC http://www.geni.com/people/Edward-I-The-Elder-King-of-the-Anglo-Saxons/6000000002363192047 Edward the Elder] (869-924) not Aethelstan.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are a number of theories that attempt to reconcile the problems. These include the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT * She was a descendant of Harald Klak, not his daughter. In this case, her parents are unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT * She was the daughter of a different Harald, perhaps a daughter of Harald Parcus, which would mean that she was a maternal granddaughter of Aethelred I.  In this case, Snorri and Saxo were both wrong. Snorri identified the wrong 
2 CONC Harald, and Saxo was wrong about her relationship to Aethelred.  
2 CONT
2 CONT * She was the daughter of a different Aethelred, perhaps a daughter of Aethelred (d. 912) who was Ealdorman of Mercia. He seems to have had an ambiguous status that would have made it possible for Saxo to call him a king. In this 
2 CONC case, Thyra’s mother could have been Æthelflæda, so Thyra would have been a great niece of Aethelred I and a cousin of Aethelstan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * She was the sister of Aethelstan, so she was the daughter of Edward not of Aethelred. In this case, she fits very well into the politics of both England and Denmark. If she was Christian, as the story says, it is more likely tha
2 CONC t she was English than Danish. Also, Edward married his daughters into many foreign families. However, the English royal family at this time almost exclusively used names that began with the letters Ae, A, and E. All of Edward’s o
2 CONC ther known children have names that follow this pattern. If Thyra was a daughter of Edward, her name was probably not Thyra originally. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Jomsvikings saga, http://vsnrweb-publications.org.uk/Saga%20of%20the%20Jomsvikings.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT == Ifølge Saxo Grammatico Forlaget Danmark, 1929 ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Da Gorm den gamle fik raad fra landets ældste, bejlede han til Thyra, en datter af den angliske konge Hedeland, men hun som var en sindigt og højhjertet pige , svarede: Hun ville gifte sig med Gorm, hvis ikke hun kunne få hele Dan
2 CONC mark til morgengave. Først da Gorm havde lovet hende det, sagde hun ja.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se: http://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thyra_Dannebod
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thyra    Thyra ]  (correctly spelled Þyrvé) was the consort of King Gorm the Old of Denmark. She is believed to have led an army against the Germans. Gorm and Thyra were the parents of King Harald Blue
2 CONC tooth.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to popular tradition, her daughter was captured by trolls and carried off to a kingdom in the far north beyond Halogaland and Biarmaland.  Tradition also has it that before Thyra consented to marry Gorm, she insisted he 
2 CONC build a new house and sleep in it for the first three nights of winter and give her an account of his dreams those nights. The dreams were told at the wedding banquet and as recorded, imitate the dreams Pharaoh had that were inter
2 CONC preted by Joseph in Genesis. Oxen came out of the sea (bountiful harvest) and birds (glory of the king to be born).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=gb&emne=nor&person=Tyre%20Danmarksbot
2 CONT
2 CONT http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thyra_Danebod
2 CONT
2 CONT http://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thyra_Dannebod
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thyra
2 CONT
2 CONT Thyra was the consort of King Gorm the Old of Denmark. She is believed to have led an army against the Germans. Gorm and Thyra were the parents of King Harald Bluetooth.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Gorm the Old had disparaging nicknames, his wife Thyra was referred to as a woman of great prudence. Saxo wrote that Thyra was mainly responsible for building the Dannevirke on the southern border, but archeology has proven 
2 CONC it much older.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm raised a memorial stone to Thyra at Jelling, which refers to her as tanmarka but, the 'Pride' or 'Ornament' of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm and Thyra were buried under one of the two great mounds at Jelling and later moved to the first Christian church there. This was confirmed when a tomb containing their remains was excavated in 1978 under the east end of the p
2 CONC resent church.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are contradictory accounts of Thyra's parentage. Saxo holds she was the daughter of Æthelred, King of England[dubious – discuss] but Snorri says her father was a king or jarl of Jutland or Holstein called Harald Klak. The la
2 CONC tter interpretation is more likely. Thyra predeceased Gorm.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Thyra levde i 911. Hennes liv og virke er i første halvdel av det 10. århundre. Mest kjent er hun for oppførelsen av "Danevirke", en 17 km lang kampestensmur som grense mellom Sønderjylland og Tyskland.
2 CONT
2 CONT De eneste samtidige kilder som beretter om dronning Thyra, er de to Jellingstenene. På den ene sier kong Harald Blåtann, at han satte stenen etter Gorm, sin far, og Thyra, sin mor. Innskriften på den andre lyder:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Gorm konge gjorde disse kumler etter Thyra sin kone Danmarkar bot."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyra_Danebod
2 CONT
2 CONT Thyra Danebod var en dansk dronning, gift med kong Gorm den gamle og mor til Harald Blåtann. Man antar at hun ledet en hær mot tyskerne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tyra har blitt referert til som en kvinne med stor klokskap. Den eneste skriftlige kilden som bekrefter henne som noe annet en sagndronning er tekstene på runesteinene på Jellinge. Tilnavnet «Danebod» er avledet av de to siste ord
2 CONC  på «Den lille Jenningsteinen»: «Danmarks bod». Det er likevel noe usikkert om teksten henviser til henne eller Gorm, og faktisk også hva ordet betyr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Da Gorm reiste steinen som minne over Tyra må man gå ut fra at hun døde før ham, altså en gang før år 958. Utifra det kan har man antatt at hun ble født helt i begynnelsen av 900-tallet, men vi vet lite om hennes opprinnelse. Det 
2 CONC er motstridende opptegnelser om hennes foreldre. Saxo Grammaticus sier at hun var datter av «Æthelred, konge av England», hvilket i så fall må ha vært Ethelred av Wessex, men denne informasjonen regnes ikke som sannsynlig eller tr
2 CONC overdig. Snorre Sturlasson, som skrev over 300 år etter at Tyra levde, mener at hennes far var en konge eller jarl av Jylland eller kanskje Holstein (som i dag er et område i Nordtyskland) og het Harald Klakk Halvdansson. Uansett 
2 CONC riktighet er Snorre langt mer troverdig enn Saxo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tradisjonen vil ha det til at hun var ansvarlig for å bygge Danevirke, en forsvarmur langs grensen helt syd i Danmark. Den lille Jenningsteinen som Gorm reiste referer til henne som «Ærerik» og «heling» av Danmark. Det kan henspil
2 CONC le på at hun ledet en hær mot tyskerne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tradisjonen vil også ha det til at før Tyra aksepterte å gifte seg med Gorm insisterte hun på at han bygget et nytt hus som hun kunne sove i for de første tre nettene av vinteren. For hver av nettene drømte hun en drøm. Drømmene b
2 CONC le fortalt på bryllupsfesten og fortolket, skjønt de var ikke originale ved at de imiterer drømmene Farao hadde som ble fortolket av Josef i Første Mosebok: oksespannet som kommer opp av havet (fruktbar høst) og fugler (ære for de
2 CONC n konge som skal bli født). Med Gorm født hun senere Harald Blåtann som etterfulgte Gorm som konge av Danmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT En annen myte som ble fortalt var at hennes datter ble fanget av troll og fraktet vekk til et rike som langt nord, hinsides Hålogaland og Bjarmeland.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Thyra
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Thyra was the consort of King Gorm the Old of Denmark. She is believed to have led an army against the Germans. Gorm and Thyra were the parents of King Harald Bluetooth.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Gorm the Old had disparaging nicknames, his wife Thyra was referred to as a woman of great prudence. Saxo wrote that Thyra was mainly responsible for building the Dannevirke on the southern border, but archeology has proven 
2 CONC it much older.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm raised a memorial stone to Thyra at Jelling, which refers to her as tanmarka but, the 'Pride' or 'Ornament' of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gorm and Thyra were buried under one of the two great mounds at Jelling and later moved to the first Christian church there. This was confirmed when a tomb containing their remains was excavated in 1978 under the east end of the p
2 CONC resent church.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are contradictory accounts of Thyra's parentage. Saxo holds she was the daughter of Æthelred, King of England[dubious – discuss] but Snorri says her father was a king or jarl of Jutland or Holstein called Harald Klak. The la
2 CONC tter interpretation is more likely. Thyra predeceased Gorm.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to popular tradition, her daughter was captured by trolls and carried off to a kingdom in the far north beyond Halogaland and Biarmaland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tradition also has it that before Thyra consented to marry Gorm, she insisted he build a new house and sleep in it for the first three nights of winter and give her an account of his dreams those nights. The dreams were told at th
2 CONC e wedding banquet and as recorded, imitate the dreams Pharaoh had that were interpreted by Joseph in Genesis. Oxen came out of the sea (bountiful harvest) and birds (glory of the king to be born).
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Saxo Grammaticus: The History of the Danes Vol II. Davidson, Hilda Ellis and Peter Fisher. (1980) D.S. Brewer: Cambridge
2 CONT
2 CONT Salmonson, Jessica Amanda.(1991) The Encyclopedia of Amazons. Paragon House. Page 251. ISBN 1-55778-420-5
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Dronning Tyrne Haraldsdatter
2 CONT
2 CONT Tyrne Haraldsdatter var datter av Kong Klakk-Harald av Danmark. Hun giftet seg med Kong Sigurd Hjort Helgeson. De hadde barna:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Guttorm Sigurdson
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Dronning Ragnhild Sigurdsdatter.
2 CONT
2 CONT I Cappelen's Norges historie nevnes Halvdan Svartes ekteskap med Ragnhild, datter til en kong Harald Gullskjegg i Sogn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre Sturlasson forteller imidlertid om to hustruer, begge med navnet Ragnhild. Snorre fører Halvdans andre hustru som datter til Sigurd Hiort, konge på Romerike, større og sterkere enn alle andre. Det sies at Sigurd, bare 12 år
2 CONC  gammel, drepte berserken Hildebrand og 11 av hans menn i enekamp. Sigurd øvet mange storverk, men en dag han red på jakt etter storvilt, ble han drept av Hake berserk med 30 menn nær Hadeland. Hake førte Ragnhild til sin gård på 
2 CONC Hadeland og holdt henne der, men måtte senere gi henne til Halvdan Svarte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd skulle være sønn til Helge "den Kvasse" og Åslaug, datter til Sigurd Orm-i-øye, hvis far var Ragnar Lodbrok fra den sagnpregede Lodbrokslekten. Slekten skal ha etterfulgt Ynglingeslekten som konger av Sverige fra 655 til 78
2 CONC 5. Det er uklart hvor stor del av Sverige slekten regjerte over. Den ble etterfulgt av Munsöslekten som egentlig var en gren av Lodbrokslekten.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Fagerskinna" kaller Halvdans andre hustru Helga "den Hårprude" og anfører at hennes far var Dag "den Frode".
2 CONT
2 CONT Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Halvdan Svartes saga:"5. Sigurd Hjort het en konge på Ringerike, han var større og sterkere enn noen annen mann, vakrere var han også enn alle andre. Far hans var Helge den kvasse, og mor hans var Åslaug, da
2 CONC tter til Sigurd Orm-i-øye, Ragnar Lodbroks sønn. ... Han hadde to barn; Ragnhild het datteren, en ualminnelig gjev og vakker kvinne, hun var i 20-års alderen den gang, og broren hennes, Guttorm, var halvvoksen. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT ... Kong Halvdan så at de kom over fjorden på isen, han var så klarsynt; og da han så en vogn med tjeld, skjønte han det hadde gått som han ville med utsendingene. Da lot han sette fram bord og sendte bud rundt om i bygda og ba ma
2 CONC nge mennesker til seg, og den dag ble det stort gjestebud og i det gjestebudet fikk Halvdan Ragnhild, og siden ble hun en mektig dronning. Mor til Ragnhild var Tyrne, datter til kong Klakk-Harald i Jylland; hun var søster til Tyra
2 CONC  Danmarksbot, som var gift med Gorm den gamle som rådde for Daneveldet den gang." 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1). Snorre Sturlasson: Halvdan Svartes saga. Cappelen's Norges Historie, Bind 2. Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 473. Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 5
2 CONT
