1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oda_Dytryk%C3%B3wna Oda Dytrykówna w Wikipedii po Polsku]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oda_von_Haldensleben Oda von Haldensleben Deutsch]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oda_von_Haldensleben Oda of Haldensleben on Wikipedia in English]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Oda von Haldensleben=
2 CONT
2 CONT Oda von Haldensleben (sometimes "Ote"; before 978 - 1023) was the daughter of the Margrave of the North March, Theoderich (or Dietrich). By most accounts, she was a nun who was abducted by Polans Duke Mieszko I from a cloister at 
2 CONC Kolbe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oda and Mieszko I, ruler of the Polans, were married in 980; they and their sons appear as Ote and Dago(me) in a document known as the Dagome iudex. There is no document itself, but a reference to it in a church book some 80 years
2 CONC  later makes reference to this. It is thus assumed to be one of the earliest Polish legal documents, and is a principal source for this portion of the history of Poland under the Piast Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly before his death Mieszko I gifted their territory to Pope John XV and received it as a fief of the pope in this Dagome iudex. This document indexes the lands of (Mieszko), referred to as "Dagome" in the document, and his w
2 CONC ife "Ote" and her sons by him. Another son, Boleslaw I of Poland, is not mentioned, perhaps to insure Oda and her sons territory to them (by papal protection) because as it turned out, upon his fathers death Boleslaw did expel Oda
2 CONC  and her sons in order to claim all territory for himself.
