2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BRANDENBURG,%20PRUSSIA.htm#_Toc285461926
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Siemomysł in a document by Henry III
2 CONT In 1040, Emperor Henry III issued a document for the cathedral in Naumburg (Saale), in which there is information about granting it a benefice in the land of Weit, which was owned by a certain (Latin) Sememizl. Researchers identif
2 CONC y the latter with the Pomeranian Duke of Siemomysl mentioned in the document from 1046 [1]. Before 1040, Siemomysł lived in Germany, from where he moved to Pomerania. The date of the prince's arrival in Pomerania is unknown. It is
2 CONC  believed that it happened at a time close to the date of the Czech prince Brzetysław I's invasion of Silesia and Greater Poland [2]. There was also a speculation that he was identical to the prince defeated by the Hungarian princ
2 CONC e Bela I around 1043 [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Siemomysł in the report of the Annals of Altaj
2 CONT On June 24, 1046, Siemomysł took part in the congress in Merseburg with the German king Henry III, Czech prince Brzetysław I and Casimir I the Restorer. He was a powerful and Christian ruler, as he was treated equally with other S
2 CONC lavic rulers [3]. On June 29 that year in Meissen, disputes between the princes were softened by the German ruler [4]. Siemomysł did not submit to the sentence of Meissen, as already in 1047 the duke supported the mighty Miecław's
2 CONC  invasion of Mazovia [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Kinship with the Piasts
2 CONT Due to the fact that the name Siemomysł was bore by one of the Polan princes from the Piast dynasty, it is believed that the Pomeranian duke was his descendant Siemomysł. This theory is supplemented by the life of St. Wojciech cal
2 CONC led (Latin) Tempore illo, who passed the information that the Prague bishop in Gdańsk was to celebrate the wedding of an unnamed Pomeranian prince and the daughter of Bolesław I the Brave. The credibility of this source is, howeve
2 CONC r, questioned [5].
2 CONT
2 CONT Siemomysł as a descendant of the Piasts on the distaff side
2 CONT There are several concepts regarding genealogical connections between the Pomeranian Duke Siemomysł and the Piasts.
2 CONT
2 CONT An unknown Pomeranian prince was to marry the daughter of Bolesław I the Brave in 996 or 997 - Oswald Balzer was a supporter of this view, based on the message (Latin) Tempore illo [6]. This view has been rejected by more recent h
2 CONC istorical literature.
2 CONT An unknown Pomeranian duke married the daughter of Mieszko I - the wedding took place either in the 80s of the 10th century, or according to the account (Latin) by Tempore illo in 996 or at the beginning of 997 [7].
2 CONT An unknown Pomeranian duke married the daughter of Siemomysła, the Duke of Polans - Henryk Łowmiański was a supporter of this view [8]. One of the most prominent experts in the genealogy of the Piasts, Kazimierz Jasiński, consider
2 CONC ed views 2 and 3 to be equally probable.
2 CONT In support of the view that Siemomysł is related to the Piasts, the text of Gallus Anonymus, who calls Świętobor, the Pomeranian prince from the turn of the 11th and 12th centuries, and his son Świętopełk, relatives of Bolesław II
2 CONC I the Wrymouth [9], is quoted. However, there were hypotheses explaining the relationship between Świętobór and Świętopełk and the Piasts, excluding Siemomysł - it was supposed that the daughter of Bolesław II Szczodry, unknown to
2 CONC  sources, was married to the Pomeranian prince, father of Świętobor [10]. There was also a theory that the Pomeranian dukes got related to the Piasts through Ruthenian mediation - the Pomeranian duchess was to be the daughter of Ś
2 CONC wiatopełek I, the Kiev prince, and his wife, the daughter of Bolesław I the Brave [11], unknown to the sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historians who consider Siemomysł to be a descendant of a Polish princess mentioned in (Latin) Tempore illo, claim that he ruled in Gdańsk Pomerania [12)
2 CONT
