1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bezprym
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Bezprym (986/987-1032), the firstborn son of King of Poland, Bolesław I Chrobry or Brave and his second wife Judith, daughter of Geza Hungarian. He was deprived of the throne of Poland due to giving it to his brother Mieszko II. A
2 CONC round 1003 Boleslaus sent Bezprym to Italy, where he became a monk in one of Saint Romuald's hermitages. In 1031, in alliance with Conrad II, Holy Roman Emperor and Rus Grand Prince Yaroslav I the Wise, with Rus and German support
2 CONC  he gained the throne. Mieszko II escaped to Bohemia and Bezprym then sent the Polish regalia to emperor. His reign was short-lived, as Bezprym was murdered in 1032 and Mieszko II returned to the throne of Poland. It is speculated
2 CONC  that Bezprym might have began a movement known as pagan reaction, but only a few historians support this theory.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that while he was in Hungary he was appointed head of Veszprém and Zala county. In this case the name "Veszprém" originated from his name. Nevertheless, this hypothesis proposed by a Hungarian researcher didn't find
2 CONC  acceptance among Polish historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bezprym
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Bezprym
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign	1031 - 1032
2 CONT
2 CONT Born	ca. 986
2 CONT
2 CONT Died	1032
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor	Mieszko II Lambert
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor	Mieszko II Lambert
2 CONT
2 CONT Dynasty	Piast dynasty
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Bolesław I the Brave
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Judith of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Bezprym (b. ca. 986 - d. 1032), was a Duke of Poland during 1031-1032.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Bolesław I the Brave, King of Poland, but was deprived of the succession by his father, who around 1001 sent him to Italy, in order to became a monk at one of Saint Romuald's hermitages in Ravenna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Expelled by his half-brother Mieszko II Lambert after the death of their father, in 1031 Bezprym became ruler of large areas of Poland following simultaneous attack of the German and Kievan forces and Mieszko II's escape to Bohemi
2 CONC a. His reign was short-lived and, according to some sources, extremely cruel. He was murdered in 1032 and Mieszko II returned to the throne of Poland. It's speculated that Pagan Reaction began during his short reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.1 Origin of his name
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.2 Early Years
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.3 Duke of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.3.1 Assumption of power
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.3.2 Government
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.4 Death and Aftermaths
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 External Link
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Life
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Origin of his name
2 CONT
2 CONT In primary sources Bezprym appears as: Besprim (Chronicles of Thietmar of Merseburg) Besfrim (Annalista Saxo), Bezbriem (Chronicles of Hildesheim and Altaic Chronicles). This name wasn't used among the Polish nobility but was know
2 CONC n in the Bohemian sources, where it appears as: Bezprim, Bezprem, Bezperem. According to one of the hypotheses the name is of Slavonic origin, and was probably originally pronounced as Bezprzem or Bezprzym.[1] Due to tradition and
2 CONC  the impossibility of determining the correct version of the name, Bezprym remains the form used, although it probably requires modifications.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Older historiography frequently combined the figures of Bolesław I two sons, Otto and Bezprym, or even attributed to Bezprym the middle name of Otto. Marian Gumowski also suggested, on the basis of numismatic research that this "c
2 CONC ombined" prince could have governed Bohemia in 1003. These theories are based on the chronicle of Wipo of Burgundy, who described only one brother of Mieszko II, Otto. Modern historians assume, however, that Bezprym in fact did ex
2 CONC ist, and that the chronicler erroneously combined Otto and Bezprym into one person.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Early Years
2 CONT
2 CONT Bezprym was the only child of Bolesław I the Brave born from his second marriage with an unknown Hungarian princess[3] who, in older literature, was identified as Judith, daughter of Géza, Grand Duke of Hungary.[4] Though opinions
2 CONC  vary about the identity of Bolesław I 's second wife, there is a number of researchers who still support the hypothesis of her being the daughter of Géza.[5] Soon after his birth, the marriage of his parents ended, probably becau
2 CONC se of the deterioration in political relations between Poland and Hungary. Bezprym's mother was repudiated and sent away, although probably she remained in Poland and died soon afterwards.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after his divorce, Bolesław I remarried with Emnilda of Lusatia, who bore him five children. The eldest son of this union, the future Mieszko II Lambert, born in 990.
2 CONT
2 CONT About Bezprym's first years of life, almost nothing is known, in contrast with his half-brother Mieszko II, whose youth was fully described in several contemporary sources. This probably showed that his father dislike him and cons
2 CONC idered Mieszko II as his successor since his birth, which was already confirmed by Bolesław I's later political activity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bezprym was then destined to the Church career, a fact who is demostrated in the Vita of St. Saint Romuald, an hermit from Ravenna. There is stated that in one of the hermitages resided a son of a Polish Duke, who in 1001 gave him
2 CONC  a horse. According to modern historians, this Polish prince only could be Bezprym.[7] However, in earlier historiography, was theorised that the Polish prince who live in the hermitage of Ravenna was Lambert, son of Mieszko I[8] 
2 CONC or an unknown son of Bolesław I from his first marriage with the daughter of Rikdag, Margrave of Meissen.[9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT It's possible that he was in Hungary and there he was appointed head of Veszprém and Zala county. In this case the name "Veszprém" originated from his name.[11] Nevertheless, this hypothesis proposed by a Hungarian researcher didn
2 CONC 't find acceptance among Polish historians. Is also probable that Bezprym was present in the coronation of his father as King of Poland in Gniezno Cathedral on 25 April 1025.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Duke of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Assumption of power
2 CONT
2 CONT Probably after Mieszko II took control over the government of Poland, both Bezprym and his youngest half-brother Otto for a short time resided in Poland. However, soon Mieszko II expelled Bezprym from the country,[13] and probably
2 CONC  he did the same with Otto in 1030, when he discovered that they conspired against him with the help of Emperor Conrad II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bezprym took refuge in Kievan Rus and probably used the weaked position of Mieszko II as a excuse to gain the alliance of the Rurikids ruler Yaroslav I the Wise and Mstislav.[14] In 1031, when Mieszko II was defended the western b
2 CONC order from the German expedition of Conrad II, Bezprym and the Kievan forces entered in Poland. Mieszko II at that time was busy defending Lusatia and consequently was unable to repel the Kievan attack. He then was forced to escap
2 CONC e to Bohemia, were was imprisoned and castrated by orders of Duke Oldrich. Yaroslav I the Wise annexed Red Ruthenia to his domains, and Bezprym ascended to the Polish throne. Yaroslav I's troops intervene directly in the central p
2 CONC rovinces of the country, aimed at embedding the new Duke on the throne, but this fact is now considered doubtful. It's possible that the new rule of Bezprym was attractive to the population. Some scholars assume that he could even
2 CONC  stand at the head of the so-called Pagan Reaction.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Government
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after taking power, Bezprym sent to the Emperor the Royal crown and regalia. Thus, he resigned from the royal title and accepted the primacy of his western neighbor. The Royal crown and regalia was personally delivered by 
2 CONC Mieszko II's wife, Queen Richeza. In 1031, together with her children Casimir, Ryksa and Gertruda, she left the country. At the court of Emperor Conrad II, the deposed Queen was received with all the honors, and also was allowed t
2 CONC o continue to use the royal title. The departure of Richeza, and especially of her son, was extremely beneficial for Bezprym, because (at least temporarily) eliminated a possible pretender to the throne. Mieszko II wasn't consider
2 CONC ed too dangerous at that time, since he was imprisoned and castrated in Bohemia by orders of Duke Oldrich.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, probably remained a large group of supporters of the former ruler. It's believed that Bezprym began his bloody persecution against them shortly after he began his government. Many representatives of the Polish social elit
2 CONC e were forced to flee as a result. According to sources, some of them took refuge in Masovia. Perhaps among the victims of the repression, there were two Bishops, whose date of death recorded in 1032 in the Chronicles of the Chapt
2 CONC er of Kraków: Roman and Lambert. The brutal fight with the opposition could lead to the above-mentioned Pagan Reaction. Probably was instigated the discontent against the power of the Church and with the state apparatus. Contempor
2 CONC ary historiography places the riots in 1031-1032, during the reign of Bezprym. The reaction wasn't only a religious background, but social. Mainly was a reflection of the economic state caused by the aggressive policy of Bolesław 
2 CONC I the Brave and less successful rule of Mieszko II. The defeat in the battle in the west during that period, cut off the basic source of livelihood of the Polish troops, who were forced to loot the western lands. As a result, the 
2 CONC cost of the existence of an extensive army, probably was too much by the population. In addition, the devastating incursions of foreign troops was another cause of dissatisfaction among the citizenship.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is noteworthy that in the older historiography can be found the today generally rejected theory of the existence of an older son of Mieszko II, called Bolesław the Forgotten (Polish: Bolesław Zapomniany) —who apparently succeed
2 CONC ed his father in 1034 until his death in 1038—, and, according to some historians[17] was the real instigator of the Pagan Reaction, who in consequence took around 1034.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death and Aftermaths
2 CONT
2 CONT The rule of Bezprym didn't last long, and the reason for his downfall was his extremely cruelty. According to the Chronicles of Hildesheim, he was murdered by his own men no later than spring of 1032. Probably the instigators of h
2 CONC is death were his half-brothers, although the main conspirator was Otto, who remained free in Germany. The place of his burial is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Polish state as a result of Bezprym's rule was been substantially weakened. After his death the country was split into three parts: between Mieszko II, Otto, and their cousin Dytryk. This significantly increased the impact of 
2 CONC the Holy Roman Empire on Polish affairs. Poland also lost for nearly a half century its status of "kingdom".
2 CONT
