2 CONT
2 CONT Edith Swannesha (Old English: Ealdgȳð Swann hnesce, "Edith [the] Gentle Swan"; c.1025 – c. 1086), also known as Edith Swanneschals or Edith the Fair,[1] is best known as the unwedded consort of King Harold II of England. Her commo
2 CONC n name comes from a historical misinterpretation that her nickname represented Old English swann hnecca, "swan neck"[citation needed]. She is sometimes confused with Ældgyth, daughter of Ealdorman Ælfgar of Mercia, and Harold's wi
2 CONC fe.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edith_Swanneck
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#_Toc214769436 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress of Harold (1): EADGYTH "Swanneshals [Swan-neck]", [daughter of --- & his wife Wulfgyth] (-after 1066).  
2 CONT
2 CONT A mid-12th century manuscript concerning the foundation of Waltham abbey names "Editham cognomento Swanneshals" as "cubicularia" of King Harold when recording that she recovered the king´s body for burial after the battle of Hasti
2 CONC ngs[2052].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The later Vita Haroldi records that "a certain woman of a shrewd intelligence, Edith by name" recovered the king´s body from the battlefield, chosen to do so "because she loved him exceedingly…[and] had been frequently present in 
2 CONC the secret places of his chamber"[2053].  The only source so far identified which refers to an earlier document which names Eadgyth is the history of the abbey of St Benet, Holme, written by John of Oxnead in 1292, which records d
2 CONC onations to the abbey, confirmed by King Edward in 1046, including the donation by "Edgyue Swanneshals" of "Thurgertone" (Thurgarton, Norfolk)[2054].  The fact of this donation is confirmed by the corresponding charter of King Edw
2 CONC ard, reproduced in Dugdale´s Monasticon[2055], which refers to the donation of "ecclesiam de Thurgartun cum tota villa" but omits the name of the donor.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Barlow suggests that Eadgyth may have been "Ealdgyth" named in the will of her mother "Wulfgyth", dated to [1042/53], who bequeathed land "at Stisted, Essex to her sons Ælfketel and Ketel…at Saxlingham, Norfolk and Somerton, Suffo
2 CONC lk to her daughters Gode and Bote, at Chadacre, Suffolk and Ashford to her daughter Ealdgyth, and at Fritton to Earl Godwin and Earl Harold"[2056].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The connection between Wulfgyth´s family and St Benet´s, Hulme is confirmed by the testament of "Ketel" (named in his mother´s will quoted above), dated to [1052/66], which includes bequests of land to the abbey[2057].  However, K
2 CONC etel´s testament names his two sisters Gode and Bote, who are also named in their mother´s will, but does not name "Ealdgyth", suggesting that the latter may have predeceased her brother.  None of the sources so far identified sug
2 CONC gests, even indirectly, that Eadgyth "Swanneshals" was the mother of the seven illegitimate children of King Harold who are shown below, but this has been assumed to be the case in secondary sources.  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harold & [Mistress (1)] had [seven] illegitimate children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.          GODWIN ([1045/55]-after 1069).  Florence of Worcester records that "Haroldi regis filii Godwinus, Eadmundus, Magnus" returned from Ireland and landed in Somerset where they were defeated, in a passage which deals with 
2 CONC events in mid-1068[2061].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that "the sons of King Harold" (unnamed, and without specifying how many sons there were) came from Ireland and landed in "the mouth of the Taw", where they were defeate
2 CONC d by "Earl Brian", before returning to Ireland[2062].  Florence of Worcester records that "duo Haroldi filiis" sailed from Ireland 24 Jun [1069] and landed "in Ostio flumis Tavi"[2063].  He and his brothers may later have taken re
2 CONC fuge with Svend II Estrithsen King of Denmark. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.          EDMUND ([1047/55]-after 1069).  Florence of Worcester records that "Haroldi regis filii Godwinus, Eadmundus, Magnus" returned from Ireland and landed in Somerset where they were defeated, in a passage which deals with 
2 CONC events in mid-1068[2064].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that "the sons of King Harold" (unnamed, and without specifying how many sons there were) came from Ireland and landed in "the mouth of the Taw", where they were defeate
2 CONC d by "Earl Brian", before returning to Ireland[2065].  Florence of Worcester records that "duo Haroldi filiis" sailed from Ireland 24 Jun [1069] and landed "in Ostio flumis Tavi"[2066]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.          MAGNUS ([1050/55]-after [1069]).  Florence of Worcester records that "Haroldi regis filii Godwinus, Eadmundus, Magnus" returned from Ireland and landed in Somerset where they were defeated, in a passage which deals wit
2 CONC h events in mid-1068[2067].  He may also have taken part in their raids in south-west England.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.          GYTHA ([1050/55]-).  Gytha is named as King Harold's daughter in Fagrskinna, which also states that she married "Valldimar Konongr sun Iarozlæifs konongs I Holmgarde".  Morkinskinna records that the mother of “Haraldr 
2 CONC Valdimarsson”, father of Malmfrid who married Sigurd King of Norway, was “Edith the daughter of Harold Godwinson” and that her husband was “the son of King Yaroslav and Ingigerdr, the daughter of Óláfr the Swede” (although this sk
2 CONC ips a generation in the generally accepted genealogy of the Rurikids)[2068].  According to Saxo Grammaticus, after her father's death she and her two brothers "immediately emigrated to Denmark" where Svend II Estrithsen King of De
2 CONC nmark "received them in a spirit of family duty" and arranged her marriage to "Waldemarus King of the Russians"[2069].  Her estimated birth date range is based on the birth dates of her supposed children and the estimated date of 
2 CONC her husband's second marriage.  The husband of Gytha has generally been identified as Grand Prince Vladimir "Monomach"[2070], but Morkinskinna appears to be the only source which suggests that this is correct.   Baumgarten cites n
2 CONC o Russian source which corroborates the marriage[2071], although his work is particularly thorough in its source citations.  In addition, it is surprising that no single name from Gytha's family was used among the known descendant
2 CONC s of Grand Prince Vladimir.  While it is true that the Rurikid dynasty rarely imported foreign names for the male descendants, it was not unusual for females to bear names which are recognisable from the families of foreign prince
2 CONC sses who married into the family, the obvious example being the Scandinavian name Ingeborg used by Vladimir's son Mstislav for his daughter by Christina of Sweden.  It is probable that Gytha herself would not have been considered 
2 CONC a good marriage prospect at the time: her mother was obscure, she herself was illegitimate, her father had been killed ignominiously, her family lived in exile without influential connections, and her brothers had fallen into comp
2 CONC lete obscurity.  If a Russian marriage was arranged for her, it is more likely that her husband was one of the lesser princes of the dynasty.  The fact that the Scandinavian sources consistently propose a name similar to Vladimir 
2 CONC should not be viewed as conclusive, as the difficult Russian first names were frequently transcribed into contemporary western sources with more creativity than accuracy.  The inevitable, if disappointing, conclusion is that this 
2 CONC Russian marriage of Gytha's should be viewed with caution.  
2 CONT
2 CONT [m ([1070]) as his first wife, VLADIMIR Vsevolodich of Pereiaslavl and Suzdal, son of VSEVOLOD Iaroslavich Prince of Pereiaslavl and Suzdal [later VSEVOLOD I Grand Prince of Kiev] & his first wife Maria [Irina] of Byzantium (1053-
2 CONC 19 May 1125).  He succeeded 1077 as Prince of Smolensk, 1078 as Prince of Chernigov, and 1113 as VLADIMIR "Monomach" Grand Prince of Kiev.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.          [ULF (-after 1087).  His parentage is confirmed by Florence of Worcester who records that Robert III "Curthose" Duke of Normandy released "Ulfam Haroldi quondam regis Anglorum filium, Dunechaldumque regis Scottorum Mal
2 CONC colmi filium" from custody after his father's death in Sep 1087, knighted them and allowed them to leave Normandy[2072].  Freeman ascribes Ulf to Harold's legitimate marriage[2073].  If this is correct, the chronology dictates tha
2 CONC t he must have been twin with his brother Harold.  Freeman appears to base his hypothesis firstly on the assumption that Ulf was younger than his brothers, and secondly on the unlikelihood of his having been held hostage in Norman
2 CONC dy if he had been illegitimate.  However, the first point indicates nothing about the identity of his mother, and the second point does not appear to be a valid supposition considering the general acceptance of illegitimate birth 
2 CONC at the time especially if the children were born from a semi-formal "marriage" of the type practised among the Norman ducal family.  Barlow is dubious about Freeman´s hypothesis[2074].] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.          child (stillborn or died young, bur Christ Church, Canterbury[2075]).  The Memorials of St Dunstan record that "filium comitis Haraldi" had been buried in Christ Church, Canterbury, next to the tomb of St Dunstan, reco
2 CONC rding that this had caused offence[2076]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.          GUNHILD (-after 1093).  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not yet been identified.  Nun at Wilton Abbey.  She was abducted from the abbey in 1093 by Alain "Rufus/the Red" Lord of Richmond.  She lived 
2 CONC with him until he died soon afterwards, subsequently living with his brother and successor Alain "Niger/the Black"[2077].  Mistress (1) of:  ALAN "Rufus/the Red" Lord of Richmond, son of EUDES de Bretagne Comte de Penthièvre & his
2 CONC  wife Orguen [Agnes] de Cornouaïlle (-4 Aug 1089[2078], bur Bury St Edmunds).  Mistress (2) of:  ALAN "Niger/the Black" Lord of Richmond, son of EUDES de Bretagne Comte de Penthièvre & his wife Orguen [Agnes] de Cornouaïlle (-1098
2 CONC [2079]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT She bore Harold several children and was his common law wife (according to Danish law, by a civil "handfast" marriage) for over 20 years. Though she was not considered Harold's wife by the Church, there is no indication that the c
2 CONC hildren she bore by Harold were treated as illegitimate by the culture at the time. In fact, one of Harold Godwinesson and Edith Swan-Neck's daughters, Gyda Haraldsdatter, (also known as Gytha of Wessex), was addressed as "princes
2 CONC s" and was married to the Grand Duke Of Kiev, Vladimir Monomakh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though King Harold II is said to have lawfully married Edith of Mercia, the widow of the Welsh ruler Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, (whom he defeated in battle), in 1064, this is seen by most modern scholars as a marriage of political mean
2 CONC s, or even dismissed as misunderstanding or propaganda. Since at the time Mercia and Wales were allied against England, the political marriage would give the English claim in two very troublesome regions, as well as give Harold Go
2 CONC dwinsson a marriage deemed "legitimate" by the clergy of the Church, something his longtime common law wife, Edith Swan-Neck unfortunately could not provide.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith Swan-Neck would be remembered in history and folklore for one very important thing: it was she who identified Harold after his defeat at The Battle of Hastings. Harold's body was horrifically mutilated after the battle by th
2 CONC e Norman army of William the Conquerer and despite the pleas by Harold's own mother for William to surrender Harold's body for burial, the Norman army refused even though Harold's mother offered William Harold's weight in gold. It
2 CONC  was then that Edith Swan-Neck walked through the carnage of battle so that she may identify Harold by markings on his chest known only to her. It was because of Edith Swan-Neck's identification of Harold's body that Harold was gi
2 CONC ven a Christian burial by the monks at Waltham. This legend was recounted in the well-known poem by Heinrich Heine, "The Battlefield of Hastings" (1855), which features Edith Swan-neck as the main character and claims that the 'ma
2 CONC rks known only to her' were in fact love bites.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''"The Battlefield of Hastings" by Heinrich Heine, Translated by Margaret Armour''
2 CONT
2 CONT   Deeply the Abbot of Waltham sighed
2 CONT
2 CONT     When he heard the news of woe:
2 CONT
2 CONT   How King Harold had come to a pitiful end,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And on Hastings field lay low.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Asgod and Ailrik, two of his monks,
2 CONT
2 CONT     On the mission drear he sped
2 CONT
2 CONT   To search for the corse on the battle-plain
2 CONT
2 CONT     Among the bloody dead.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The monks arose and went sadly forth,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And returned as heavy-hearted.
2 CONT
2 CONT   O Father, the world's a bitter world,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And evil days have started.
2 CONT
2 CONT   For fallen, alack! is the better man;
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Bastard has won, and knaves
2 CONT
2 CONT   And scutcheoned thieves divide the land,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And make the freemen slaves.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The veriest rascals from Normandy,
2 CONT
2 CONT     In Britain are lords and sirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT   I saw a tailor from Bayeux ride
2 CONT
2 CONT     With a pair of golden spurs.
2 CONT
2 CONT   O woe to all who are Saxon born!
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ye Saxon saints, beware!
2 CONT
2 CONT   For high in heaven though ye dwell,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Shame yet may be your share.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Ah, now we know what the comet meant
2 CONT
2 CONT     That rode, blood-red and dire,
2 CONT
2 CONT   Across the midnight firmament
2 CONT
2 CONT     This year on a broom of fire.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Twas an evil star, and Hastings field
2 CONT
2 CONT     Has fulfilled the omen dread.
2 CONT
2 CONT   We went upon the battle-plain,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And sought among the dead.
2 CONT
2 CONT    While still there lingered any hope
2 CONT
2 CONT     We sought, but sought in vain;
2 CONT
2 CONT   King Harold's corse we could not find
2 CONT
2 CONT     Among the bloody slain.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Asgod and Ailrik spake and ceased.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Abbot wrung his hands.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Awhile he pondered, then he sighed,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Now mark ye my commands.
2 CONT
2 CONT   By the stone of the bard at Grendelfield,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Just midway through the wood,
2 CONT
2 CONT   One, Edith of the Swan's Neck, dwells
2 CONT
2 CONT     In a hovel poor and rude.
2 CONT
2 CONT   They named her thus, because her neck
2 CONT
2 CONT     Was once as slim and white
2 CONT
2 CONT   As any swan's--when, long ago,
2 CONT
2 CONT     She was the king's delight.
2 CONT
2 CONT   He loved and kissed, forsook, forgot,
2 CONT
2 CONT     For such is the way of men.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Time runs his course with a rapid foot;
2 CONT
2 CONT     It is sixteen years since then.
2 CONT
2 CONT   To this woman, brethren, ye shall go,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And she will follow you fain
2 CONT
2 CONT   To the battle-field; the woman's eye
2 CONT
2 CONT     Will not seek the king in vain.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Thereafter to Waltham Abbey here
2 CONT
2 CONT     His body ye shall bring,
2 CONT
2 CONT   That Christian burial he may have,
2 CONT
2 CONT     While for his soul we sing.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The messengers reached the hut in the wood
2 CONT
2 CONT     At the hour of midnight drear.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Wake, Edith of the Swan's Neck, rise
2 CONT
2 CONT     And follow without fear.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The Duke of Normandy has won
2 CONT
2 CONT     The battle, to our bane.
2 CONT
2 CONT   On the field of Hastings, where he fought,
2 CONT
2 CONT     The king is lying slain.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Arise and come with us; we seek
2 CONT
2 CONT     His body among the dead.
2 CONT
2 CONT   To Waltham Abbey it shall be borne.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Twas thus our Abbot said.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The woman arose and girded her gown,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And silently went behind
2 CONT
2 CONT   The hurrying monks. Her grizzly hair
2 CONT
2 CONT     Streamed wildly on the wind.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Barefoot through bog and bush and briar
2 CONT
2 CONT     She followed and did not stay,
2 CONT
2 CONT   Till Hastings and the cliffs of chalk
2 CONT
2 CONT     They saw at dawn of day.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The mist, that like a sheet of white
2 CONT
2 CONT     The field of battle cloaked,
2 CONT
2 CONT   Melted anon; with hideous din
2 CONT
2 CONT     The daws flew up and croaked.
2 CONT
2 CONT   In thousands on the bloody plain
2 CONT
2 CONT     Lay strewn the piteous corpses,
2 CONT
2 CONT   Wounded and torn and maimed and stripped,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Among the fallen horses.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The woman stopped not for the blood;
2 CONT
2 CONT     She waded barefoot through,
2 CONT
2 CONT   And from her fixed and staring eyes
2 CONT
2 CONT     The arrowy glances flew.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Long, with the panting monks behind,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And pausing but to scare
2 CONT
2 CONT   The greedy ravens from their food,
2 CONT
2 CONT     She searched with eager care.
2 CONT
2 CONT   She searched and toiled the livelong day,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Until the night was nigh;
2 CONT
2 CONT   Then sudden from her breast there burst
2 CONT
2 CONT     A shrill and awful cry.
2 CONT
2 CONT   For on the battle-field at last
2 CONT
2 CONT     His body she had found.
2 CONT
2 CONT   She kissed, without a tear or word,
2 CONT
2 CONT     The wan face on the ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT   She kissed his brow, she kissed his mouth,
2 CONT
2 CONT     She clasped him close, and pressed
2 CONT
2 CONT   Her poor lips to the bloody wounds
2 CONT
2 CONT     That gaped upon his breast.
2 CONT
2 CONT   His shoulder stark she kisses too,
2 CONT
2 CONT     When, searching, she discovers
2 CONT
2 CONT   Three little scars her teeth had made
2 CONT
2 CONT     When they were happy lovers.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The monks had been and gotten boughs,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And of these boughs they made
2 CONT
2 CONT   A simple bier, whereon the corse
2 CONT
2 CONT     Of the fallen king was laid.
2 CONT
2 CONT   To Waltham Abbey to his tomb
2 CONT
2 CONT     The king was thus removed;
2 CONT
2 CONT   And Edith of the Swan's Neck walked
2 CONT
2 CONT     By the body that she loved.
2 CONT
2 CONT   She chanted litanies for his soul
2 CONT
2 CONT     With a childish, weird lament
2 CONT
2 CONT   That shuddered through the night. The monks
2 CONT
2 CONT     Prayed softly as they went.
2 CONT
