2 CONT
2 CONT GYTHA ([1050/55]-).  Gytha is named as King Harold's daughter in Fagrskinna, which also states that she married "Valldimar Konongr sun Iarozlæifs konongs I Holmgarde".  Morkinskinna records that the mother of “Haraldr Valdimarsson
2 CONC ”, father of Malmfrid who married Sigurd King of Norway, was “Edith the daughter of Harold Godwinson” and that her husband was “the son of King Yaroslav and Ingigerdr, the daughter of Óláfr the Swede” (although this skips a genera
2 CONC tion in the generally accepted genealogy of the Rurikids)[2068].  According to Saxo Grammaticus, after her father's death she and her two brothers "immediately emigrated to Denmark" where Svend II Estrithsen King of Denmark "recei
2 CONC ved them in a spirit of family duty" and arranged her marriage to "Waldemarus King of the Russians"[2069].  Her estimated birth date range is based on the birth dates of her supposed children and the estimated date of her husband'
2 CONC s second marriage.  The husband of Gytha has generally been identified as Grand Prince Vladimir "Monomach"[2070], but Morkinskinna appears to be the only source which suggests that this is correct.   Baumgarten cites no Russian so
2 CONC urce which corroborates the marriage[2071], although his work is particularly thorough in its source citations.  In addition, it is surprising that no single name from Gytha's family was used among the known descendants of Grand P
2 CONC rince Vladimir.  While it is true that the Rurikid dynasty rarely imported foreign names for the male descendants, it was not unusual for females to bear names which are recognisable from the families of foreign princesses who mar
2 CONC ried into the family, the obvious example being the Scandinavian name Ingeborg used by Vladimir's son Mstislav for his daughter by Christina of Sweden.  It is probable that Gytha herself would not have been considered a good marri
2 CONC age prospect at the time: her mother was obscure, she herself was illegitimate, her father had been killed ignominiously, her family lived in exile without influential connections, and her brothers had fallen into complete obscuri
2 CONC ty.  If a Russian marriage was arranged for her, it is more likely that her husband was one of the lesser princes of the dynasty.  The fact that the Scandinavian sources consistently propose a name similar to Vladimir should not b
2 CONC e viewed as conclusive, as the difficult Russian first names were frequently transcribed into contemporary western sources with more creativity than accuracy.  The inevitable, if disappointing, conclusion is that this Russian marr
2 CONC iage of Gytha's should be viewed with caution.
2 CONT
2 CONT [m ([1070]) as his first wife, VLADIMIR Vsevolodich of Pereiaslavl and Suzdal, son of VSEVOLOD Iaroslavich Prince of Pereiaslavl and Suzdal [later VSEVOLOD I Grand Prince of Kiev] & his first wife Maria [Irina] of Byzantium (1053-
2 CONC 19 May 1125).  He succeeded 1077 as Prince of Smolensk, 1078 as Prince of Chernigov, and 1113 as VLADIMIR "Monomach" Grand Prince of Kiev.]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyda Haraldsdatter levde fra 1085 til 1132. Hun var datter av Kong Harald II Godwinson av England og Edgyth Swan-neck.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyda giftet seg med Storfyrste Vladimir II Monomakh av Novgorod omkring 1070. De hadde barna.:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Storfyrste Mstislav I av Novgorod. FÃ?dt 1076. DÃ?d 15.04.1132.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Fyrste Vesvolod II av Novgorod. FÃ?dt 1099. DÃ?d 11.02.1136.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyda benevnes "Gyda den gamle" av Snorre Sturlasson, han oppgir at hun var datter til den engelske kongen Harald Gudinesson. Hennes mor var antagelig Edgyth Swan-neck (Svanehals).
2 CONT
2 CONT Fra Snorre Storlasson: MagnussÃ?nnenes saga:"20. Kong Sigurd ble gift med Malmfrid, datter til kong Harald Valdemarsson Ã?st fra Holmgard. Mor til kong Harald var Gyda den gamle, datter til den engelske kongen Harald Gudinesson.
2 CONC  ... ". 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1). Snorre Sturlasson: MagnussÃ?nnenes saga, avsnitt 20. Mogens Bugge: VÃ?re forfedre, nr. 556. Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 89.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gytha_of_Wessex updated June 2020
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gytha of Wessex''' (born c. 1053/1061 - died 1098 or 1107;[1] Old English: Gȳð) was one of several daughters of Harold Godwinson, the last Anglo-Saxon King of England, by his consort, Edyth Swannesha. Through marriage to Vladim
2 CONC ir II Monomakh Gytha became a Grand Princess consort of Kievan Rus.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the thirteenth-century chronicler Saxo Grammaticus, after the death of their father Harold Godwinson (d. 1066), Gytha and two of her brothers (probably Magnus, son of Harold Godwinson and one of Godwin and Edmund) esc
2 CONC aped to the court of their first cousin once-removed, King Sweyn Estridsson of Denmark.[3] The two brothers were treated by Sweyn with hospitality, Magnus entering into high-level service with Bolesław II the Generous while their 
2 CONC sister was married to Waldemar, King of Ruthenia, i.e. Vladimir II Monomakh, one of the most famous rulers of Kievan Rus.[4] This took place in 1069/1070 when Bolesław restored Grand Prince Iziaslav I of Kiev and Gertruda (Bolesła
2 CONC w's aunt) to power after they had been deposed. Gytha's role in Vladimir’s rule isn't documented. Vladimir explained in a book of 'Instructions' (Pouchenie) for his sons, written in the twelfth-century: “Love your wives, but grant
2 CONC  them no power over you.”[5] In his book, Vladimir also mentioned Yuri mother's recent death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gytha was the mother of Mstislav the Great, the last ruler of united Kievan Rus. In the Norse sagas, Mstislav is called Harald, after his grandfather. The patericon of St. Pantaleon Cloister in Cologne says that "Gytha the Queen" 
2 CONC (Gida regina) died as a nun on 10 March.[6] A year later Vladimir Monomakh married another woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children===
2 CONT
2 CONT With Vladimir, Gytha had several children, including:[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mstislav the Great (1076–1132)
2 CONT * Izyaslav Vladimirovich, Prince of Kursk († 6 September 1096)
2 CONT * Svyatoslav Vladimirovich, Prince of Smolensk and Pereyaslav († 16 March 1114)
2 CONT * Yaropolk II of Kiev († 18 February 1139)
2 CONT * Viacheslav I of Kiev († 2 February 1154)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Legacy===
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her son Yuri Dolgorukiy she was an ancestor of all major Rurik rulers in Russian history, including Alexander Nevsky, Ivan I Kalita, Dmitry Donskoy, Ivan III the Great and Ivan IV the Terrible.
2 CONT Through her son Mstislav the Great she was an ancestor of both Philippa of Hainault and King Edward III of England, hence of all subsequent English and British monarchs. According to Russian politician and historian Vladimir Medin
2 CONC sky, Gytha was a significant influence on Monomakh's public relations: "Knyaz's English wife wasn't wasted". As a source, Medinsky quotes M. P. Akekseev's comparative analysis between Monomakh writings' and Alfred the Great's, and
2 CONC  other anonymous then contemporary Anglo-Saxon texts.[8]
2 CONT
