2 CONT
2 CONT Bernhard, Count of Anhalt (c. 1140 – 2 February 1212) was a member of the House of Ascania and by paternal inheritance, Count of Anhalt and Ballenstedt and Lord of Bernburg. Since 1180 he was also Duke of Saxony as Bernhard III.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernhard, Herzog von Sachsen (* 1140; † 9. Februar 1212 in Ballenstedt) war Herzog von Sachsen, Markgraf der Nordmark, Graf von Anhalt und Ballenstedt, Herr zu Bernburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernhard,_Count_of_Anhalt
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernhard, Count of Anhalt (c. 1140 – 2 February 1212) was a German prince of the House of Ascania, Count of Anhalt and Ballenstedt, and Lord of Bernburg through his paternal inheritance. From 1180 he was also Duke of Saxony as Ber
2 CONC nhard III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernhard was the youngest of the seven sons of Albert the Bear, Margrave of Brandenburg and Duke of Saxony, by his wife Sophie of Winzenburg. In 1157 he was present together with his father and brothers at the funeral of Conrad th
2 CONC e Great, a member of the House of Wettin and Margrave of Meissen. In 1159, Bernhard accompanied the emperor Frederick I Barbarossa to Italy with his brother Otto. After the death of his father in 1170, Bernhard inherited the state
2 CONC s of Anhalt and the district of Ascaria (Aschersleben) as well as the former Gau Serimunt between the Saalde, Mulde, and Elbe rivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his brother Albert in 1172 without male issue, Bernhard also inherited his County of Ballenstedt. In the same year he solicited the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa in the Reichstag of Goslar for possession of Plötz
2 CONC kau, which passed to him in 1173. A dispute over control over the County of Plötzkau led to a war with Henry the Lion that led to the destruction of Aschersleben and Gröningen and nearly resulted in the destruction of Halberstadt.
2 CONC  Bernhard nonetheless was able to confirm his possession of Plötzkau.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Fall of Henry the Lion
2 CONT
2 CONT When Henry the Lion was outlawed by the Emperor in 1180, the Reichstag also dispossessed him of his fiefs (Würzburg and the Duchies of Bavaria and Saxony). In Gelnhausen on 13 April 1180, Bernhard was granted the eastern part of t
2 CONC he Welf lands, including the Archbishopric of Bremen-Hamburg, which was passed on to his elder brother Siegfried, and the Duchy of Saxony. This latter award was without real value, however, since this duchy was a radically reduced
2 CONC  territory consisting of three unconnected parcels of land along the river Elbe: (1) Hadeln around Otterndorf, (2) a parcel around Lauenburg upon Elbe and (3) a parcel around Wittenberg upon Elbe. Previously the duchy had been div
2 CONC ided, so that Bernhard could only receive the region between Meissen and the Mark of Brandenburg as his formal possession. Besides these, he also received several small territories: the towns of Aken and Wittenberg and the Burgrav
2 CONC iate of Magdeburg. The dignity Duke of Saxony, Angria and Westphalia was only an empty title. The rich lands of Engern and Westphalia, in addition to the ducal title of Highness, were conferred on the Archbishopric-Electorate of C
2 CONC ologne. The counts of Holstein were released from their homage to the Saxon dukes, the County of Stade was taken by the Archbishop of Bremen, Lübeck became an Imperial City, the Saxon Palatinate was bestowed on Count Hermann of Th
2 CONC uringia and, in addition, the Saxon bishops took back their fiefs. Bernhard was forced to support the Emperor in his renewed war against Henry the Lion in 1181. In spite of his defeat, Henry the Lion was able to preserve his Allod
2 CONC ial title, from which the Duchy of Brunswick and Lüneburg was later formed.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Ruling as Duke of Saxony
2 CONT
2 CONT In Nordalbingien and the areas between the Elbe and the Baltic Sea, Bernhard's vassals soon rebelled against him and gave their support to Henry the Lion. Bernhard tried to assert his claims, thanks to the support of his brothers 
2 CONC Otto I of Brandenburg and Siegfried, Archbishop of Bremen. At first the vassals of Artlenburg swore an oath of fidelity. After them, the counts of Ratzeburg, Danneberg, Luckow and Schwerin also swore. However, the most powerful of
2 CONC  these vassals, Count Adolf of Holstein, would not accept the Bernhard's lordship and became his adversary. Conflicts broke out around Dithmarschen, in western Holstein, but without success for Adolf.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Adolf's defeat, Lauenburg (Polabenburg) on the lower Elbe, became the focal point for opposition to Bernhard's rule. Determined to eliminate the opposition against him in his lands, he leavied high taxes on rebellious territ
2 CONC ories, which led to an attack against Lauenburg and its destruction in 1182.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1183 another of Bernhard's brothers, Count Dietrich of Werben died without surviving male issue. His possessions fell mostly to Bernhard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Warfare involving Henry the Lion also expanded to the Slavic countries. Henry Borwin I, the son of Pribislav, was a supporter of Henry the Lion like his father, and remained an opponent of Bernhard. His cousin Nicholas I (Niklot),
2 CONC  granted Burg Malchow by Henry the Lion in 1164, lost part of it due to his association with Bernhard. Borwin allied himself with Duke Bogislaw I of Pomerania and Nicholas with Prince Jaromir I of Rügen, a faithful vassal of Denma
2 CONC rk. But shortly after Bogislaw had to punish Borwin (in response to a secret order of the emperor) with the help of King Canute VI of Denmark for his refusal to render homage, by which his lands were split between the Elbe and Or 
2 CONC to the Empire and Denmark. Borwin was taken into captivity by King Canute and was forced, like Nicholas, to accept his lands from the king as fiefs. After incursions of the Danes in Pomerania in 1184 and 1185, Bogislaw shared the 
2 CONC same destiny.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the success of the Danes, the Emperor in 1184 pressed for a political balance between Bernhard and his vassal. Count Adolf of Holstein kept the regions disputed between them, however he had to pay 700 marks to Bernhard and sw
2 CONC ear the oath of fidelity that he had earlier refused to do. Counts Bernhard of Ratzeburg and Gunzelin of Schwerin were also obliged to make payments. The destroyed Lauenburg had to be rebuilt.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, after Henry's return in 1188 it came again to discussions with Bernhard, who finally lost the town of Bardowick.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Duke of Saxony, Bernhard became at the same time Marshal of the Holy Roman empire. He asserted this important post for the first time in 1190 at the coronation of Emperor Henry VI, but spoiled his good terms with the Emperor th
2 CONC rough his opposition to turning over the hereditary German crown to the House of Hohenstaufen. In 1198 he supported Philipp of Swabia as Emperor. Philipp was killed on 21 June 1208 by Otto of Wittelsbach at Altenburg in Bamberg. T
2 CONC hereupon a new imperial election was held in Halberstadt and (with Bernhard's vote included) Otto of Brunswick was elected on 22 September and crowned immediately in Frankfurt.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his acquisition of Saxon estates, Bernhard moved his residence and court to Wittenberg. In 1260 (with effect as from 1296 on) his grandsons split the Duchy into the Duchies of Saxe-Wittenberg (German: Herzogtum Sachsen-Witten
2 CONC berg) and Saxe-Lauenburg (German: Herzogtum Sachsen-Lauenburg), the latter holding the unconnected two northern territories. Wittenberg remained a residence of the House of Ascania until the extinction of this line in 1422. On his
2 CONC  death, aged seventy-two, Bernhard was buried beside his father Albert and several of his brothers in the Church of the Benedictine monastery in Ballenstedt.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernhard first married Brigitte (or Jutta), a daughter of Canute V of Denmark; secondly Sophia, daughter of Louis II, the Iron, Landgrave of Thuringia; and thirdly Judith (b. bef. 1154 – d. aft. 12 December 1201), daughter of Mies
2 CONC zko III the Old, Duke of Greater Poland and High Duke of all Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * First marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Albert I, Duke of Saxony (b. ca. 1175 – d. 7 October / 8 November? 1260).
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Sophia (or Adelheid?), (d. 16 July 1244), Abbess of Gernrode (1221–44).
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Henry I, Count of Anhalt (b. ca. 1170 – d. 1252).
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Magnus (d. young)
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Hedwig, married ca. 1204 Count Ulrich of Wettin
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Second marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. John, Provost in Halberstadt (1256)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Da Knud IV i 1182 hadde overtatt makten i Danmark, meldte problemet i forholdet til keiseren seg igjen. Men Frederik Barbarossa var for opptatt av større oppgaver til å kunne ta seg av nordiske forhold. Han krevde derfor etter Val
2 CONC demar den Stores død at Knud skulle komme ned til ham og avlegge lenseden, slik hans far hadde gjort. Dette ble avvist. Barbarossa truet da med å ta lenet fra Knud og gi det til en annen. Knud svarte at keiseren i så fall først må
2 CONC tte finne en annen. Man mente i Danmark at keiseren ikke kunne begynne et felttog mot nord, eller at noen av hans lendmenn var oppgaven voksen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den nye sachserhertug, Bernhard av Anhalt, hadde ikke samme styrke som Henrik Løve, og kunne neppe bli en trussel mot det danske riket.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Noted events in his life were:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Acceded: Duke of Saxony, Cir 1170-1180. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hertig Bernhard III av Sachsen, född omkring 1140, död i Bernburg 9 februari 1212, begravd i Kloster Ballenstedt, greve av Aschersleben, greve av Anhalt 1170-1212, hertig av Sachsen 1180-1212. Son till hertig Albrekt Björnen (död 
2 CONC 1170) och Sophie av Winzenburg (död 1160).
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernhard erhöll vid faderns död och den påföljande delningen av det askaniska arvet 1170 grevskapet Aschersleben. 1171 ärvde han Ballenstedt efter brodern Adalbert. Bernhard deltog i rikskriget mot welfiske hertig Henrik Lejonet v
2 CONC arunder Henrik lät bränna Aschersleben. Efter att Henrik störtats 1180 förlänade kejsar Fredrik I "Barbarossa" Bernhard östra halvan av hertigdömet Sachsen (Sachsen-Wittenberg), dock ej utan motstånd från såväl andliga som världsl
2 CONC iga furstar och grevar vilka eftersträvade större autonomi. Västra halvan av hertigdömet Sachsen förlänades kyrkan i Köln. Medan Bernhard i Westfalen och Engern avstod från utövande av inflytande gjorde han anspråk på högheten öve
2 CONC r grevskapen Holstein, Ratzeburg, Schwerin, Lüchow och Dannenberg, utan att helt lyckas med sina avsikter. Welfernas växande inflytande och kejsarens inkonsekventa hållning förorsakade Bernhard svårigheter, och 1189/1190 förjagade
2 CONC s han ur Holstein och Schwerin av den återvändande Henrik Lejonet. Bernhard lyckades efter detta hävda sin hertigliga makt i Ostfalen.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1197 föreslogs Bernhard till tysk tronkandidat, detta på inrådan av ärkebiskop Adolf av Köln. Bernhard avböjde dock kandidaturen och förblev staufern Philip av Schwaben trogen i kampen mot welfiske motkungen Otto IV. Först efter P
2 CONC hilips död 1208 övergick Bernhard till Otto IV:s parti. Såsom varande Ottos anhängare kunde Bernhard hävda de gamla sachsiska länsanspråken över Pommern, Mecklenburg och Polen, dock ej utan att råka i gräl med sina kusiner i Brand
2 CONC enburg vilka förde samma anspråk.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1203 vann Bernhard Ratzeburg-Lauenburg men miste det omgående till Danmark. Han ingrep i de danska brödrakrigen och understödde ärkebiskop Valdemar av Schleswig-Bremen. Det var under ett fälttåg till Bremen till stöd för Valdemar 
2 CONC som den högt ansedde hertig Bernhard avled.
2 CONT
2 CONT De askaniska stamlanden utgjorde kärnan i hertig Bernhards besittningar. Efter Bernhards död 1212 tillföll de askaniska besittningarna mellan Harz och mellersta Elbe äldste sonen Henrik (linjen Anhalt). Yngre sonen Albrekt blev he
2 CONC rtig av Sachsen i Wittenberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hertug av Sachsen 1170 (1180?) - 1212.
2 CONT
2 CONT Da Knud IV i 1182 hadde overtatt makten i Danmark, meldte problemet i forholdet til keiseren seg igjen. Men Frederik Barbarossa var for opptatt av større oppgaver til å kunne ta seg av nordiske forhold. Han krevde derfor etter Val
2 CONC demar den Stores død at Knud skulle komme ned til ham og avlegge lenseden, slik hans far hadde gjort. Dette ble avvist. Barbarossa truet da med å ta lenet fra Knud og gi det til en annen. Knud svarte at keiseren i så fall først må
2 CONC tte finne en annen. Man mente i Danmark at keiseren ikke kunne begynne et felttog mot nord, eller at noen av hans lendmenn var oppgaven voksen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den nye sachserhertug, Bernhard av Anhalt, hadde ikke samme styrke som Henrik Løve, og kunne neppe bli en trussel mot det danske riket.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tekst: Tore Nygaard
2 CONT
