2 CONT Father	Gerhard I, Count of Holstein
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Elisabeth of Mecklenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig of Holstein [1][2] (Swedish: Helvig,[3][4] German: Helwig) (1260–1324) was a Swedish queen consort, spouse of King Magnus III of Sweden. She was the child of Gerhard I, Count of Holstein (died 1290) and Elisabeth of Mecklen
2 CONC burg (died 1280).
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig was married to Magnus Ladulås in 1276, and was granted the fief of Dåvö in Munktorp in Västmanland. Magnus succeeded in preventing a Danish-Holstein alliance by marrying her. However, he had obtained a dispensation for thei
2 CONC r marriage only after the wedding.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father was captured during the Folkunge party revolt (Folkungaupproret) by rebellious noblemen in Skara in 1278 and the queen was also targeted. The actions of the rebels were well timed; they coincided with her journey throug
2 CONC h Sweden. She sought refuge in the convent in the city.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig was crowned Queen of Sweden in the city of Söderköping on 29 June 1281; this is the first confirmed coronation of a queen consort in Sweden. It included the prayers for her fertility, which was the matter of great importanc
2 CONC e. [7] She founded the Greyfriars convent (Gråmunkekloster) in Stockholm and several other churches and convents. As a queen, however, she is not very much heard of, despite the fact that she held the position for fourteen years, 
2 CONC she lived a discreet life, both as a queen and as a dowager queen. She took a prominent part in processions which accompanied the inauguration of bishops, celebrations of a feast day and the installation of relics, such as the Mas
2 CONC s for St. Erik in 1277.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her spouse in 1290, Hedwig acted as one of the executors of the will of the King. In 1291, she withdrew to her estate Dåvö in Västmanland. She is not known to have taken any political role, formal or informal, d
2 CONC uring or after the reign of her spouse. She was described as a noble, loyal and peace-loving mother figure, tormented by the conflicts between her sons. She acted as a foster mother for her son's future bride, princess Martha of D
2 CONC enmark, who spent a lot of her childhood in Sweden as the future queen of Sweden after 1290. In 1302, she was present at the coronation of her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT As dowager queen, she governed Fjärdhundraland, which was given to her as dower (morning gift).[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Hedwig is buried in Riddarholm church in Stockholm, with her husband and her daughter Richeza.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig's wedding took place at Kalmar castle the 11 November 1276 with king Magnus III Ladulås of Sweden. The had the following children: [10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingeborg Magnusdotter of Sweden, born abt. 1279. Married King Eric VI of Denmark, Erik Menved.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birger Magnusson, born abt. 1280, king of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric Magnusson, Duke of Södermanland in 1302 and Halland etc c 1305, born abt. 1282. Died of starvation 1318 at Nyköpingshus castle while imprisoned by his brother King Birger.
2 CONT
2 CONT Valdemar Magnusson, Duke of Finland in 1302 and Öland 1310. Died of starvation 1318 at Nyköpingshus castle while imprisoned by his brother King Birger.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richeza Magnusdotter, abbess of the convent of St. Clara at Stockholm. Died after 1347.
2 CONT
