1 CONT [from Ancestry.com 139798.GED]
1 CONT Svend I (known in England as Sweyn) Forkbeard was King of England in the autumn of 1013 and King of Denmark about 985. He had two sons: Harald II Svendsen, King of Denmark on 3 February 1014; and Knud (Cnut or Canute) I, "The Grea
1 CONC t", King of England and Denmark in 1019, also King of Norway 1028, Lord of Norse Colonies, Overlord of the Scots, King of Dublin (?).
0 @N6000000000945044387@ NOTE De sikre informasjoner vi har om Gunhild er ytterst få, hennes slaviske navn er ikke 
1 CONT kjent. Adam av Bremen setter hennes ekteskap med Erik Segersäll i forbindelse med et forenet
1 CONT svensk-slavisk angrep på Danmark, men den historiske bakgrunn for dette kan ikke
1 CONT kontrolleres. Ifølge Adam fødte hun Erik sønnen Olof Skötkonung. Etter Eriks død giftet hun
1 CONT seg med Svend Tjugeskjegg, muligens da denne ca. år 1000 inngikk forbund med kong Olav.
1 CONT Hun ble senere bortvist av Svend, og levde i mange år i Vendland, inntil hennes sønner etter
1 CONT Svends død hentet henne hjem til Danmark. De islandske sagaforfatterne har knyttet
1 CONT forskjellige ikke historiskt korrekte trekk til hennes navn, og dette har senere i tradisjonen blitt
1 CONT fortrengt av den svenske dronning Sigrid Storråde.
1 CONT Gunhild var søster til Boleslav I av Polen (992 - 1025) og ikke hans datter som Snorre
1 CONT Sturlasson beretter.
1 CONT Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Olav Trygvessons saga:
1 CONT ?22. Mens Olav lå ved Bornholm, fikk han kvast vær og stormsjø, så han kunne ikke
1 CONT holde seg der; så seilte de derfra og sør under Vendland (Pommern), og der fikk de god havn.
1 CONT Der gikk alt fredelig for seg, og de ble der en stund. Kongen i Vendland het Burislav, døtrene
1 CONT hans var Geira, Gunhild og Astrid. ...?
0 @N6000000002176669820@ NOTE Widow of King Erik the Victorious of Sweden.
0 @N6000000002322328014@ NOTE Widow of King Erik the Victorious of Sweden.
0 @N6000000002244417829@ NOTE Widow of King Erik the Victorious of Sweden.
0 @N6000000006412176826@ NOTE Sigríð stórráða Tóstadóttir was born about 927 in Sweden, daughter to Tósti, a well-regarded Swedish warrior. The nickname carried  by Sigríð, stórráða, meaning "the Haughty", "the Proud", "the Ambitious", or "the Strong-Minded", 
1 CONC is not perjorative -- in Viking culture it was expected that one would be proud about one's accomplishments and belongings, and it was no shame to boast of them.  A proud carriage showed a woman who knew her own worth.  It only be
1 CONC came a vice when one was haughty but had no cause to be.  Sigríð definitely had much cause for her pride - she was the daughter of a well-known and respected war-leader, she was the wife of one king and the mother of another, and 
1 CONC her personal riches and land holdings were vast.  And certainly Sigríð was strong-minded -- she was never one to duck a hard decision, nor to avoid hard conflict when honor so demanded.
1 CONT
1 CONT Haralds saga Gráfeldar (The Saga of Harald Greycloak), chapter 11, explains that:
1 CONT
1 CONT Tósti was the name of a man in Sweden, one of the noblest and most powerful there of those who were not of princely birth.  He was a great warrior and had been for a long time on Viking expeditions.  He was called Skoglar-Tósti 1.
1 CONC   Harald Grenski (Harald the Greenlander) joined his company, following Tósti in his expeditions in summertime; and Harald was esteemed highly by everybody.  In the winter following, Harald stayed with Tósti.  Sigríð was the name 
1 CONC of Tósti's daughter, a handsome and very haughty young woman.  Later on she was married to the Swedish king, Eiríkr Sigrsæll (Eiríkr the Victorious), and their son was Óláf of Sweden, who afterwards ruled that country.  Eirík died
1 CONC  of a sickness at Uppsala, ten years after the fall of Styrbjorn.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigríð married the first time ca. 948, wedding King Eiríkr VI Sigrsæll (Eiríkr the Victorious) of Sweden.  She had three children by this marriage:  Bjorn Eiríksson (b. 949), Edmund Eiríksson (b. 961), and King Óláf II Eiríksson, 
1 CONC called Skotkonung (b. 970).
1 CONT
1 CONT In Óláfs saga Tryggvasonar (The Saga of Óláf Tryggvason), chapter 43, we are told:
1 CONT
1 CONT As set down before, Harald Grenski was king in the Westfold District 2.  He married Ásta, the daughter of Guðbrandr kúla 3.  One summer, when Harald Grenski had gone on a Viking expedition to the Baltic to acquire possessions, he 
1 CONC came to Sweden.  At that time Óláf the Swedish was king there.  He was the son of King Eiríkr Sigrsæll and Sigríð, a daughter of Skoglar-Tósti. Sigríð was widowed then and owned many and large estates in Sweden.  Now when she lear
1 CONC ned that Harald Grenski, her foster brother, had come ashore not far away, she sent messengers to him, inviting him to a banquet.  He did not delay long and came with a large company of men.  They were entertained splendidly there
1 CONC .  The king and the queen 4 sat in the high-seat and drank together in the evening, and all his men were entertained most lavishly.  In the evening, when the king sought his bed he found it decked with covers of costly stuff and m
1 CONC ade up with sheets of precious material.  There were few persons in these lodgings.  And when the king had undressed and gotten into bed, the queen came to him and herself poured out a beaker for him, enticing him much to drink, a
1 CONC nd treating him in the most ingratiating way.  The king was dead drunk, and so was she.  Then the king went to sleep, and the queen also lay down 5.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigríð was an exceedingly clever woman and prescient about many things 6.  Again in the morning following, the entertainment was of the best.  But then it happened, as is generallythe case, when men have drunk to excess, that on t
1 CONC he following day most of them go slow about drinking.  But the queen was merry, and she and the king talked with one another.  She said that she valued her possessions and the dominion she had in Sweden no less than his kingdom an
1 CONC d his possessions in Norway.  The king became displeased with her utterances 7. He grew cool about everything and prepared to leave in great ill humor; but the queen was in a most cheerful mood and said farewell to him, presenting
1 CONC  him with lordly farewell gifts.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the autumn following, Harald returned to Norway and remained in rather poor spirits.  When summer came he sailed into the Baltic again with his fleet.  He steered to Sweden and sent word to Queen Sigríð that he wished to see he
1 CONC r again.  She rode down the coast to meet with him, and they spoke together.  He soon came to the point, asking if she would marry him.  She said that he was insincere in proposing that, because he was so well married that he shou
1 CONC ld be well-satisfied.  Harald replied that Ásta was, to be sure, a good woman and worthy, "but she is not as highborn as I am."
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigríð said, "It may well be that you are of nobler birth than she.  But I should think that the good fortune of both of you reposes with her8."  Few more words were exchanged bewteen them before the queen rode away.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Harald remained behind in heavy spirits.  He made ready to ride inland to meet Queen Sigríð again.  Many of his men advised against that, but he proceeded nonetheless with a numerous company of men and arrived at the estate b
1 CONC elonging to the queen.  That same evening another king came there.  He was Vissavald from Garðaríki in the east 9.  He came to ask for her in marriage.  Both kings and their retinue were housed in a large and ancient hall furnishe
1 CONC d in the same manner.  Plentiful drink was served there in the evening.  It was so potent that all became dead drunk and that both their bodyguards and the watch posted without fell asleep.  Then Queen Sigríð had them assailed in 
1 CONC the night with both fire and sword.  The hall burned, together with the men inside, and those who got out were slain.  Sigríð said that in this way she was going to break kinglets of the habit of visiting her to ask for her in mar
1 CONC riage.  In after times she was called Sigríð Stórráða.
1 CONT
1 CONT Here Sigríð is protecting her rights, her personal freedom, and her possessions.  A widow with children in Norse culture had many rights, among them the right to select her own spouses (if she chose to remarry) and the right to ow
1 CONC n and manage her property, such of it as was not the inheritance of her sons.  Sigríð had shown great favor, and even erotic interest in Harald, however he made it quite clear that he did not want to marry her for herself, but onl
1 CONC y for the fact that she was wealthier than he, and that in fact he was so greedy he would set aside his good and noble wife to marry Sigríð instead.  When Sigríð reasonably refused, Harald intemperately continued to press his suit
1 CONC , bringing with him a small army to force Sigríð if need be.  It is not known whether Vissavald was a similarly poor choice as a bridegroom, but apparently so, for an enraged Sigríð had them both slaughtered.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later in Óláfs saga Tryggvasonar chapter 60, Queen Sigríð's story continued:
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Sigríð of Sweden, who was called Stórráða, resided on her estates.  That winter messengers went between King Óláf Tryggvason and Queen Sigríð, and through them King Óláf asked for the hand of Queen Sigríð.  She received his 
1 CONC suit favorably, and the betrothal was definitely agreed on.  Thereupon King Óláf sent Queen Sigríð the large gold arm ring which he had taken from the temple gate at Hlathir 10.  It was considered a splendid possession.  Their mee
1 CONC ting to arrange matters for the wedding was to be in the spring following at the boundary on an island in the Gaut Elf River.
1 CONT
1 CONT Now, as to this ring which King Óláf had sent Queen Sigríð and which was so greatly valued by all -- with the queem there were two smiths, brothers.  And when they held the ring in their hands and weighed it and talked secretly to
1 CONC gether, the queen had them called to her and asked them why they laughed in their sleeves about the ring.  They would not say.  Then she told them by all means to let her know what they had noticed.  They said that the ring was co
1 CONC unterfeit.  Then she had the ring broken in two, and there was seen to be copper inside it.  Then the queen was furious and said that Óláf would defraud her in more things than that.
1 CONT
1 CONT The importance of the false ring cannot be overstated here.  The whole status of the wedding turned around the bride-price paid by the groom -- if the groom could not come up with a certain minimum value (the so-called "poor man's
1 CONC  price") the marriage was not even legal and any children born of it would be bastards.  It's impossible to say what the actual value of the arm ring was, but certainly a piece of gold-plated copper is worth far less than an equal
1 CONC  amount of solid gold, so Sigríð has been mortally insulted by this.  The value of the ring in essence reflects the value or esteem in which the bridegroom holds his bride, and so Sigríð had been insulted 11.
1 CONT
1 CONT Worse, the insult had been a major and public one -- folks all around the countryside had been talking about this fine and magnificent arm ring, and now the ring Sigríð was actually given turned out to be a cheap fake.  It doesn't
1 CONC  matter at this point whether or not King Óláf knew it to be a fake or not, for the insult has occurred and Queen Sigríð made a public laughingstock.  And that is the most intolerable insult of all -- the Vikings put a high price 
1 CONC upon personal dignity and honor, so much so that the laws insisted that a person who had been insulted three times can only settle lawsuits for the insults on the first two, and must avenge the third in blood or else be called cow
1 CONC ard and forever lose the right to legal redress for future insults 12.
1 CONT
1 CONT The tale continues in Óláfs saga Tryggvasonar, chapter 61:
1 CONT
1 CONT Early in spring King Óláf journeyed east to Kongungahella for the meeting with Queen Sigríð.  And when they met they discussed the matter which had been broached in winter, that they were to marry; and matters went very well.  The
1 CONC n King Óláf said that Sigríð should be baptized and accept the True Faith.  She replied in this wise: "I do not mean to abandon the faith I have had, and my kinsmen before me.  Nor shall I object to your belief in the god you pref
1 CONC er."
1 CONT
1 CONT Here Sigríð espouses the typical pagan Norse response.  It is good and right to honor one's ancestors, and a part of this is tied up in the pagan religious worship, which included elements of ancestor worship as well.  But like mo
1 CONC st free-thinking Viking Age pagans, she has no desire to foist her religion on another, and is perfectly content that Óláf should worship in his own way as well.  However, in a re-enactment of the entire struggle between the Old R
1 CONC eligion and the New, the laissez faire attitude of the pagan Sigríð was to meet determined and overwhelming deadly hostility from the Christian convert King Óláf...
1 CONT
1 CONT Then King Óláf became very angry and said hastily, "Why should I want to marry you, you dog of a heathen?" and slapped her in the face with the glove he had in his hand.  Whereupon he rose, and she too.  Then Sigríð said, "This ma
1 CONC y well be your death!" With that they parted.  The king returned north to Vík, the queen east to Sweden.
1 CONT
1 CONT Here Óláf has further compounded his prior insults against Sigríð.  One of the most demeaning and vicious insults that a man could offer a woman in Viking society was to slap her iin the face, and it was a much worse insult if don
1 CONC e in public.  An analysis of the sagas shows that women who were slapped in this way always took revenge, and the usual revenge involved deadly force.  It always resulted at the least in divorce, at the worst in a feud between cla
1 CONC ns that killed dozens before the issue ground to a bloody halt 13.
1 CONT
1 CONT Óláfs saga Tryggvasonar continues the tale of Queen Sigríð in chapter 91 where we find that Queen Sigríð finally remarries.  Sigríð married King Svein Tjuguskegg (Svein Forkbeard) of Denmark in 979, by whom she had  a daughter, Es
1 CONC trið Astrið Margaret (b. 980).  The sources are divided on Sigríð's next-to-youngest child, a daughter named Hólmfrið or Svantoslava (Swietoslava) (b. 972) and who has been listed as either the last child of Sigríð and Eiríkr or e
1 CONC lse the first child of  Sigríð and Svein14.
1 CONT
1 CONT By chapter 98, Queen Sigríð's rage against Óláf for his insults has continued to simmer over the years at a long, slow boil:
1 CONT
1 CONT As was written above, King Svein Tjuguskegg had married Sigríð Stórráða.  Sigríð was King Óláf's bitterest enemy, because King Óláf had broken the agreement with her and had slapped her face, as was written above.  She constantly 
1 CONC incited King Svein to wage war against King Óláf Tryggvason, saying that sufficient reason for that was having shared the bed of Thyri, King Svein's sister, "without asking your permission; nor would your forebears have stood for 
1 CONC that."  Such pleadings Queen Sigríð often made, and succeeded so well that King Svein was persuaded to follow her advice.  And early in the spirng King Svein sent messengers east to Sweden to the court of Óláf, King of Sweden, his
1 CONC  stepson 15 and Earl Eiríkr Hákonarson, informing them that Óláf, king of Norway, had called out an army and intended to sail to Wendland in the summer.  The messengers furthermore were charged to request the Swedish king and Earl
1 CONC  Eiríkr to levy troops and with them join King Svein, when they all together were to give battle to King Óláf.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigríð passes out of the saga's tale at this point, but the battle which was fought was the famous Battle of Svold in which King Óláf Tryggvason in his longship the Ormenlange ("Long Serpent") battled the combined Swedish and Dani
1 CONC sh forces.  Queen Sigríð won her vengeance that day, for King Óláf saw his Norwegian forces defeated and he himself leapt into the sea to drown rather than face capture by his enemies.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigríð herself lived a long life, happy in the knowledge that the grave insults paid her by King Óláf Tryggvason were avenged by his death. When King Svein Tjuguskegg went to England, Sigríð accompanied him there (ca. 1000).  Svei
1 CONC n died in 1014, with his son Knutr suceeding him to the rule of England and Denmark. Sigríð herself died ca. 1014, apparently just after the death of Svein.
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT 1  The term skoglar does not appear in the Cleasby-Vigfusson dictionary.  The nearest term is skógar, "forester, forest-man, outlaw".
1 CONT
1 CONT 2  Westfold or Vestfold is located in Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3  The by-name kúla means "ball, knob, hunch-back".
1 CONT
1 CONT 4  Here the king is Harald and the queen is Sigríð.
1 CONT
1 CONT 5  It was expected that children who were fostered together would often form close attachments, even romantic attachments.  However it is also possible that Sigríð deliberately took this action to make Harald believe that he had s
1 CONC lept with her, perhaps to prevent a more forceful attempt at rape later. Scholars are divided on the interpretation.
1 CONT
1 CONT 6  Traditionally throughout early Germanic history women had always been held to have the innate power of prophecy, a power that was increased in queens because of their sacral duties as representatives of the community.
1 CONT
1 CONT 7  Apparently King Harald was angry and embarrassed because Sigríð was wealthier than he was.
1 CONT
1 CONT 8  This is an instance of Sigríð's foretelling abilities.
1 CONT
1 CONT 9  Garðaríki, "the land of garths or towns", meaning Russia.
1 CONT
1 CONT 10  This would have been an oath-ring sacred to the god Þórr that the Christian king had looted from a desecrated pagan temple.
1 CONT
1 CONT 11  See the Viking Answer Lady article about Viking Weddings for further details about bride-price and legitimacy of children.
1 CONT
1 CONT 12  See the Viking Answer Lady article on Holmgang for more information on the area of insult and redress.
1 CONT
1 CONT 13  See the Viking Answer Lady article on Viking Weddings in the "Divorce" section for more details.
1 CONT
1 CONT 14  This latter is most likely -- probably the girl's name was Hólmfrið, and she gained the second name a nickname after her marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT 15  This Olaf was Sigríð's son by King Eiríkr Sigrsæll.
