0 @N6000000003615738228@ NOTE Alias:<ALIA> Harald /Bluetooth/ 
1 CONT Harold Bluetooth (BLAATAND)
1 CONT Born 911; died 1 November, 985 or 986. He was the son of King Gorm the
1 CONT Old of Denmark and ofThyra, daughter of a noblemen of Schleswig
1 CONT (Sunderjylland) who is supposed to have been kindly disposed towards
1 CONT Christianity. His mother must have implanted in the child's soul the
1 CONT first germs of faith which his father, a devout servant of Wotan, did his
1 CONT utmost to destroy. The latter's invasion of Friesland in 934 involved him
1 CONT in war with the German King, Henry I. Having been vanquished, he was
1 CONT forced to restore the churches which he had demolished as well as to
1 CONT grant toleration to his Christian subjects, and he died one year later,
1 CONT bequeathing his throne to Harold. Bishop Unni of Bremen, accompanied by
1 CONT Benedictine monks from the Abbey of Corvey, preached the gospel in
1 CONT Jutland (Jylland) and the Danish isles, and soon won the confidence of
1 CONT the young ruler, although he did not succeed in persuading him to receive
1 CONT baptism. Harold sought to shut the Germans out of his kingdom by
1 CONT strengthening the "Danawirk"-a series of ramparts and fortifications that
1 CONT existed until the latter half of thenineteenth century; moreover, as
1 CONT absolute quiet prevailed throughout the interior, he was even able to
1 CONT turn his thoughts to foreign enterprises. Again and again he came to the
1 CONT help of Richard the Fearless of Normandy (in the years945 and 963),
1 CONT while his son conquered Semland and, after the assassination of King
1 CONT Harold Graafeld of Norway, he also managed to force the people of that
1 CONT country into temporary subjection to himself. Meanwhile the new religion
1 CONT had become more and more deeply rooted among the Danes. Even a few
1 CONT members of the nobility (such as Frode, Viceroy of Jutland) embraced the
1 CONT faith and soon episcopal sees were established (Schleswig, Ribe, Aarhus).
1 CONT However the prominent part the Germans had in these achievements as well
1 CONT as the lofty idea of the Roman Empire then prevailing led Otto I, the
1 CONT Great, to require Harold to recognize him as "advocatus", or lord
1 CONT protector of the Danish church, and evenas "Lord Paramount". It is easy
1 CONT to understand why the indignant king of the Danes replied to this demand
1 CONT with a declaration of war, and why the "emperor"sought to force his
1 CONT "vassal" into subjection. The devastating expeditions, which were pushed
1 CONT as far as the Lÿmfjord, enabled the emperor to beat down allopposition
1 CONT (972), and to compel Harold not only to conclude peace but to accept
1 CONT baptism. Henceforth paganism steadily lost ground. The Bishopric of
1 CONT Odense was established at Fünen (Fyen) in 980; the sacrificial grove at
1 CONT Lethra (on Zealand), which, until then, had been from time to time the
1 CONT scene of many human sacrifices, was deserted. King Harold removed his
1 CONT royal residence to Roeskilde and erected there a wooden church dedicated
1 CONT to the Holy Trinity. Later (in the eleventh century) it was replaced by a
1 CONT basilica which in turn was soon torn down. Since about the year 1200 its
1 CONT site has been occupied by the Gothic cathedral (dedicated to St. Lucius),
1 CONT the burial place of the kings of Denmark. Christian houses of worship
1 CONT were also built in many other places during Harold's reign; in these
1 CONT German and Danish priests preached the gospel of the crucified and risen
1 CONT Saviour. There is no doubt that Harold professed Christianity at that
1 CONT time: it is also true that he contributed to its spread. But his moral
1 CONT conduct in many respects distinctly violated the Divine commandments.
1 CONT Consequently many people looked on the plots that were directed against
1 CONT the sovereignty and life of the ageing prince by his own son (Svend) as a
1 CONT punishment from Heaven. Although baptized, the latter joined forces with
1 CONT Palnatoke, the most powerful chieftain on Fünen, who was leader of the
1 CONT heathen party. The fortunes of war varied for a time, but finally Harold
1 CONT was slain on 1 November, 985 or 986. His remains were buried in the
0 @N6000000003635713186@ NOTE [1062] WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 17441666 = 1841422
1 CONT
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta Encyclopedia, 1993
1 CONT Harold Bluetooth (died about 985), king of Denmark (circa 940-85), who consolidated the Danish realm as a unitary kingdom. He was the son of Gorm the Old (d. about 940) and inherited his kingdom, centered on Jelling in Jutland. Al
1 CONC though he temporarily (974-83) lost southern Jutland to Holy Roman Emperor Otto II, Harold secured all Denmark under his control and extended his rule to much of Norway. Baptized in 960 and thus introducing Christianity into Denma
1 CONC rk, he had the famous Jelling rune stone erected in his parents' memory. Harold was fatally wounded during a rebellion by his son and successor, Sweyn I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Blaatand Gormson - COMYN4.TAF (Compuserve), p. 5
1 CONT
1 CONT "The Illustrious Lineage of the Royal House of Britain" Harald Blaatand, d 986
0 @N6000000003949978553@ NOTE
1 CONT In 948 Harald submitted to emperor Otto I and had German bishops establish the bishoprics of Ribe, Arhus and Schleswig. In 960 Harald was himself converted at a place called Poppo stone.
1 CONT During his reign, Harald oversaw the reconstruction not only of the Jelling runic stones but of other projects as well. Some believe that these projects were a way for him to preserve the economic and military control of his count
1 CONC ry. During that time, ring forts were built in five strategic locations: Trelleborg on Sjælland, Nonnebakken on Fyn, Fyrkat in central Jylland, Aggersborg near Limfjord, and Trelleborg near the city of Trelleborg in Scania in pres
1 CONC ent-day Sweden. All five fortresses had similar designs: "perfectly circular with gates opening to the four corners of the earth, and a courtyard divided into four areas which held large houses set in a square pattern"[4] A sixth 
1 CONC Trelleborg is located in Borgeby, in Scania in present-day Sweden. This one has been dated to the vicinity of 1000 AD and has a similar design, so it too may have been built by king Harald.
1 CONT While absolute quiet prevailed throughout the interior, he was even able to turn his thoughts to foreign enterprises. Again and again he came to the help of Richard the Fearless of Normandy (in the years 945 and 963), while his so
1 CONC n conquered Samland and, after the assassination of King Harald Graafeld of Norway, he also managed to force the people of that country into temporary subjection to himself.
1 CONT The Norse sagas presents Harald in a rather negative light. He was forced twice to submit to the renegade Swedish prince Styrbjörn the Strong of the Jomsvikings- first by giving Styrbjörn a fleet and his daughter Tyra, the second 
1 CONC time by giving up himself as hostage and an additional fleet. Styrbjörn brought this fleet to Uppsala in Sweden in order to claim the throne of Sweden. However, this time Harald broke his oath and fled with his Danes in order to a
1 CONC void facing the Swedish army at the Battle of the Fýrisvellir.
1 CONT As a consequence of Harald's army having lost to the Germans in the shadow of Danevirke in 974, he no longer had control of Norway and Germans having settled back into the border area between Scandinavia and Germany. The German se
1 CONC ttlers were driven out of Denmark in 983 by an alliance consisting of Obodrite soldiers and troops loyal to Harald. Soon after, Harald was killed fighting off a rebellion led by his son Sweyn. He was believed to have died in 986, 
1 CONC although there are many other accounts that claim he died in 985.
0 @N6000000004533040921@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Harald Bluetooth)
1 CONT Harold Bluetooth Gormson (Danish Harald Blåtand, Norwegian HaraldBlåtann) (ca 935- November 1, 986), sometimes Harold II, succeeded hisfather Gorm the Old as king of Denmark in 958 (or 959) and was king ofNorway for a few years, p
1 CONC robably around 970.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although his predecessors had accepted Christianity at the instigationof the Frankish Carolingian kings in 826, many Danes and othernortherners were still heathens for centuries. Harald Bluetoothembraced Christianity around 960 or
1 CONC  965, but whether this was a purelypolitical move or the result of an actual conversion on Harold's partis impossible to say. His son Sweyn (Forkbeard) was baptized alongwith the rest of the royal family, and given the name of the
1 CONC  HolyRoman emperor Otto the Great. Harold was wounded in battle against theforces of his son and successor Sweyn. He is believed to have died onNovember 1st, 986, although 985 and 987 are also mentioned, and isbest remembered for 
1 CONC having erected a large runic stone at Jelling:"Harold, king, bade these memorials to be made after Gorm, his father,and Thyra, his mother. The Harald who won the whole of Denmark andNorway and turned the Danes to Christianity."
1 CONT
1 CONT (In Old Norse: Haraltr kunukr bath kaurua kubl thausi aft kurm fathursin auk aft thaurui muthur sina sa haraltr ias sar uan tanmaurk alaauk nuruiak auk tani karthi kristna.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Harold may have had three wives or consorts: Thora, Gunhilde and Gyrid(the niece of the Swedish king Eric the Victorious). He is believed tohave had four children: Håkon, Sweyn I, Gunhild and Tyra (who marriedStyrbjörn Starke).
