0 @N6000000002187645696@ NOTE Eric VI of Sweden
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Eric the Victorious)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Eric the Victorious (VI), Old Norse: Eiríkr inn sigrsæli, Modern Swedish: Erik Segersäll, (970?- 994 or 995), was king of the Swedes during the second half of the 10th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT The extent of his kingdom is disputed. In addition to the Swedish heartland round lake Mälaren it may have extended down the Baltic Sea coast as far south as Blekinge.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Norse sagas relate that he was the son of Björn Eriksson and that he ruled together with his brother Olof Björnsson. He married Sigrid the Haughty, the daughter of the legendary Viking Skagul Toste, but would later divorce her
1 CONC  and give her Götaland as a fief. Before this happened, his brother Olof died, and a new co-ruler had to be appointed. The Swedes refused to accept his rowdy nephew Styrbjörn Starke as his co-ruler and the controversy was settled 
1 CONC when Eric suggested that the new co-ruler would be his and Sigrid's unborn child, on condition that it was a son. Styrbjörn was given 60 longships by Eric and sailed away to live as a Viking. Styrbjörn would become the ruler of Jo
1 CONC msborg and an ally and brother-in-law of the Danish king Harold Bluetooth. Styrbjörn returned to Sweden with a major Danish army, which Eric defeated in the Battle of the Fýrisvellir at Old Uppsala.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Adam of Bremen, Eric would conquer Denmark and chase away its king Sweyn Forkbeard and proclaimed himself the king of Sweden and Denmark which he ruled until his death which would have taken place in 994 or 995. He is
1 CONC  said to have been baptised in Denmark, but later returned to the Norse gods.
1 CONT
1 CONT In all probability he founded the town of Sigtuna, which still exists and where the first Swedish coins were stamped for his son and successor Olof Skötkonung.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Adam of Bremen only gives Emund Eriksson as predecessor to Eric the Victorious, but it is possible that Emund and Björn were co-rulers, like Erik and Olof and their semi-legendary ancestors Björn at Hauge and Anund Uppsal
1 CONC e.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Björn Eriksson(?)
1 CONT and/or Emund Eriksson King of Sweden
1 CONT with Olof Björnsson 
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Olof of Sweden 
