0 @N6000000003949721621@ NOTE Erik the Victorious was married several times. One of his wives is said to have been Sigrid Storrada ("great ruler'), the daughter of Skoglar Toste, a famous Viking from Vastergotland. According to Snorri, she was mother of Erik's
1 CONC  son Olof.
1 CONT First King of all Sweden who reigned from 970-994. He was on many warring expeditions and increased the dominion of the Swedes and defended it valiantly. He was easy to approach for advice.
1 CONT About 985, according to the ancient tales, Erik fought a great battle against his still-rebellious nephew Stybjorn on the great plains of Fyrisvall, near Uppsala. . . . 
1 CONT Erik found it hard to get along with his wife Sigrid, due to her imperious temperament. After a while they went their separate ways. She retired to her estates in Vastergotland, where we are told that she ruled in great splendor a
1 CONC nd refused many suitors who were hungry for both her beauty and her lands. Among others, she is said to have refused Harald Granske of Norway and King Vifavald of Russia (a petty king of whom we know nothing further). Sigrid has b
1 CONC een called "the Haughty" and she seems to have truly earned this surname. [Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, & Kiev]
1 CONT  From the above I assume that Sigrid "divorced" Erik a short while after the battle in 985
0 @N6000000004533089657@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Eric the Victorious (VI), or Erik Segersäll, (985?- 995), was king ofthe Swedes during the last two decades of the 10th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT The extent of his kingdom is disputed. In addition to the Svealandheartland round lake Mälaren it may have extended down the Baltic Seacoast as far south as Blekinge. Details on Eric's reign are sparse. Itis claimed that he defeat
1 CONC ed a major Danish army under the command ofhis nephew Styrbjörn Starke at the Fyris Wolds at Old Uppsala, fromwhich his accolade "the victorious" stems. In all probability hefounded the town of Sigtuna, which still exists and wher
1 CONC e the firstSwedish coins were stamped for his son and successor Olof Skötkonung.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eric is claimed to have married Sigrid the Haughty, daughter ofMieszko I of Poland, in the 980s.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Sagas, Eric the Victorious ruled together with hisbrother Olof (II) Björnsson. After Olof Björnsson's death, Ericproclaimed his still unborn child co-ruler instead of his nephewStyrbjörn Starke. The disinherited S
1 CONC tyrbjörn was to attack Eric with alarge Danish force. The victory rendered Eric the cognomen"Victorious".
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Adam of Bremen, who is considered to be more reliable thanthe sagas, only gives Emund Eriksson as predecessor to Eric theVictorious. He does, however, not claim that Emund was the father ofErik, and nothing contradicts th
1 CONC e possibility that Emund and Björnwere co-rulers, like Erik and Olof and their semi-legendary ancestorsBjörn at Hauge and Anund Uppsale.
0 @N6000000007329725721@ NOTE døde han "sotdöden" på kongsgården i Gamla Uppsala, den største,
1 CONT "slugaste och lyckosammaste" nordmannakongen i sin tid.
1 CONT ble han konge ved sin fars død, i begynnelsen sammen med sin yngre bror
1 CONT Olof.
1 CONT ble han konge alene ved Olofs død.
1 CONT avslo han kravet fra Olofs sønn, den 12-årige Styrbjörn starke, på farens
1 CONT del av kongemakten.
1 CONT "försköt" han sin dronning (en vendisk kongsdatter, ifølge sagaen den
1 CONT norske stormannsdatteren Sigrid Storråda) og giftet seg med en datter av
1 CONT jarl Håkon i Norge.
1 CONT vant han etter tradisjonen tredagersslaget på Fyrisvallarna mot
1 CONT Styrbjörns och Harald Blåtands av Danmark overmakt (tusen seil).
1 CONT fordrev han Sven Tveskjegg og la under seg Danmark og Skåneriket, som
1 CONT begge dog gikk tapt ved hans død.
1 CONT skall han ha blitt døpt, men straks etter fallt tilbake til hedendommen.
