0 @N6000000001562663911@ NOTE Following the defeat of her first husband, Aethelred the Unready in 1013, Emma took her two children, Alfred and Edward to Normandy for safety. Here they grew up and were influenced by French culture and ways. She returned to Engl
1 CONC and where she married Canute, leaving her children behind. In 1036, Alfred, known as the Aetheling, came to England to see his mother, possibly as a trap set up by Emma, who had no real love of the children she had with Aethelred.
1 CONT
1 CONT So affected was Edward by the way he had been treated by his mother, he removed her entitlement to her land and confiscated all her possessions.
0 @N6000000005598043964@ NOTE Emma, Daughter of Duke Richard I of the reigning house of Normandy.
0 @N6000000002187576660@ NOTE Emma of Normandy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Emma (c. 985–March 6, 1052 in Winchester, Hampshire), called Ælfgifu, was daughter of Richard the Fearless, Duke of Normandy, by his second wife Gunnora. She was Queen consort of the Kingdom of England twice, by successive marriag
1 CONC es: initially as the second wife to Ethelred the Unready of England (1002-1016); and then to Canute the Great of Denmark (1017-1035). Two of her sons, one by each husband, and two stepsons, also by each husband, were to be kings o
1 CONC f England, as was her great-nephew, William the Conqueror, Duke of Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Family tree 
1 CONT 3 Bibliography 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Upon the Danish invasion of England in 1013, Emma's sons by Ethelred - Edward the Confessor and Alfred Atheling - went to Normandy as exiles, where they were to remain. Canute, the King of England, after the deaths of Ethelred and
1 CONC  his son, and Emma's stepson, Edmund II Ironside, married her himself. He was to pledge that Harthacanute, Emma's son by him, should be the heir to his Danish sovereignty, which meant, through this wedding, the Normans were kept a
1 CONC t arms length, contentedly quiet and quietly content.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ethelred's marriage to Emma was an English strategy to avert the aggression of dangerous Normandy, and the Danish strategy was much the same. With a Normandy in feudal subordination to the kings of France, who kept it as their duk
1 CONC edom, England was the Norman dukes' main target, after baronic feuds and rampaging pillages through Brittany had run their course. English kings could not afford to underestimate the Norman threat. Harthacanute, named after the fi
1 CONC rst head of Canute's royal house, was certainly intended to rule as the Danish ruler of England, along with most of Scandinavia, which, if he had succeeded, may have made for a very different history. It is thought though, due not
1 CONC  least to the extolling of her encomium, that Canute was fond of Emma. In this, an affectionate marriage and the ability to keep the threat from over the channel at bay, was seen as a happy coincidence. Unfortunately, events did n
1 CONC ot go as well as they might.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Canute's death, Edward and Alfred returned to England out of exile in 1036, in an expedition to see their mother and under their half-brother Harthacanute's protection. This was seen as a move against Harold Harefoot, Canute
1 CONC 's son by Aelfgifu of Northampton, who now put himself forward as Harold I with the support of many of the English noblity. In contempt of Harthacanute, and at war with his enemies in Scandinavia, the younger Alfred was captured, 
1 CONC blinded, and shortly after died from his wounds. The elder, Edward, escaped to Normandy. Emma herself was soon to leave for Bruges and the court of the Count of Flanders. It was at this court that the Encomium Emmae was written.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Twice the Queen of the English kingdom, Emma of Normandy sits here in receipt of the Encomium Emmae, with her sons Harthacanute and Edward the Confessor in the frame.The death of Harold I in 1040 and the accession of the more conc
1 CONC iliatory Harthacanute, who had lost his Norwegian and Swedish lands, although he had made his Danish realm secure, meant Edward was officially made welcome in England the next year. Harthacanute told the Norman court that Edward s
1 CONC hould be made king if he himself had no sons. Edward was subsequently King of England on the death of Harthacanute, who, like Harold I, met his end in the throes of a fit. Emma was also to return to England, yet was cast aside, as
1 CONC  she supported Magnus the Noble, not Edward, her son - she is not thought to have had any love for her children from her first marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emma of Normandy might well have seen herself as one coming second to the first wife, in two marriages. In England, with respect to Ethelred's first wife Aelfgifu, who possibly died in childbirth, or from complications during labo
1 CONC ur[1], she, was known as Aelfgifu[1], a mere replacement. With her marriage to Canute, set in the shade of his 'handfast' wife, Aelfgifu of Northampton, she, at the time was known as Aelfgifu of Normandy. Each of her marriages, th
1 CONC en, in some way left her as a second Aelfgifu, which she was clearly inclined to abandon, preferring her other name, Emma. Despite her being a second wife her noble marriages, created a strong connection between England and Norman
1 CONC dy, which was to find its culmination under her great-nephew William the Conqueror, and 1066.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family tree
