1 CONT In re Holy Trinity, Bosham [2004] Fam 124 - decision of the Chichester Consistory Court regarding opening King Harold's proposed grave. 
0 @N6000000002322325979@ NOTE Harold was a short-lived King of England; ruled from January 6, 1066 to
1 CONT October 14, 1066, when he died in battle during the Norman conquest at the
1 CONT Battle of Hastings in 1066 having succeeded Edward the Confessor who had
1 CONT died in January. Harold had earlier been made the Earl of Anglia in 1045 and
1 CONT was to be the last Anglo-Saxon King of England. William I, the Conqueror,
1 CONT would succeed him as King of England. Still earlier, Harold became Earl of
1 CONT Wessex when his father, Godwin, died in 1053.
0 @N6000000001744974559@ NOTE Became King upon the death of Edward the Confessor in 1066. Was challenged by his brother Tosti and the Norwegian army, led by Harald Hardrade, in September of 1066 at Stamford Bridge, at which time both Tosti and Harald Hardrade 
1 CONC are slain. However, he was defeated and killed 4 days later at the Battle of Hastings by another of Norse descent, William the Conqueror of Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Snorre Sturlason (1179-1241), Heimskringla or the Lives of the Norse Kings (NY: Dover, 1932, 1990) 554-555.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following from: http://www.camelotintl.com/royal/cgi/person?p=148:
1 CONT
1 CONT Harold
1 CONT            Title(s):
1 CONT                   Earl of East Anglia (1044 cr)
1 CONT                   King of the English (1066 , Jan - Oct)
1 CONT       Date of birth:
1 CONT                   About 1022
1 CONT       Place of birth:
1 CONT                   Unknown
1 CONT      Date of death:
1 CONT                   October 14, 1066
1 CONT       Age at death:
1 CONT                   45
1 CONT      Place of death:
1 CONT                   Hastings, East Sussex, England
1 CONT     Cause of death:
1 CONT                   Killed in Battle
1 CONT         Burried at:
1 CONT                   Waltham Abbey, Essex, England
1 CONT            Father:
1 CONT                   Godwin, Earl of Wessex
1 CONT           Mother:
1 CONT                   Gytha
1 CONT  Other Information:
1 CONT                   Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT  Marriages:
1 CONT     Unknown date:
1 CONT                   Agatha
1 CONT      January 1066:
1 CONT                   At age 44 married Edith
1 CONT
1 CONT  Events
1 CONT     January 6, 1066
1 CONT                   Coronation
1 CONT                   Westminster Abbey, London
1 CONT                   England
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes:
1 CONT 1.  According to some sources, Harold had a mistress (or she may have been his
1 CONT first wife) named Edith Swanneshals, who bore him six children: Godwin,
1 CONT Edmund, Magnus, Ulf, Gytha and Gunhild.
1 CONT 2. Harold was crowned by Ealdred, Archbishop of York.
1 CONT 3. Killed by an arrow (possibly in the eye) at the battle of Hastings.
1 CONT
1 CONT ******
1 CONT Royal Database
1 CONT                      Edith (wife of King Harold II)
1 CONT
1 CONT  Personal Details:
1 CONT          Name:
1 CONT                Edith
1 CONT    Date of birth:
1 CONT                Unknown
1 CONT    Place of birth:
1 CONT                Unknown
1 CONT    Date of death:
1 CONT                Unknown
1 CONT   Place of death:
1 CONT                Unknown
1 CONT  Cause of death:
1 CONT                Unknown
1 CONT         Father:
1 CONT                Unknown
1 CONT         Mother:
1 CONT                Unknown
1 CONT
1 CONT  Marriages:
1 CONT    January 1066:
1 CONT                Harold II, King of the English, age 44
1 CONT
1 CONT  Notes:
1 CONT               1
1 CONT                Daughter of Elfgar, Earl of Mercia.
1 CONT               2
1 CONT                Before marrying Harold, Edith had been married to Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, Ruler
1 CONT                of All Wales, who was killed in 1063.
1 CONT
1 CONT ********
1 CONT Harold II "Harold Godwinson" 1066
1 CONT
1 CONT Although on Edward the Confessor's bequest William of Normandy was to be his successor the Saxon council or Witanagemot would not follow his wishes. Upon Edward's death the son of that arch-meddler in the politics of the time, Ear
1 CONC l Godwin of Wessex and Kent, was elected the new King of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Harold had become a close ally to Edward and had helped his predecessor rule in the capacity of an under-king.
1 CONT
1 CONT As with many of the successions to the throne in these times there were of course more than one claimant to the crown. With an almost indecent haste Harold had himself crowned at the newly built Westminster Abbey just one day afte
1 CONC r the death of Edward. His coronation led to his immediate priority in ruling his nation being its defence. He faced not only the Normans from the south but also the Norwegians, under Harold Hardrada in alliance with the exiled Ea
1 CONC rl of Northumbria, to the north.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Norwegians struck first, with their King throwing back his claim to the throne to the days of Magnus and Harthacnut. Harold may have been ambitious in the extreme but he was no less a warrior and at the Battle of Stamford Brid
1 CONC ge despatched the Norwegian claims along with the lives of King Magnus and the Earl of Northumbria.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was while he and his army celebrated their victory that they learned of William's landing along the Sussex coast, many miles from their present position in York. It took a full fourteen days for the Saxons to march south and pr
1 CONC epare for the Battle of Hastings, time used wisely by the Normans to
1 CONT organise their order of battle. October 14, was a long and bloody day. The Normans and the Saxons exchanging fortunes at every turn. The bloodshed finally ended at sunset when Harold was cut down by a Norman arrow and killed by th
1 CONC e heavily armoured Norman foot soldiers.
1 CONT
1 CONT If Harold had won the battle and had survived on the throne he would have altered the course of British history.
1 CONT
1 CONT Harold was married twice. His first wife, Eadgyth Swan-neck presented him with six children; Godwine, Eadmund, Magnus, Ulf, Gytha and Gunhild. His second wife, Ealdgyrth ap Llywelyn, (Widow of the Ruler of All Wales) produced one 
1 CONC son, Harold. It is thought that Harold II was in his early forties when he died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another account: "Harold was about 45 years old at his death. William is said to have had his body buried on the sea shore, but there is a tradition that Harold's mistress Edith Swan-neck sought it out and took it to Waltham Abbey
1 CONC  for burial. Harold had lived happily with Edith for many years and she had borne him many children, including a daughter Gytha, named after Harold's mother, who was to
1 CONT seek refuge abroad after the Conquest and eventually to marry Vladimir Monomakh, Grand Duke of Kiev. In 1065 Harold married Ealdgyth, the widow of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, King of Gwynedd and Powys, and dau of Alfgar, Earl of Mercia.
1 CONC  She bore him at least one son, Harold, whose fate is unknown. There is much confusion between Ealdgyth and Edith Swan-neck. With such spelling, is
1 CONT there any wonder?" From http://ftp.cac.psu.edu/~saw/royal/r32.html#I1540.
