1 CONT           På nyåret reiste de av birkebeinerne - som hadde overlevd slaget på Re ved
1 CONT           Tønsberg til Sverre Sigurdsson, og ville ha ham som konge, og uten betenkeligheter
1 CONT           påtok han seg å være fører for dem. Sammen med birkebeinerne slet han seg frem til
1 CONT           Nidaros, og greide å slå kong Magnus Erlingsson sine lendmenn. Sverre Sigurdsson
1 CONT           ble tatt til konge i Viken i Gulatings- lagdømmet i mars 1177. I to år var kong Sverre
1 CONT           stadig på krigsfot. Først i 1179 vant han og birkebeinerne i hovedslaget på
1 CONT           Kalvskinnet - den gang en åker ved Nidarosdomen. Her falt Erling Skakke (19. juni
1 CONT           1179) sammen med mange lendmenn og hirdmenn, men kong Magnus Erlingsson
1 CONT           klarte å rømme og kom seg til Danmark. Kong Magnus Erlingsson led et nytt
1 CONT           nederlag på Ilevollen - den 27. mai 1180, og måtte gi opp Trøndelag. Men han
1 CONT           hadde mektig støtte i vest og sør, samt fra Danmar
1 CONT           Det avgjørende slaget stod den 15. juni 1184 i Norafjorden (Nordfjord) - Fimreite i
1 CONT           Sogn. Kong Magnus Erlingsson drukna og en rekke lendmenn mistet livet.
1 CONT           Mannefallet var stort - trolig langt over 1000. Kong Sverre Sigurdsson gravla liket til
1 CONT           kong Magnus Erlingsson i Bergen med den største ære, og holdt selv minnetale.
1 CONT           Kong Magnus Erlingsson var 28 år da han falt. Nå ble Sverre Sigurdsson konge
1 CONT           over hele landet. Han belønnet birkebeinerne det de fortjente, gjorde mange til
1 CONT           lendmenn og sysselmenn, og lot mange få rike giftemål. Erkebiskop Øystein - som
1 CONT           hadde vært en arg uvenn - gikk nå i forlik med kong Sverre, og det samme gjorde
1 CONT           flere av de gamle stormenn. Det danske herredømmet i Viken ble det nå slutt på. 
1 CONT           Mangt og mye ble nå forandret i Norge. Kongemakten ble nå tatt ut av hendene på
1 CONT           stormennene. Kong Sverre var etter den gamle arveretten odelsbarn til
1 CONT           kongedømmet, men turde ikke snakke med bispene og lendmenn om dette. 
1 CONT           Bondestanden hadde etter hvert blitt mer demokratisk. Kong Sverre arbeidet for et
1 CONT           sterkt kongedømme på demokratisk grunnlag, og gjorde mye for at det skulle
1 CONT           fortsette slik det hadde begynt. Han innsatte flere lagmenn i hvert lagdømme - som
1 CONT           skulle ta seg av tvister - så allmuen som ikke var lovkyndig eller ikke hadde råd til
1 CONT           søksmål skulle slippe å gå til lagtinget. For at kongedømmet skulle bli sterkt tok kong
1 CONT           Sverre makten fra lendmennene, noe de hadde tilegnet seg i ufredsår. Tidligere
1 CONT           hadde ikke kongen andre enn årmenn, som var lite ansett, og som trengte hjelp av
1 CONT           lendmennene. Det ble nå innsatt sysselmenn - som var storætta - særlig på de
1 CONT           stedene i riket hvor kongen skjelden hadde tilsyn med selv. Kong Sverre trengte
1 CONT           pålitelige embetsmenn - som det stod respekt av - derfor innsatte han sysselmenn i
1 CONT           alle byene. 
1 CONT           Kirken mistet nå en del av makten. Erkebiskopen rømte til Danmark, men allikevel
1 CONT           fikk kong Sverre biskopene til å krone og salve seg i Bergen, så ingen lenger kunne
1 CONT           nekte ham retten til kongedømmet. Men paven holdt med den rømte erkebiskopen
1 CONT           og lyste kong Sverre i bann - det vil si - ut av kirkesamfunnet.
1 CONT           Det var mange i landet som ikke likte den nye tilstanden - som var vant til ufred og
1 CONT           slåssing. Den ene flokken etter den andre reiste seg, og kong Sverre fikk aldri ro.
1 CONT           Den slu bispen Nikolaus Arnesson fikk reist en flokk med menn, som kalte seg
1 CONT           baglerne (av bagall, en bispestav). Disse fikk hjelp av danskene - som ville hevne seg
1 CONT           på kong Sverre - for at han hadde tatt makten fra dem i Viken. Baglerne ble snart
1 CONT           farlige, og lå raskt i strid over hele landet. Baglerne tente på Bergen - så 6 kirker og
1 CONT           mange hus brente ned - bisp Nikolaus stod selv for denne ugjerningen. Kong Sverre
1 CONT           kom etter hvert i vanskeligheter. Til sist lyste pave Innocens III. bann over kong
1 CONT           Sverre og alle som var på hans side, slik at alle ble lyst ut av kirkesamfunnet. Alle
1 CONT           kirker skulle stenge og ingen skulle begraves i kristi jord. Prestene skulle bare ha lov
1 CONT           til å døpe barn og skrifte for folk som lå på det sist
1 CONT           I Viken hatet folket kong Sverre slik at det en gang dro minst 15000 bønder fra hele
1 CONT           området - fra Telemark til Svinesund, til Oslo. Men kong Sverre slo dem i en hard
1 CONT           strid, og vant gjentatte ganger over baglerne. Kong Sverre laget et stridsskrift mot
1 CONT           prestene, som enten han selv eller en av hans venner skrev. Her anklaget han
1 CONT           presteskapet både for løgn og annet, og tilføyde at han hadde kongedømmet fra
1 CONT           Gud, og hadde rett til å styre både landet og kirken. På denne måten sloss kong
1 CONT           Sverre både med penn og sverd, helt til det endelig så ut til at han skulle slå ned alle
1 CONT           fiendene. Men alt dette strevet og maset hadde tatt hardt på kongen - så han ble syk.
1 CONT           Han var da i Tønsberg - fordi baglerne utøvet kamper - og hadde tatt festningen på
1 CONT           "Slottsfjellet". Baglerne og deres høvding, Reidar Sendemann, holdt ut i 20 uker. Da
1 CONT           var baglerne så utsultne og syke at de gav opp festningen. Kong Sverre, som var
1 CONT           god og mild, fikk sine menn til å ta seg av de syke baglerne, som om de var hans
1 CONT           egne menn, men mange av dem døde. Straks etter seilte kong Sverre til Bergen. Da
1 CONT           han kom dit ble sykdommen forverret. Han ba sine venner løfte seg opp i høysetet,
1 CONT           og der døde han. Han ble gravlagt i domkirken, med all den stas som høver for en
1 CONT           konge, selv om han var bannlyst. Kong Sverre Sigurdsson var omkring 50 år
1 CONT           gammel da han døde.
0 @N6000000003525468785@ NOTE Konge av Norge ( 1177-1202 )
1 CONT PLAC Konge av Norge
0 @N6000000003525479670@ NOTE Konge av Norge ( 1177-1202 )
1 CONT PLAC Konge av Norge
0 @N6000000002187978922@ NOTE Sverre of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sverre Sigurdsson (Old Norse: Sverrir Sigurðarson) (c. 1145/1151 – 9 March 1202) was king of Norway from 1184 to 1202. He married Margareta Eriksdotter, the daughter of the Swedish king Eric the Saint, by whom he had the daughter 
1 CONC Kristina Sverresdotter.
1 CONT
1 CONT Many consider him one of the most important rulers in Norwegian history. He assumed power as the leader of the rebel group, the Birkebeiners, during their struggle against King Magnus Erlingsson. After Magnus fell at the Battle of
1 CONC  Fimreite in 1184, Sverre ruled as sole king of Norway. Differences with the Church, however, led to his excommunication in 1194. Another civil war began against the church-supported Baglers, which lasted beyond Sverre's death in 
1 CONC 1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most important historical sources on Sverre’s life are his biography, Sverris saga, in part written while Sverre was alive, and the Bagler sagas. This saga is likely biased, since the foreword states that part was written unde
1 CONC r Sverre’s direct sponsorship. Correspondence between the Pope and the Norwegian bishops can be used as an alternate source when it comes to church affairs. The saga and the letters mostly agree about the hard facts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Supposedly, King Sverre was short, so he usually directed his troops from horseback during battles. The contrast is great to the traditional Norse warrior ideals where the king was expected to lead his men from the front of the ba
1 CONC ttle line. Sverre was a talented improviser, both in political and military life. His innovative tactics often helped the Birkebeiners against more tradition-bound opponents. During battle he had his men operate in smaller groups,
1 CONC  while previously tactics similar to the shield wall had been preferred. This made the Birkebeiners more mobile and adaptable.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT           o 1.1 The veracity of Sverre’s claim
1 CONT           o 1.2 Norway in 1176
1 CONT           o 1.3 Sverre meets the Birkebeiners
1 CONT     * 2 Rise to power
1 CONT           o 2.1 Difficult years
1 CONT           o 2.2 Victory over the Heklungs
1 CONT     * 3 Troubled reign
1 CONT           o 3.1 Kuvlungs and Øyskjeggs
1 CONT           o 3.2 Sverre and the Church
1 CONT     * 4 The Bagler war
1 CONT     * 5 Notes
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT     * 7 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the saga, Sverre was born in 1151 to Gunnhild and her husband Unås, a comb maker from the Faroes. When Sverre was five, the family moved to the Faroes where Sverre was raised in the household of Unås’ brother Roe, bis
1 CONC hop of the Faroes on Kirkjubøargarður in Kirkjubøur. It was here that Sverre studied for the priesthood and was ordained. The priest school of Kirkjubøur must have been of a high standard, for Sverre was later described as very we
1 CONC ll educated. The legend says that he was hidden in a cave a bit outside of the village. This cave actually exists and gave the mountain Sverrihola (303 m, "Sverre's hollow") on the south tip of Streymoy its name.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sverre, however, was not suited for a priestly life. The saga states that he had several dreams which he interpreted as a sign that he was destined for greater things. Further, in 1175, his mother revealed that Sverre was really t
1 CONC he son of king Sigurd Munn. In the following year, Sverre travelled to Norway to seek his destiny.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT The veracity of Sverre’s claim
1 CONT
1 CONT The tale told in Sverre’s saga is the official version. This is problematic, especially with regard to Sverre's alleged paternity. According to the saga, when Sverre was born, Gunnhild and Unås were married. Obviously, Sverre can'
1 CONC t be the son of both Unås and Sigurd. We have only his own word for his paternity. Most modern historians consider his claim to be King Sigurd’s son to be false, an opinion shared with many of Sverre’s contemporaries. Although the
1 CONC  fact that kings fathered illegitimate sons was taken for granted, the main problem with Sverre's claim is chronological. According to the saga, Sverre was 24 when he learned of his ancestry. According to Canon law, however, candi
1 CONC dates had to be at least 30 years old to be eligible, which places Sverre’s birth no later than 1145. Sigurd Munn was born in 1133. Following the assumptions of this time-line, his paternal claim is not possible. Although the age 
1 CONC requirement could have been waived if no other suitable candidates for the priesthood existed, other facts indicate that Sverre was in his early thirties when he came to Norway, such as the age of his own sons or his nephews. If S
1 CONC verre’s claim was false, however, he would lack royal legitimacy, dooming his plans to failure. Some historians allow for the possibility that Sverre himself believed his own claim. Regardless, his motivation is clear: capturing t
1 CONC he throne of Norway despite any issues of royal blood, especially given Norway had seen other claimants, since Harald Gille, whose paternity was equally questionable.
1 CONT
1 CONT The fact that Sigurd Munn's daughter Cecilia acknowledged Sverre as Sigurd's son lacks evidentiary value. Sverre's actions offered her a welcome possibility to divorce from the marriage with Folkvid the Lawspeaker, into which she 
1 CONC claimed to have been forced by Erling Skakke.
1 CONT
1 CONT Support from Earl Birger Brosa of Sweden is more a sign of pragmatic politics from the part of the Swedes, as their ally party in Norway needed a new leader and had chosen Sverre. Sverre was not the Earl's first choice, however. T
1 CONC hey had first supported Øystein Møyla, who had died. The Swedes obviously chose to support Sverre only after he had proven to be an effective leader.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Norway in 1176
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1176, Norway was slowly recovering from decades of multiple civil wars. The causes were largely due to the lack of any clear succession laws. According to the old customs, all the king’s sons, legitimate or illegitimate, had eq
1 CONC ual right to the throne. It was customary for brothers to rule the kingdom together, but when quarrels arose war was frequently the result.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigurd Munn, claimed by Sverre as his father, had been slain by his brother Inge Krokrygg in 1155. Sigurd’s son Håkon Herdebrei had been chosen to be king by his father’s followers. The conflict was now a regional conflict, with K
1 CONC ing Inge having the strongest support in Viken, while most of Håkon’s followers from Trøndelag. Inge Krokrygg fell in 1161. His party then took the five year old Magnus Erlingsson as king. Magnus was the son of Erling Skakke and K
1 CONC ristin, daughter of King Sigurd Jorsalfar. In 1162, at the Battle of Veøy, Håkon Herdebrei fell and his faction began to fall apart. In 1164 Magnus was crowned by Øystein Erlendsson, Archbishop of Nidaros. With the Church and most
1 CONC  of the aristocracy on his side, Magnus’ kingship seemed secure. Several uprisings followed, but they were all suppressed. Erling Skakke had been regent during his son’s minority and continued to be the country’s real ruler even a
1 CONC fter Magnus had come of age.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sverre meets the Birkebeiners
1 CONT
1 CONT Thus when Sverre came to Norway he found the prospects for a successful uprising to be small. Distraught he travelled east and came to Östergötland in Sweden just before Christmas. There he met with the local ruler, Birger Brosa, 
1 CONC who was married to Sigurd Munn’s sister, Brigit Haraldsdotter. Sverre revealed to Birger Brosa his claim to the throne, but Birger was at first unwilling to give any aid. He was already supporting another group, the Birkebeiners —
1 CONC  the Birchlegs. This group had risen in 1174 under the leadership of Øystein Møyla who claimed to be the son of King Øystein Haraldsson. They had received the name Birkebeiners because their poverty led some of them to wind the ba
1 CONC rk of the birch about their legs, instead of wearing shoes. But in January 1177, the Birkebeiners met a crushing defeat at the Battle of Re and Øystein fell. Sverre met with the remnants in Värmland. After some initial doubts, Sve
1 CONC rre let himself be persuaded to become the Birkebeiners’ next leader.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Rise to power
1 CONT Sverre's journey to Øreting in Nidaros
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Sverre's journey to Øreting in Nidaros
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon Sverre's initial contact, the Birkebeiners had been reduced to a ragtag army of brigands and vagabonds with no more than 70 men, according to the saga. Many regard Sverre's achievement of forging them into a force of skilled 
1 CONC and professional soldiers as proof of his leadership qualities.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Difficult years
1 CONT
1 CONT During the early years as leader of the Birkebeiners, Sverre and his men were almost constantly on the move. The Birkebeiners were generally viewed as troublemakers with little chance of success by the general populace, who most o
1 CONC f all desired peace. Although peasant gatherings were no match for the battle hardened Birkebeiners, Magnus or Erling Skakke frequently had the Birkebeiner on the run.
1 CONT
1 CONT In June 1177, Sverre first led his men to Trøndelag where Sverre was proclaimed as king at Øreting. Since this was the traditional place to choose a king, the event carried important symbolic weight. The Birkebeiners then moved so
1 CONC uth to Hadeland, where they were forced northwards again. Sverre then decided to turn west, attempting to take Bergen by surprise. At Voss, however, the Birkebeiners were ambushed by the local peasants. Although the Birkebeiners w
1 CONC ere victorious, the surprise element on Bergen was eliminated, forcing the group eastwards again. After almost freezing to death on Sognefjell, they wintered in Østerdal.
1 CONT
1 CONT The next spring, after a short stay in Viken, Sverre and the Birkebeiners returned to Trøndelag. The Birkebeiners now shifted to a more confrontational strategy. However an attack on Nidaros ended in defeat at the Battle of Hattha
1 CONC mmeren. After fleeing south, they met Magnus’ army in Ringerike, with the skirmish ending in a tactical victory for the Birkebeiners. Encouraged, the Birkebeiners returned to Trøndelag and managed to subdue the region enough to st
1 CONC ay in Nidaros during the winter.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Spring of 1179, Magnus and Erling Skakke attacked Nidaros, forcing another apparent retreat. Confident that the Birkebeiners had again fled southwards, Magnus and his men were complacent. Sverre, however, had turned around 
1 CONC at Gauldal and marched again upon the city. The two armies met 19 June in the Battle of Kalvskinnet. Erling Skakke was killed in a battle that ended in a clear victory for Sverre. This victory secured Sverre's hold on Trøndelag.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Victory over the Heklungs
1 CONT Sverre's journey across the mountains to Voss and back
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Sverre's journey across the mountains to Voss and back
1 CONT
1 CONT After Sverre’s victory at Kalvskinnet, the war changed somewhat in character. The Trønders accepted Sverre as their king; the two sides were now much more equal in power. At some point, Magnus’ party acquired the nickname Heklungs
1 CONC . Hekle is Old Norse for hood and is here probably meant to imply the traditional monk garb. The Heklungs thus probably got their name from their close connection with the church.
1 CONT
1 CONT Several battles now followed. Magnus Erlingsson again attacked Trøndelag in the spring of 1180, this time reinforced by conscripts from western Norway. But in the Battle of Ilevollene, just outside of Nidaros, the Heklungs were ag
1 CONC ain defeated and Magnus fled to Denmark. With Magnus out of the country, Sverre could sail south and occupy Bergen, but his hold on the region remained weak.
1 CONT
1 CONT Determined to achieve a decisive victory against the Birkebeiners, Magnus returned with his fleet the next year. The two forces met at sea 31 May 1181 in the Battle of Nordnes. The battle ended in a tactical victory for the Birkeb
1 CONC einers; the Heklungs fled when Magnus was mistakenly believed to have been killed. With his men in poor shape, Sverre decided to withdraw to Trøndelag. Some attempts at negotiation were now made, but these soon broke down. Magnus 
1 CONC would not accept Sverre as co-king with equal status, and Sverre could not accept becoming Magnus’ vassal.
1 CONT
1 CONT With Magnus controlling western Norway from his seat at Bergen, it became problematic for Sverre to keep his men supplied. Sverre therefore led his men south to Viken, a firm Heklung stronghold. He could therefore let his men plun
1 CONC der here with little damage to his cause. However, Magnus exploited Sverre’s absence well. In November he raided Trøndelag and managed to seize and burn the Birkebeiner fleet. Sverre had to return or risk losing his one secure foo
1 CONC thold.
1 CONT
1 CONT During summer 1182, Magnus made an attempt to take Nidaros by siege, but was repulsed with grave losses when the Birkebeiners launched a surprise night attack. Sverre now started an extensive ship-building program. Without a fleet
1 CONC , he could have no hopes of expanding his influence further south. In spring 1183 Sverre attacked Bergen with parts of his new fleet. Avoiding detection by the enemy scouts, he caught the Heklungs off guard, seizing their entire f
1 CONC leet. Magnus fled to Denmark, leaving crown and sceptre behind.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the sea battles of medieval Scandinavia, the side with largest and highest ships would usually have an advantage since this meant the crew could attack the enemy from above with projectiles and other weapons. Sverre built the l
1 CONC argest ship afloat at the time, the Mariasuda. As a result of its great size, the seaworthiness of the Mariasuda was rather poor and it would only be useful within the narrow fjords. Either because of luck or good strategy such a 
1 CONC situation would soon arise.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early spring 1184, Magnus returned to Viken from Denmark with new ships. In April he sailed north towards Bergen. At about the same time, Sverre had gone to Sogn to put down a local uprising and was still there when Magnus came to
1 CONC  Bergen in June. After chasing out the few Birkebeiners there, Magnus set sail again, having heard news of Sverre’s current position. The two fleets met 15 June at Fimreite in the long and narrow Sognefjord. The Battle of Fimreite
1 CONC  proved to be final struggle between Birkebeiners and Heklungs. Magnus had several large ships, but none as huge as the Mariasuda. While the Mariasuda held up half of the enemy fleet, the rest attacked the outlying enemy ships. Pa
1 CONC nic began to spread as the Heklungs fled aboard their larger ships. These ships soon became overloaded and begun to sink. Many of the wounded and tired men could not keep themselves afloat and drowned, including King Magnus. Most 
1 CONC of the Heklung leadership fell there, along with a huge number of men at both sides, 2160 according to the saga (though this number is probably too high). Leaderless, the Heklungs were now broken as a political party. Sverre could
1 CONC  now finally, after a six years long struggle, claim to be the sole and uncontested king of Norway.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Troubled reign
1 CONT Locations of the most important battles during Sverre's reign
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Locations of the most important battles during Sverre's reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Now that the dissatisfied priest and his band of vagrants and outcasts had become King and rulers of Norway, Sverre worked to consolidate his power. He placed his loyal men in high positions throughout the kingdom and negotiated m
1 CONC arriage alliances between the old and new nobility. Sverre himself married Margareta, daughter of Erik the Saint and sister of King Knut Eriksson of Sweden.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Norway had seen several conflicts the last decades, the victor had reconciled with his opponents. Reconciliation at Sverre's hand, however, proved to be difficult. It was a long war with more casualties than previous conf
1 CONC licts. Most of the older noble dynasties had lost men and thirsted for vengeance. Further, that many people of non-noble origin were now elevated to noble standing was difficult for many to accept. Peace was not to last long.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Kuvlungs and Øyskjeggs
1 CONT
1 CONT Autumn 1185 the Kuvlungs rose in Viken. Their leader, Jon Kuvlung, was a former monk and was claimed to be the son of Inge Krogrygg. This group was in many ways the direct successor of the Heklungs, with many of its members coming
1 CONC  from former Heklung families. The Kuvlungs soon gained control of eastern and western Norway, the old Heklung strongholds.
1 CONT
1 CONT In autumn 1186, the Kuvlungs attacked Nidaros. This offensive took Sverre by surprise; he took refuge in the recently constructed stone castle Sion. The Kuvlungs, unable to take the castle, were forced to retreat. In 1188 Sverre s
1 CONC ailed south with a large fleet. They first met at Tønsberg, but neither side dared to offer battle. The Kuvlungs slipped away to Bergen. Sverre attacked Bergen just before Christmas. Here Jon Kuvlung was killed, which ended the Ku
1 CONC vlung rising.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some minor uprisings followed, but these never rose above banditry and were suppressed on a local scale.
1 CONT
1 CONT The next serious threat came in 1193 with the Øyskjeggs – the Isle Beards. The Pretender to the throne of this group was Sigurd, a child claimed to be the bastard son of Magnus Erlingsson. The real leader was Hallkjell Jonsson who
1 CONC  was Magnus’ brother-in-law. Conspiring with the Earl of Orkney, Harald Maddadsson, Hallkjell gathered most of his men on the Orkneys and Shetland, hence the name of the group. After establishing themselves in Viken, the Øyskjeggs
1 CONC  sailed on to Bergen. Although they occupied the city itself and the surrounding regions, a force of Birkebeiners held on in Sverresborg castle. Spring 1194 Sverre sailed south to confront the Øyskjeggs. The two fleets met 3 April
1 CONC  in the Battle of Florvåg. Here the battle experience of the Birkebeiner veterans proved to be decisive. Hallkjell fell with most of his men.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sverre and the Church
1 CONT
1 CONT The Church of Norway had been organized under the Archbishopric of Nidaros in 1152. Øystein Erlendsson, who had become archbishop in 1161, had been one of Magnus Erlingsson’s main supporters. In return the church had secured its p
1 CONC osition as an independent institution and also gained several other privileges.
1 CONT
1 CONT Øystein had returned to Nidaros from England in 1183 and during his last years a state of truce existed between church and king. When Øystein died 26 January 1188, the bishop of Stavanger, Eirik Ivarsson, was elected as his succes
1 CONC sor. Sverre now probably hoped that his relationship with the church could be normalised. He therefore approached Eirik with hopes of being crowned — the definite proof of recognition. However, in Eirik’s eyes, Sverre was little m
1 CONC ore than a usurper and king-murderer.
1 CONT
1 CONT The situation now escalated into an open breach as Sverre began building up a list of privileges that were contrary to the church law made by St. Olaf, the traditional founder of the Norwegian Church. Eirik on side preached agains
1 CONC t the king and his men, and sent letters of complaint to the Pope, but in the short term his offensive weapons were few. In 1190 Sverre now attempted to force the Archbishop into submission, claiming that Eirik had broken the law 
1 CONC by having 90 armed men in his service. According to law, the Archbishop’s guard was limited upwards to 30 men. Rather than to submit to the king’s will, Eirik fled to Lund where the Danish archbishop had his seat. From there he se
1 CONC nt a delegation to Rome asking the Pope for advice.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT With the archbishop absent, Sverre tightened his grip on the bishops, and on Nikolas Arnesson, who became bishop of Oslo in 1190, in particular. Nikolas was the half-brother of Inge Krokrygg, had been among the Heklung leadership 
1 CONC and in 1194 he appeared to be suspiciously close to the Øyskjeggs. After the destruction of the Øyskjeggs at Florvåg, Sverre arranged a meeting with Nikolas where he charged the bishop with treason and threatened severe punishment
1 CONC . Nikolas submitted, and on 29 June, together with the other bishops, he crowned Sverre as King of Norway. Sverre’s domestic priest was elected bishop of Bergen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, archbishop Eirik had at last received a reply from Rome. In a letter dated 15 June 1194, Pope Celestine III lay out the foundational rights of the Norwegian Church supporting Eirik on every point.[2] Empowered by this l
1 CONC etter, Eirk could go to the step of excommunicating Sverre and order the Norwegian bishops to join him in exile in Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT Next spring Sverre sent the still loyal Tore, bishop of Hamar to Rome to plead his case before the Pope. He returned early 1197, according to the saga, carrying a papal letter which annulled Sverre’s excommunication. In Denmark To
1 CONC re is said to have fallen ill and died under suspicious circumstances, but not before pawning the papal letter. The pawnbrokers then travelled to Norway and delivered it to Sverre who used it for everything it was worth. No other 
1 CONC sources confirm this story and most historians now agree that the letter was forged.
1 CONT
1 CONT With the death of Pope Celestine in January 1198, the conflict entered a short lull until the new pope, Innocent III had brought himself up to date, but then the conflict was further escalated. In October, Innocent III placed Norw
1 CONC ay under the interdict and in letters to Eirik accuses Sverre of forgery.[3] He also sent letters to admonish neighboring kings to dispossess Sverre. They did the contrary: Sweden continued actively to support the Birkebeiners and
1 CONC  John I of England sent mercenaries to help Sverre. In 1200 Innocent found it necessary to warn the Archbishop of Canterbury from accepting further gifts from Sverre.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT Around this time someone close to Sverre wrote A speech against the bishops. In this work the unknown author discuss the relationship between King and Church. By referring to well known theological works such as the Decretum Grati
1 CONC ani and the writings of Augustine of Hippo, the author attempted to prove that Sverre's excommunication is unjust and thus not binding. The author also tried to defend Sverre's right to appoint bishops. To support this view he had
1 CONC  to interpret Norwegian law, since the Church had long considered this to be simony. By now Sverre had his hands full with the church-supported Bagler rising, and the direct struggle with the church became a sideshow, at least for
1 CONC  him personally.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Bagler war
1 CONT
1 CONT During spring 1196 the Bagler party was formed at Halør in Denmark in opposition against Sverre. Their leaders were Nikolas Arnesson, the nobleman Reidar the Messenger from Viken and Sigurd Jarlsson, a bastard son of Erling Skakke
1 CONC . Eirik the Archbishop also gave his support. As their king they choose Inge Magnusson, supposedly the son of Magnus Erlingsson. They then sailed back to Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sverre happened to be in Viken, and the two forces soon encountered each other although no major battles were fought. Sverre gave his oldest son, Sigurd Lavard the responsibility of guarding a ballista he had had built. However th
1 CONC e Baglers launched a surprise night attack during which the ballista was destroyed and Sigurd and his men were chased away. Sverre was furious and never gave his son a command again. After some more indecisive fighting, Sverre sai
1 CONC led north to Trondheim where he spent the winter. The Baglers had Inge hailed as king on Borgarting and soon established a firm control over the Viken region with Oslo as their main seat.
1 CONT
1 CONT Spring 1197 Sverre called out the leidang from the northern and western parts of the country and in May he was able to sail south to Viken with more than 7000 men, a considerable force. The Birkebeiners attacked Oslo 26 July and a
1 CONC fter many casualties on both sides, the Baglers were forced inland. Sverre now spent some time war-taxing the region, but with his leidang troops close to mutiny, Sverre withdrew to Bergen where he had decided to spend the winter.
1 CONC  This was to be a near fatal mistake. The Baglers had meanwhile travelled north to Trøndelag by land where they had entered Nidaros with little opposition. The garrison at Sverresborg held fast for a while until their commander To
1 CONC rstein Kugad changed sides and let the Baglers into the castle. The Baglers had Sverresborg completely dismantled. Sverre’s home region was now in enemy hands.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1198 was to be the nadir of Sverre’s fortunes. In May Sverre launched his attempt to recapture Trøndelag. This time Sverre failed to achieve surprise and the Birkebeiner fleet consisted mostly of smaller ships. In the sea battle t
1 CONC hat followed, the Birkebeiners were soundly beaten. In the aftermath of this battle the Baglers further consolidated their hold on Trøndelag and many went over to what they believed to be the winning side.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his defeat, Sverre limped back to Bergen. He was soon followed by a numerically superior Bagler army under the leadership of Nikolas Arnesson and Hallvard of Såstad. Sverre continued to hold Bergenhus fortress. This castle p
1 CONC roved to be impregnable, giving the Birkebeiners a secure base of operation. The following summer was to be called the “Bergen’s summer” and was dominated by indecisive skirmishing in the Bergen area. On 11 August the Baglers set 
1 CONC fire to Bergen. The destruction was complete, even the churches were burnt down. Facing starvation, Sverre slipped away with most of his men to Trøndelag.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Trøndelag, most of the population was still loyal to Sverre, and many of those who had joined the Baglers now changed sides again. Sverre was also able to play on the Baglers brutality at Bergen. The Trønders promised to provid
1 CONC e Sverre with a new fleet, in all 8 large ships were constructed and several transport ships were converted. The Baglers sailed into the Trondheimsfjord in early June. On 18 June 1199 the two fleets met at the Battle of Strindafjo
1 CONC rd. Here Sverre won a crushing victory and surviving Baglers fled to Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sverre could now take control over Viken and prepared to spend the winter in Oslo, but the countryside remained largely hostile. Early next year a spontaneous uprising took place as huge numbers of people started drifting towards 
1 CONC Oslo to throw the Birkebeiners out. This peasant army was both untrained and without organization and were no match for the battle hardened Birkebeiners. In a battle 6 March 1200 the peasants were defeated piecemeal. However the B
1 CONC irkebeiners’ grip on the region was still weak, and Sverre decided to sail back to Bergen.
1 CONT
1 CONT With Sverre gone, the Baglers could return in force from Denmark and soon they had re-established their hold on Eastern Norway. The two sides now spent a year raiding each other’s territories with no lasting gains for either side,
1 CONC  although the Birkebeiners had the upper hand at sea.
1 CONT
1 CONT Spring 1201 Sverre sailed out from Bergen with a large leidang force in what would be his last campaign season. With this army he could uncontested demand war taxes at both sides of the Oslofjord during the summer. In September he
1 CONC  set up camp at Tønsberg and laid siege to Tønsberg Fortress which was garrisoned by Reidar the Messenger and his men. The siege dragged on as the other Bagler leaders dared not send a relief force and the garrison did not fall fo
1 CONC r any of Sverre’s tricks. At last on 25 January Reidar and his men surrendered, and Sverre decided to sail back to Bergen.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the return journey Sverre fell ill and by the time they reached Bergen, the king was dying. On his death bed Sverre appointed his sole living son, Håkon, as his heir and successor and in a letter advised him to seek reconci
1 CONC liation with the Church. Sverre died 8 March 1202.
1 CONT [edit]
