0 @N6000000002187734535@ NOTE Styrbjörn the Strong
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Styrbjörn the Strong (Styrbjörn Sterki) or Styrbjörn the Swedish Champion (Styrbjörn svía kappi) (c. 960- c. 984) was according to the Norse sagas the son of the Swedish king Olof, and the nephew of Olof's co-ruler and successor E
1 CONC ric the Victorious. At his father's death, which probably took place in the 970s, Björn could claim greater right to the throne of Sweden than Eric's own soon to be born son.
1 CONT
1 CONT The earliest attestation of Styrbjörn is from a contemporary scaldic poem a lausavísa 1:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eigi vildu Jótar 
1 CONT reiða gjald til skeiða, 
1 CONT áðr Styrbjarnar stœði 
1 CONT Strandar dýr á landi ; 
1 CONT nú's Danmarkar dróttinn 
1 CONT í drengja lið genginn ; 
1 CONT landa vanr ok lýða 
1 CONT lifir ánauðr hann auðar.[1]   
1 CONT
1 CONT It is believed that there once was a larger saga on Styrbjörn, but most of what is extant is found in the short story Styrbjarnar þáttr Svíakappa. Parts of his story are also retold in Eyrbyggja saga and in Hervarar saga. He is mo
1 CONC reover mentioned in the Heimskringla (several times), and in Yngvars saga víðförla where Ingvar the Far-Travelled is compared to his kinsman Styrbjörn.
1 CONT
1 CONT In modern days, he is also the hero of a novel called Styrbiorn the Strong by the English author Eric Rucker Eddison (1926), and he figures in The Long Ships, by Frans G Bengtsson.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Synopsis 
1 CONT 1.1 Youth 
1 CONT 1.2 Career 
1 CONT 1.3 The Battle of the Fýrisvellir 
1 CONT 2 Archaeological evidence 
1 CONT 3 Notes 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Synopsis
1 CONT The following synopsis is based on Styrbjarnar þáttr Svíakappa:
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Youth
1 CONT Styrbjörn was unusually big, strong and unruly (for a Viking) and although he was only a little boy he managed to kill a courtier who accidentally had hit him on the nose with a drinking horn.
1 CONT
1 CONT When he was 12 years old he asked his uncle for his birthright, but when he was denied the co-rulership of Sweden he sulked for a long time on his father's mound.
1 CONT
1 CONT When he was 16 the Ting decided that he was too unruly to be king of Sweden. Eric decided to make his own unborn child co-regent on the condition that it was a son. As a compensation his uncle Eric gave him 60 well-equipped longsh
1 CONC ips whereupon the frustrated Styrbjörn took his sister Gyrid and left.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Career
1 CONT He ravaged the shores of the Baltic Sea and when he was twenty, he conquered the stronghold of Jomsborg from its founder Palnetoke, and became the ruler of the Jomsvikings.
1 CONT
1 CONT After some time he allied with the Danish king Harold Bluetooth and married his sister Gyrid to him. Styrbjörn married Harold's daughter Tyra, whom he was given by Harold for conquering Jomsborg. (Styrbjörn had the son Torkel Styr
1 CONC björnsson with Tyra. Torkel had a daughter named Gytha Thorkelsdóttir, who married Godwin, Earl of Wessex and became the mother of Harold II of England).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT The Battle of the Fýrisvellir
1 CONT Harold gave him even more warriors and now Styrbjörn was about to reclaim the throne of Sweden. He sailed with a huge force which included 200 Danish longships in addition to his own Jomsvikings. When they arrived at Föret (Old No
1 CONC rse: Fyris) in Uplandia he burnt the ships in order to force his men to fight to the end. The Danish force changed its mind and returned to Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT Styrbjörn marched alone with his Jomsvikings to Gamla Uppsala. His uncle was, however, prepared and had sent for reinforcements in all directions.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the first two days, the battle was even. In the evening, Eric went to the statue of Odin at the Temple at Uppsala where he sacrificed. He promised Odin that if he won the battle, he would belong to Odin and arrive at Valhal
1 CONC la in ten years from then.
1 CONT
1 CONT The third day, Eric threw his spear over the enemy and said "I sacrifice you all to Odin". Styrbjörn and his sworn men stayed, and died.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Archaeological evidence
1 CONT The runestone DR 295 in Hällestad, Hallandia says: Eskil raised this stone after Toke Gormsson, his beloved master. He did not flee at Uppsala. 
1 CONT
1 CONT A : askil : sati : stin : þansi : ift[iR]  : tuka : kurms : sun : saR : hulan : trutin : saR : flu : aigi : at : ub::salum 
1 CONT B satu : trikaR : iftiR : sin : bruþr stin : o : biarki : stuþan : runum : þiR : 
1 CONT C (k)(u)(r)(m)(s) (:) (t)(u)(k)(a) : kiku : (n)(i)(s)(t)[iR] 
1 CONT
1 CONT A Æskel satti sten þænsi æftiR Toka Gorms sun, seR hullan drottin. SaR flo ægi at Upsalum 
1 CONT B sattu drængiaR æftiR sin broþur sten a biargi støþan runum. ÞeR 
1 CONT C Gorms Toka gingu næstiR. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The runestone DR 279 in Sjörup, Scania, relates: He did not flee at Uppsala, but fought as long as he had weapons. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [+ sa]ksi : sati : st[in] : þasi : huftiR : o[s]biurn : (s)in : fil(a)go ' (t)u-a[s : sun :] saR : flu : aki : a[t :] ub:sal(u)m : an : ua : maþ : an : uabn : a(f)þi ' 
1 CONT
1 CONT Saxi satti sten þæssi æftiR Æsbiorn, sin felaga, To[f]a/To[k]a sun. SaR flo ægi at Upsalum, æn wa mæþ han wapn hafþi. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT On the Högby Runestone, it says The good freeman Gulli had five sons. The brave champion Asmund fell on the Fyris Wolds. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT Note 1: The highly source-critical Nationalencyklopedin states that he was a historical figure due to his mention in a scaldic poem. Unfortunately, it does not name it.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Henriksson, Alf: Stora mytologiska uppslagsboken. 
1 CONT Nationalencyklopedin 
1 CONT Nordisk familjebok 
1 CONT A chapter in Swedish from Verner von Heidenstam's "Svenskarna och deras hövdingar" where he recounts the tale of Styrbjörn 
1 CONT Philology and Fantasy before Tolkien, by Andrew Wawn (this scholar only knows of two sources for Styrbjörn) 
1 CONT Cultural Paternity in the Flateyjarbók Óláfs saga Tryggvasonar by Elizabeth Ashman Rowe (this scholar has got Eric's agreement with Odin slightly wrong. Eric did not promise 10 years to Odin, he promised to belong to Odin after 10
1 CONC  years) 
0 @N6000000002240745972@ NOTE could this actually be "Styrbjorn Olafsson" as found at:
1 CONT http://www2.gdi.net/~gwydion/odell/denmark.htm
1 CONT http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Plains/2064/dat3.htm#9
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth : @959
1 CONT                Death : 986
1 CONT                Parents:
1 CONT
1 CONT                     Father: Olaf Bjornsson, King of Sweden
1 CONT
1 CONT Thyra Haraldsdottir (born before 1000 and died on Sept. 18, 1000). She
1 CONT married
1 CONT    Styrbjorn Olafsson of Sweden and had Bjorn Stybjornsson and Thorkils
1 CONT Sprakalegg    (born about 936). Thyra then married Mieczislaw
1 CONT (Burislaf) I of Poland, Duke of   Poland, but had no children. She
1 CONT married Olaf Tryggvason of Norway, King of Noray      and they were the
1 CONT parents of Harald Olafsson (born 999).
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/cgi-bin/gedlkup/n=royal?royal05354
1 CONT Olafsson, Styrbjorn of Sweden
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Born: ABT 959
1 CONT Died: 986, battle
1 CONT
1 CONT Father: Bjornsson, Olaf of Sweden, King of Sweden
1 CONT
1 CONT Married to Haraldsdottir, Thyra
1 CONT
1 CONT Child 1: Styrbjornsson, Bjorn
1 CONT Child 2: Sprakalegg, Thorkils, b. ABT 936
1 CONT
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT    A young Viking, King Olaf Tryggvason of Norway, became a convert to
1 CONT the Christian religion some time before AD 1000. His passion for the
1 CONT new religion was backed by a military force that threatened all who
1 CONT refused baptism. Some Norsemen had already become Christians, mainly
1 CONT through Irish influence, though on the whole the Vikings were content
1 CONT with their own gods. Gradually Norway was Christianized, then the
1 CONT Faeroe Islands and Iceland, and finally Greenland. The first Christian
1 CONT missionaries in Greenland were brought there from Norway by Leif
1 CONT Ericson.
1 CONT    A Viking chieftain was buried with everything he might need to get
1 CONT to Valhalla. One third of his property might be used in this way.
1 CONT (Another third went to his widow and the remainder to his children.)
1 CONT The goods buried included money, tools, changes of clothing, weapons,
1 CONT horses, chariots, boats, and even ships. Women's graves contained many
1 CONT of the things they might need in afterlife, such as needles and thread,
1 CONT looms, kitchen utensils, and cooking vessels.
1 CONT    Sometimes a dead warrior would be placed aboard his ship, which was
1 CONT set afire and allowed to drift out to sea. Sometimes people were buried
1 CONT in boat-shaped coffins, which were covered with earth mounds.
1 CONT Fortunately, ships were not always burned, and a few have been
1 CONT preserved.
1 CONT    Next to the sagas, graves have been the best source of information
1 CONT about the Norsemen. In Scandinavian museums there are examples of
1 CONT almost every art known to the Viking Age. Among these are jewelry,
1 CONT weapons, furniture, and bronze and silver utensils. Most have survived
1 CONT because they were made of such durable materials as stone, metal, and
1 CONT hardwood. But woolen clothes in good condition have been found in parts
1 CONT of Greenland where they had lain in the frozen soil for centuries.
1 CONT
1 CONT History from the Sagas
1 CONT
1 CONT    The Norsemen, like the Greeks of Homer's time, were storytellers and
1 CONT poets. At all assemblies, weddings, and funerals, those skilled at
1 CONT storytelling and reciting verses would perform.
1 CONT    When Christianity came to the mainland of Scandinavia, folk poems
1 CONT and stories were frowned upon by the clergy. But Iceland was protected
1 CONT by distance from the influence of Europe. So, long after Christianity
1 CONT became the official religion, the Icelandic people struggled to
1 CONT preserve their historical and literary heritage. Their religious
1 CONT leaders enjoyed the storytelling and found no offense in it.
1 CONT    During the 12th and 13th centuries, the clergy and scholars of
1 CONT Iceland wrote many manuscripts. All were written as the saga tellers
1 CONT related them. Some were true and some were pure fiction. Among the
1 CONT serious historical records are sagas that tell of the kings and of
1 CONT Viking conquests. They tell of their discovery and colonization of
1 CONT Iceland and Greenland and their discovery of the American mainland.
1 CONT    Two significant manuscripts dealing with the religion and philosophy
1 CONT of the Norsemen were written in Iceland the Elder Edda (in poetry) and
1 CONT the Younger Edda (in prose). Much of what is known of early Norse
1 CONT mythology came from the Eddas. (See also Saga; Scandinavian Literature.)
1 CONT    In Iceland much of the old Norse language has been retained. In
1 CONT Norway, Sweden, and Denmark the languages are as different from the old
1 CONT Norse as modern English is from early Anglo-Saxon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Evelyn Stefansson Nef
1 CONT
1 CONT
