0 @N6000000002322263972@ NOTE could this actually be "Styrbjorn Olafsson" as found at:
1 CONT http://www2.gdi.net/~gwydion/odell/denmark.htm
1 CONT http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Plains/2064/dat3.htm#9
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth : @959
1 CONT                Death : 986
1 CONT                Parents:
1 CONT
1 CONT                     Father: Olaf Bjornsson, King of Sweden
1 CONT
1 CONT Thyra Haraldsdottir (born before 1000 and died on Sept. 18, 1000). She
1 CONT married
1 CONT    Styrbjorn Olafsson of Sweden and had Bjorn Stybjornsson and Thorkils
1 CONT Sprakalegg    (born about 936). Thyra then married Mieczislaw
1 CONT (Burislaf) I of Poland, Duke of   Poland, but had no children. She
1 CONT married Olaf Tryggvason of Norway, King of Noray      and they were the
1 CONT parents of Harald Olafsson (born 999).
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/cgi-bin/gedlkup/n=royal?royal05354
1 CONT Olafsson, Styrbjorn of Sweden
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Born: ABT 959
1 CONT Died: 986, battle
1 CONT
1 CONT Father: Bjornsson, Olaf of Sweden, King of Sweden
1 CONT
1 CONT Married to Haraldsdottir, Thyra
1 CONT
1 CONT Child 1: Styrbjornsson, Bjorn
1 CONT Child 2: Sprakalegg, Thorkils, b. ABT 936
1 CONT
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT    A young Viking, King Olaf Tryggvason of Norway, became a convert to
1 CONT the Christian religion some time before AD 1000. His passion for the
1 CONT new religion was backed by a military force that threatened all who
1 CONT refused baptism. Some Norsemen had already become Christians, mainly
1 CONT through Irish influence, though on the whole the Vikings were content
1 CONT with their own gods. Gradually Norway was Christianized, then the
1 CONT Faeroe Islands and Iceland, and finally Greenland. The first Christian
1 CONT missionaries in Greenland were brought there from Norway by Leif
1 CONT Ericson.
1 CONT    A Viking chieftain was buried with everything he might need to get
1 CONT to Valhalla. One third of his property might be used in this way.
1 CONT (Another third went to his widow and the remainder to his children.)
1 CONT The goods buried included money, tools, changes of clothing, weapons,
1 CONT horses, chariots, boats, and even ships. Women's graves contained many
1 CONT of the things they might need in afterlife, such as needles and thread,
1 CONT looms, kitchen utensils, and cooking vessels.
1 CONT    Sometimes a dead warrior would be placed aboard his ship, which was
1 CONT set afire and allowed to drift out to sea. Sometimes people were buried
1 CONT in boat-shaped coffins, which were covered with earth mounds.
1 CONT Fortunately, ships were not always burned, and a few have been
1 CONT preserved.
1 CONT    Next to the sagas, graves have been the best source of information
1 CONT about the Norsemen. In Scandinavian museums there are examples of
1 CONT almost every art known to the Viking Age. Among these are jewelry,
1 CONT weapons, furniture, and bronze and silver utensils. Most have survived
1 CONT because they were made of such durable materials as stone, metal, and
1 CONT hardwood. But woolen clothes in good condition have been found in parts
1 CONT of Greenland where they had lain in the frozen soil for centuries.
1 CONT
1 CONT History from the Sagas
1 CONT
1 CONT    The Norsemen, like the Greeks of Homer's time, were storytellers and
1 CONT poets. At all assemblies, weddings, and funerals, those skilled at
1 CONT storytelling and reciting verses would perform.
1 CONT    When Christianity came to the mainland of Scandinavia, folk poems
1 CONT and stories were frowned upon by the clergy. But Iceland was protected
1 CONT by distance from the influence of Europe. So, long after Christianity
1 CONT became the official religion, the Icelandic people struggled to
1 CONT preserve their historical and literary heritage. Their religious
1 CONT leaders enjoyed the storytelling and found no offense in it.
1 CONT    During the 12th and 13th centuries, the clergy and scholars of
1 CONT Iceland wrote many manuscripts. All were written as the saga tellers
1 CONT related them. Some were true and some were pure fiction. Among the
1 CONT serious historical records are sagas that tell of the kings and of
1 CONT Viking conquests. They tell of their discovery and colonization of
1 CONT Iceland and Greenland and their discovery of the American mainland.
1 CONT    Two significant manuscripts dealing with the religion and philosophy
1 CONT of the Norsemen were written in Iceland the Elder Edda (in poetry) and
1 CONT the Younger Edda (in prose). Much of what is known of early Norse
1 CONT mythology came from the Eddas. (See also Saga; Scandinavian Literature.)
1 CONT    In Iceland much of the old Norse language has been retained. In
1 CONT Norway, Sweden, and Denmark the languages are as different from the old
1 CONT Norse as modern English is from early Anglo-Saxon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Evelyn Stefansson Nef
1 CONT
1 CONT
