0 @N6000000003051273760@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Boleslaus II of Bohemia", retrieved 22 Oct 07]
1 CONT Boleslaus II the Pious (Czech: Boleslav II. Pobo?ný; c. 920 - February 7, 999) was the duke of Bohemia from 972, a member of the Premyslid dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Boleslaw also called Boleslaus I and Biagota, Boleslaus II became Duke (or Prince) in on his father's death. Boleslaus maintained good relations with the Ottonian German kings, and in 975 supported Otto II during his ci
1 CONC vil war against Henry II, Duke of Bavaria. In 977, Boleslaus again attacked Bavaria, but on this occasion was barred from annexing any lands by Otto II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus' reign is most notable for the foundation of the diocese of Prague in 973. It was placed within the jurisdiction of the Archbishop of Mainz. In 982, Vojtech (later known as Saint Adalbert) was appointed to this position 
1 CONC until he abandoned his primacy to lead a mission to the Old Prussians in 994. War between Poland and Bohemia was continual in this period and by 990 Boleslaus had occupied Silesia.
1 CONT
1 CONT On September 28, 995, Boleslaus and his confederate Vr?ovci stormed Libice in southern Bohemia and massacred Slavník's dynasty. This clan had been the main rival of Premyslid power in Bohemia. Boleslaus' brutal triumph ensured the
1 CONC  unity of Bohemia under a single ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus was succeeded by, Boleslaus, his eldest son by his first wife, Adiva, daughter of Edward the Elder, King of England. His second son, Wenceslaus, died as an infant, but his two youngest sons, Jaromír and Oldrich, were bot
1 CONC h later dukes. Boleslaus' second wife was Emma of Melnik.
1 CONT
1 CONT SVU, the organization of Czecho-Slovak emigrants, claims that this king was one of the many European ancestors of U.S. Presidents George H. Bush and George W. Bush.
