0 @N6000000006127688621@ NOTE Hertig Erik I av Sachsen-Lauenburg, född 1280/1282, död i Nienburg 1361 (dödsstörtade från sin häst under flykt), hertig av Sachsen-Lauenburg 1305-1361. Son till hertig Johan I av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död 1286) och Ingeborg Birgersd
1 CONC otter(Bjälboätten).Erich var ännu minderårig då han och hans bröder efterträdde fadern Johann I vid dennes död 1286. Efter flera strider om arvet efter fadern delade bröderna 1305 upp hertigdömet mellan sig, varvid Erich erhöll de
1 CONC len Lauenburg. Efter brodernAlbrechts tidiga död 1308 tillföll även dennes andel Ratzeburg Erich. Detta fick dock Erichs äldre (?) bror Johann II att känna sig förfördelad.Erich I var även herre av Neuengamme och delar av Hadeln. 
1 CONC Han stöddes av Danmark och deltog 1311 vid furstedagen i Rostock. 1313 slöt Erich ett valförbund med den berömde markgreven Waldemar "den store" av Brandenburg (död 1318/1319). Dessa tvåföredrog i tyska kungavalet 1314 hertig Ludw
1 CONC ig av Ober-Bayern (tyske kung Ludwig IV "Bajraren") framför hertigen av Österrike, Friedrich III av Habsburg (död 1330). Erichs ställningstagande för Ludwig "Bajraren" ledde till öppna strider medkusinlinjen i Wittenberg om den sa
1 CONC chsiska kurvärdigheten. Erich menade sig ha större rätt till kurvärdigheten då Wittenberg tillhörde en yngre linje av det sachsiska hertighuset. Strax efter kungavalet 1314 anslöt sig Erich I till det förbund sombildats mot markgr
1 CONC eve Waldemar av Brandenburg, och 1316 deltog Erich i belägringen av Stralsund.Erichs regering präglades även av förödande fejder med Mecklenburg, Pommern, Holstein och de uppåtsträvande hansestäderna. 1348 miste Erich sina sista l
1 CONC änsrättigheter i Pommern och Mecklenburg som då slutgiltigt upphöjdes till riksfurstendömen.Den Gyllene Bullan av år 1356 grusade Erichs förhoppningar på kurfurstlig värdighet, vilket nu slutgiltigt tillföll linjen Wittenberg.Eric
1 CONC h I var en gammal gubbe, därtill troligen något senil, när han 1361 (1359?) under sonen Erich II:s fejd med hertig Wilhelm av Braunschweig-Lüneburg dödsstörtade från sin häst under flykt.Äktenskap och barn Erik gifte sig 1316 med 
1 CONC Elisabeth av Pommern (död 1349). Paret fick följande barn:1. Erik II av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död 1368/1369), hertig av Sachsen-Lauenburg 2. Johan av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död 1364/1370), biskop av Kammin 3. Helene av Sachsen-Lauenburg 
1 CONC (död efter 1354), gift med greve Johann II av Hoya-Bruchhausen (död 1377) 4. Jutta av Sachsen-Lauenburg (död efter 1354), gift med greve Gerhard III av Hoya (död 1383) 
0 @N6000000000945049489@ NOTE Albrecht tilhørte ?det askaniske hus? og var hertug av Sachsen 1212-1260. 
1 CONT Albrecht var sin fars annen sønn og ble hertug ved farens død i 1212. Hertugdømmet
1 CONT omfattet de anhaltiske stambesittelser omkring Wittenberg og de nyervervede besittelser ved
1 CONT Unterlebe.
1 CONT Høsten 1226 startet nye krigshandlinger mellom kong Valdemar Sejr og greve Henrik av
1 CONT Schwerin, da Valdemar gikk over Ejderen. Valdemar inntok Rendsborg, og samtidig fikk han
1 CONT støtte sydfra av greve Otto av Lüneburg, som fortsatt var lojal. Det var på dette tidspunkt at
1 CONT greve Henrik og hans allierte nordtyske grever måtte be den sachsiske hertug om hjelp. Den
1 CONT skulle de også få, men på hårde betingelser. Han ønsket anerkjennelse av sin lensoverhøyhet
1 CONT over hele Nord-Tyskland, eller med andre ord samme stilling som i sin tid Henrik Løve hadde
1 CONT hatt. Men uten hertugens bistand kunne grevene ikke regne med å motstå Valdemars angrep.
1 CONT De gikk derfor med på hertugens betingelser. I slaget ved Bornhøved 22.07.1227 førte
1 CONT Albrecht den venstre fløy.
1 CONT Etter at Agnes døde var han gift ytterligere to ganger.
1 CONT Han var den egentlige grunnlegger av hertugdømmet Lauenburg.
0 @N6000000002188118465@ NOTE William of Germany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William II of Holland, (February 1228-28 January 1256), was a count of Holland and Zeeland (1235-1256) and king of Germany (1247-1256). He was the son of Floris IV and Mathilde of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father was killed at a tournament at Corbie, William was only seven years old. His uncles William and Otto (bishop of Utrecht) were his guardians until 1239.
1 CONT
1 CONT With the help of Henry II, Duke of Brabant and the archbishop of Cologne, he was elected in 1247 as king of Germany after Emperor Frederick II was excommunicated. After a siege of five months, he took Aachen in 1248 from Frederick
1 CONC 's followers. Only then could he be crowned as king. Many of the German princes recognized his claim only after his marriage to Elizabeth, daughter of Otto the Child, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, in 1252.
1 CONT Coat of Arms of the Holy Roman Emperor William of Holland.
1 CONT Coat of Arms of the Holy Roman Emperor William of Holland.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his home county, William fought with Flanders for control of Zeeland. He made himself (being king of Germany) count of Zeeland. In July 1253, he defeated the Flemish army at Westkapelle, and a year later a cease-fire followed. 
1 CONC His anti-Flemish policy worsened his relationship with France.
1 CONT
1 CONT From 1254, he fought a number of wars against the West Frisians. He build some strong castles in Heemskerk and Haarlem and created roads for the war against the Frisians. In a battle near Hoogwoud on January 28, 1256, his horse fe
1 CONC ll through the ice, and in this vulnerable position, William was killed by the Frisians.
1 CONT Coats of Arms of the Counts of Holland.
1 CONT Coats of Arms of the Counts of Holland.
1 CONT
1 CONT William's death robbed him of the opportunity to be crowned Holy Roman Emperor by Pope Innocent IV. His body was recovered 26 years later by his son Floris of Holland, who was only 2 years old when he succeeded his father. William
1 CONC  was buried in Middelburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT William gave city rights to Haarlem, Delft, 's-Gravenzande and Alkmaar. A castle he had built in 1248 was the beginning of the city of The Hague.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Counts of Holland family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Conrad IV King of Germany
1 CONT 1247–1256 Succeeded by
1 CONT Alfonso of Castile
1 CONT and Richard of Cornwall
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Floris IV Count of Holland and Zeeland
1 CONT 1235–1256 Succeeded by
1 CONT Floris V
0 @N6000000002187829167@ NOTE Albert the Fat, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert (Latin Albertus; died 22 September 1318), called the Fat (pinguis), was duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT The second son of Albert the Tall, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Albert was a boy when his father died in 1279. He was first under guardianship of his uncle, Conrad, Bishop of Verden, and then of his elder brother, Henry the Strange
1 CONC , Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg. In 1286 the three brothers divided their father's territory; Albert received the areas around Göttingen, Minden, Northeim, Calenberg, and Hanover. He made Göttingen his residence. In 1292, the third b
1 CONC rother, William, died childless, and Albert and Henry quarrelled about William's share, the areas around Brunswick and Wolfenbüttel; Albert finally prevailed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert married Rixa, daughter of Henry I, Prince of Werle, in 1284. They had the following children that reached adulthood:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Adelaide, married John, Landgrave of Lower Hesse
1 CONT     * Richenza, Abbess of Gandersheim
1 CONT     * Mechtild
1 CONT     * Jutta
1 CONT     * Luder, joined the Teutonic Order
1 CONT     * Albert, Bishop of Halberstadt (died 1358)
1 CONT     * Henry, Bishop of Hildesheim (died 1362)
1 CONT     * Otto (died 1344)
1 CONT     * Magnus (died 1369)
1 CONT     * Ernest (died 1367)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Allgemeine Deutsche Biographie, vol. 1, p. 261-263
1 CONT
1 CONT     * At the House of Welf site
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Albert the Tall Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, Prince of Wolfenbüttel
1 CONT 1279–1286 Succeeded by
1 CONT William I
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT none Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, Prince of Göttingen
1 CONT 1286–1318 Succeeded by
1 CONT Otto the Mild
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT William I Duke of Brunswick and Lüneburg, Prince of Wolfenbüttel
1 CONT 1292–1318 Succeeded by
1 CONT Otto the Mild
